<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"> <head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="description" content="A timeline of publicly disclosed security incidents in the Model Context Protocol ecosystem. Because what could possibly go wrong when you let language models autonomously talk to everything?"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"><link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/favicon.png"><meta name="generator" content="Astro v5.18.0"><link rel="canonical" href="https://modelcontextproblems.com/"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;700&family=JetBrains+Mono:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,700;1,400&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"><meta property="og:title" content="Model Context Problems"><meta property="og:description" content="A timeline of publicly disclosed security incidents in the Model Context Protocol ecosystem. Because what could possibly go wrong when you let language models autonomously talk to everything?"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:url" content="https://modelcontextproblems.com/"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Model Context Problems"><meta name="twitter:description" content="A timeline of publicly disclosed security incidents in the Model Context Protocol ecosystem. Because what could possibly go wrong when you let language models autonomously talk to everything?"><title>Model Context Problems</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/about.9l519BMJ.css"></head> <body>  <div class="page"> <header class="masthead"> <nav class="nav-mast"> <a href="/" class="active">timeline</a> <a href="/about" class>about</a> <a href="/rss.xml">rss</a> </nav> <h1 class="brand"><a href="/">model context <span class="x">problems</span></a></h1> <p class="tagline">
a running record of publicly disclosed security incidents in the Model
    Context Protocol ecosystem. because what could possibly go wrong when
    you let language models autonomously talk to everything?
</p> </header> <section class="stats" aria-label="incident statistics"> <div class="stat"><div class="n">181</div><div class="l">incidents tracked</div></div> <div class="stat"><div class="n crit">46</div><div class="l">critical severity</div></div> <div class="stat"><div class="n">83</div><div class="l">high severity</div></div> <div class="stat"><div class="n">268</div><div class="l">CVEs assigned</div></div> <div class="ed">numbers update as new incidents are disclosed.</div> </section> <div class="filters" role="toolbar" aria-label="filter incidents"> <span class="filter-label">filter:</span> <button class="chip" data-filter-type="all" aria-pressed="true">all</button> <button class="chip" data-filter-type="severity" data-filter-value="critical" aria-pressed="false"> critical </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="severity" data-filter-value="high" aria-pressed="false"> high </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="severity" data-filter-value="medium" aria-pressed="false"> medium </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="severity" data-filter-value="low" aria-pressed="false"> low </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="severity" data-filter-value="informational" aria-pressed="false"> informational </button> <button class="chip" data-filter-type="cve" aria-pressed="false">cve assigned</button> <button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">
#advisory </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">
#authorization </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">
#authentication </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">
#rce </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">
#credential-theft </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">
#data-leak </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">
#ssrf </button><button class="chip" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">
#protocol-design </button> </div> <script type="module">const c=Array.from(document.querySelectorAll("[data-filter-type]")),d=Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".incident")),u=Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".date-stamp")),r=document.querySelector(".no-matches");function n(t,a){let l=0;d.forEach(e=>{let s=!0;t==="severity"?s=(e.dataset.severity||"").toLowerCase()===a:t==="cve"?s=e.dataset.hasCve==="true":t==="tag"&&(s=(e.dataset.tags||"").split(" ").includes(a||"")),e.classList.toggle("is-hidden",!s),s&&l++}),u.forEach(e=>{const s=e.nextElementSibling,i=!s||s.classList.contains("is-hidden");e.classList.toggle("is-hidden",i)}),r&&(r.style.display=l===0?"block":"none"),c.forEach(e=>{const s=e.dataset.filterType,i=e.dataset.filterValue||null,o=t===null?s==="all":s===t&&i===a;e.setAttribute("aria-pressed",o?"true":"false")})}c.forEach(t=>{t.addEventListener("click",()=>{const a=t.dataset.filterType,l=t.dataset.filterValue||null;if(t.classList.contains("chip")&&t.getAttribute("aria-pressed")==="true"&&a!=="all"){n(null,null);return}a==="all"?n(null,null):a&&n(a,l)})});</script>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 05</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-176" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Official Rust SDK&#39;s Client Reads Whatever the Server Sends, All of It <a href="#incident-176" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">DaleSeo published GHSA-c9f9-cp8h-jcj5 on November 5, 2026 against <code>rmcp</code> 0.2.0 before 3.9.0, rated Moderate at 6.5 under CVSS 3.1 with no CVE, crediting kalman5 and, as an independent reporter of the same finding, jlockwood10. The built-in Streamable HTTP clients, the reqwest one and the Unix socket one, read non-streaming response bodies into memory with no size limit, on successful <code>application/json</code> responses and on non-2xx error responses alike. A server answering with endless whitespace, or with a 500 that never ends, grows the client&#39;s memory until the host application dies, and it works during <code>initialize</code>, before authentication. The SSE path is unaffected, because each event is bounded by <code>max_sse_event_size</code>, 16 MiB by default, and events are handled one at a time. Version 3.9.0 caps successful JSON bodies at 16 MiB and non-success bodies at 64 KiB, raising <code>StreamableHttpError::ResponseBodyTooLarge</code> rather than truncating.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The SSE transport has carried a configurable cap the whole time. The JSON path next to it had none, which is the familiar shape of a limit added only where somebody happened to be thinking about limits. Note the direction of travel: the server is the attacker here and the client is the target, which is backwards from most of this site.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A malicious server kills any Rust MCP client that connects to it, including during the handshake, by answering with a body that does not end.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-c9f9-cp8h-jcj5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c9f9-cp8h-jcj5: Unbounded response-body buffering in rmcp Streamable HTTP clients allows memory exhaustion</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-174" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf local-network advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-122140</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Trawl Checks for a Loopback Spelling Its Own URL Parser Never Produces <a href="#incident-174" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">germondai published GHSA-mj45-84p2-v77q on November 3, 2026 against Trawl 1.5.0 through 1.9.3, crediting Leardian, rated Moderate at 5.3 under CVSS 3.1. The outbound policy in <code>apps/api/src/outbound-policy.ts</code> recognised IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses only in their dotted-decimal form, so <code>::ffff:127.0.0.1</code> was refused while the hexadecimal <code>::ffff:7f00:1</code> fell through to the default not-private answer. URL parsing normalises the dotted form into hexadecimal before the check runs, so every spelling was affected in practice. MCP is off by default behind <code>MCP_ENABLED</code>, and when it is on the validator runs on the scrape handler, before each HTTP hop and on manual redirects. Version 1.10.0 replaces the regular expression checks with numeric subnet classification and enforces public egress at connection time. The reporter confirmed a mapped-address request reaching a loopback listener both directly and through a redirect from an allowed page.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/is-this-a-pigeon.jpg" alt="anime guy gesturing at a butterfly: is this a private address?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>anime guy gesturing at a butterfly: is this a private address?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The filter knew <code>::ffff:127.0.0.1</code> and the layer above it handed over <code>::ffff:7f00:1</code>. Writing an address check against the spelling a human types, rather than the one your own parser emits, is the entire bug.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>On an instance with MCP enabled, anyone who can supply a scrape URL, or get an allowed page to redirect, pulls responses from loopback and private-range destinations.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/germondai/trawl/security/advisories/GHSA-mj45-84p2-v77q" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-mj45-84p2-v77q: MCP public-destination bypass via IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-122140" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-122140, the identifier VulnCheck assigned</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-175" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-121458</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Instatic&#39;s MCP Read Tools Skip the Check Its HTTP API Runs <a href="#incident-175" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">DavidBabinec published GHSA-x5cc-8486-8qqx on November 3, 2026, crediting Sriman Aluvala, rated Moderate at 6.5 under CVSS 3.1. The <code>content_get_document</code>, <code>content_list_documents</code> and <code>content_search_documents</code> tools in <code>server/ai/tools/content/readTools.ts</code> check only that the caller holds a required capability, never whether that caller may read a specific table or a specific row. A user holding <code>ai.chat</code> and <code>content.edit.own</code> reads other users&#39; unpublished drafts, enumerates rows including their <code>authorUserId</code> values, and reaches system-table rows without the capability for them. The same request against the ordinary HTTP data-row endpoint returns 404. The reporter reproduced it on 0.0.20 and traced the behaviour back to 0.0.5; 0.0.21 fixes it. VulnCheck filed CVE-2026-121458 the same day and scores it 7.1 under CVSS 4.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The 404 is the tell. The authorization boundary exists and is correct, and it lives in the HTTP handler, so tools that reach the data layer directly inherit exactly none of it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any low-privileged account with chat and own-content capabilities reads every other user&#39;s private drafts through the MCP surface.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/CoreBunch/Instatic/security/advisories/GHSA-x5cc-8486-8qqx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-x5cc-8486-8qqx: Missing authorization in Instatic AI/MCP content read tools</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/instatic-before-authorization-bypass-via-mcp-content-read-tools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Instatic authorization bypass via MCP content read tools (CVE-2026-121458)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-172" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem aws authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-116635</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Ships Its Third MCP Bulletin in Eleven Days <a href="#incident-172" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS published security bulletin 2026-164 on November 2, 2026, covering CVE-2026-116635 in <code>awslabs.aws-serverless-mcp-server</code> before 0.2.1 and crediting Syed Anas Mohiuddin through coordinated disclosure. The deployment status component takes a project name supplied through the MCP agent and joins it into a path without confining the result, so directory traversal sequences or an absolute path reach JSON files outside the intended deployment status directory. Where write access has been enabled, the same input creates or overwrites JSON files outside it. AWS says no workarounds are available and tells operators to patch forked and derivative code too. The matching advisory, GHSA-r6mm-377j-qg8m, went up on <code>awslabs/mcp</code> the same day.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/charlie-conspiracy.jpg" alt="charlie at the string board: three aws mcp bulletins in eleven days" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>charlie at the string board: three aws mcp bulletins in eleven days</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Bulletin 2026-149 on October 23, 2026-152 on October 27, this one on November 2. Three different AWS MCP servers, one recurring assumption: that the thing on the other end of a tool call is a configuration value rather than attacker input.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who can influence the agent&#39;s tool arguments reads any JSON file the server process can reach, and on a write-enabled deployment overwrites one.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-164-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-164: Issue with AWS Serverless MCP Server (CVE-2026-116635)</a><a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-r6mm-377j-qg8m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-r6mm-377j-qg8m: Improper limitation of a pathname to a restricted directory in the AWS Serverless MCP Server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-173" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-120492</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Chainlit&#39;s SSRF Fix Added an Allowlist and a Semicolon Walks Past It <a href="#incident-173" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-120492 on November 2, 2026 against Chainlit 2.12.0, credited to Gavin Blacklock and scored 6.3 under CVSS 4.0. <code>validate_mcp_url</code> does not reject the semicolon character, so a URL carrying <code>..;</code> segments submitted to the unauthenticated <code>/mcp</code> endpoint bypasses path-scoped entries in the operator&#39;s <code>allowed_urls</code>, on any origin that strips semicolon path parameters. That allowlist is itself the remediation Chainlit shipped in 2.12.0 for GHSA-hvfh-5mj3-5f3j, the August 25, 2026 advisory covering SSRF through the same endpoint&#39;s <code>sse</code> and <code>streamable-http</code> transports, which made user-supplied MCP servers opt-in and required a default-deny <code>allowed_urls</code>.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The August advisory was careful enough to publish its own residual risks: anonymous access on unauthenticated deployments, hostname matching with no private-IP blocking, header filtering by denylist. The one it did not list is that path scoping is only as good as the URL parser under it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller reaches paths the operator never granted, on any origin that treats `..;` as a path parameter rather than as part of the path.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/chainlit-2.12.0-ssrf-via-semicolon-path-segment-in-mcp-allowed-urls" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Chainlit 2.12.0 SSRF via semicolon path segment in MCP allowed_urls (CVE-2026-120492)</a><a href="https://github.com/Chainlit/chainlit/security/advisories/GHSA-hvfh-5mj3-5f3j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-hvfh-5mj3-5f3j: SSRF via MCP SSE and streamable-http transports, the August advisory whose fix introduced allowed_urls</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 01</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-170" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce sandbox-escape filesystem advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-120338</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> FastMCP&#39;s Generative UI Tool Feeds Code Into a Sandbox That Gave Deno Everything <a href="#incident-170" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">JFrog Security Research published JFSA-2026-001699278 on November 1, 2026, covering CVE-2026-120338 in <code>prefab-ui</code> 0.14.0 through 0.20.2 and credited to Yuval Moravchick. The Generative UI feature runs caller-supplied Python in Pyodide inside a Deno subprocess and is documented as isolation for untrusted code, including model-written UI. <code>PyodideSandbox._start</code> launches Deno with unrestricted <code>--allow-read</code>, <code>--allow-write</code> and <code>--allow-net</code>, and the runner leaves the Deno API on the JavaScript global object, which Pyodide&#39;s <code>js</code> module proxies into Python. The usual remote path is FastMCP&#39;s <code>GenerativeUI</code> provider, which registers the MCP tool <code>generate_prefab_ui</code> and forwards that tool&#39;s <code>code</code> argument into the sandbox with no further checks. JFrog scores it 10.0 against a FastMCP server with no authentication configured and 9.9 against one with authentication, giving the 9.9 as CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H and no version for the 10.0. The CVE record carries JFrog&#39;s 10.0 under CVSS 3.1. Version 0.21.0 revokes Deno&#39;s read, write and network permissions after startup and before any code is accepted.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/where-monkey.png" alt="two monkeys looking around: everyone assumed somebody else sandboxed it" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>two monkeys looking around: everyone assumed somebody else sandboxed it</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The sandbox is the whole product here. Its own runner comment and the public docs describe the process as read-only, and <code>--allow-write</code> had been in the launcher since 0.14.0. Filed High rather than Critical because Generative UI is opt-in and the host has to have Deno installed, neither of which a default deployment does.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can call `generate_prefab_ui` reads and writes files as the server&#39;s OS user. The write reaches the server virtualenv&#39;s site-packages, where a planted `.pth` file runs on the next Python process to use that environment.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://research.jfrog.com/vulnerabilities/prefab-ui-is-vulnerable-to-remote-code-execution-via-a-pyodide-js-bridge-to-deno-cve-2026-120338-jfsa-2026-001699278/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JFrog: Prefab UI is vulnerable to Remote Code Execution via a Pyodide js Bridge to Deno (JFSA-2026-001699278)</a><a href="https://github.com/PrefectHQ/prefab/pull/538" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PrefectHQ/prefab pull request 538, the 0.21.0 permission revocation</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-120338" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-120338, carrying JFrog&#39;s 10.0 under CVSS 3.1</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · november · 01</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-171" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-120450</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> LaraDashboard&#39;s MCP Mailer Answers to a Read Permission <a href="#incident-171" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">ManiruzzamanAkash published GHSA-gjfv-h634-pxpv on November 1, 2026, crediting EVIL0RD, rated High at 8.5 under CVSS 3.1. The MCP <code>SendEmailTool</code> is gated on <code>email_template.view</code>, a read permission, so any user or token holding it can send mail to any recipient with any subject and a raw HTML body, which the custom-html blade template renders without escaping. The same advisory records a second flaw in <code>McpTokenService</code>: a token stores a snapshot of its owner&#39;s abilities taken at mint time and nothing rotates it when roles or permissions change, which the advisory files under the heading that old tokens widen silently as later grants land. VulnCheck filed CVE-2026-120450 the next day covering the send-email half, dated it 11/2/2026 and scored it 5.3 under CVSS 4.0. The CVE record also carries VulnCheck&#39;s 4.3 for it under CVSS 3.1, against the advisory&#39;s 8.5 under the same version. The GHSA lists no patched version. VulnCheck says 1.4.9 fixes it.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body"><code>email_template.view</code> is permission to look at email templates. Somebody wired the send tool to it, and the abstraction held right up until an MCP client asked politely. The two records disagree by more than four points under the same CVSS version, and do not agree on whether a fix exists at all, which is its own small finding.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A low-privileged account that can only read email templates sends attacker-written HTML from the site&#39;s own mailer, at the site&#39;s own sending reputation. A token minted earlier picks up permissions its owner is granted afterwards, and nothing rotates it to flag that.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/laradashboard/laradashboard/security/advisories/GHSA-gjfv-h634-pxpv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-gjfv-h634-pxpv: MCP SendEmailTool Phishing Relay + Token Ability Snapshot Widens Silently</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/laradashboard-1.4.0-before-1.4.9-improper-authorization-via-mcp-send-email-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: LaraDashboard 1.4.0 before 1.4.9 improper authorization via MCP send-email tool (CVE-2026-120450)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-120450" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-120450, carrying VulnCheck&#39;s CVSS 3.1 score</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-161" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem rce supply-chain advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-119979</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> LM Studio&#39;s Model Downloader Writes Outside the Models Directory and Leaves an MCP Bridge Behind <a href="#incident-161" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-119979 on October 30, 2026 against LM Studio before 0.4.22+1, credited to Ariel Fogel of Pillar Security. <code>resolveDownloadRequest</code> in the model downloader accepts backslash-separated traversal filenames, so a crafted model repository writes files outside the models directory. The advisory describes planting a plugin <code>manifest.json</code>, an <code>install-state.json</code> and an <code>mcp-bridge-config.json</code>, which together run an attacker-supplied Python payload on every launch. CVSS 8.7 under 4.0 at VulnCheck, with a vector that requires the user to download the repository.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The traversal is ordinary. The payload is not. The file that makes it persistent is an MCP bridge config, which means the local agent plumbing is now a convenient place to leave something that starts itself.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Downloading the wrong model gives an attacker persistent code execution that re-runs every time LM Studio starts.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/lm-studio-before-0.4.22-1-path-traversal-via-model-downloader" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: LM Studio before 0.4.22+1 path traversal via model downloader (CVE-2026-119979)</a><a href="https://lmstudio.ai/changelog/lmstudio-v0.4.22" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LM Studio 0.4.22 changelog</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-162" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization data-leak web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-111960, CVE-2026-111961</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Royal MCP Gets Two More Authorization Bugs Six Days After Its First <a href="#incident-162" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">WPScan published two more Royal MCP findings on October 26, 2026, six days after the preview-token bypass this site carries as id 155, each credited to a different researcher. CVE-2026-111960, credited to Vincent Cassiau of CyberVanguard, is the plugin&#39;s <code>wp_search</code> ability skipping object-level and post-type authorization checks, so a subscriber reads the title and content of non-public entries. CVE-2026-111961, credited to Usama Arshad, is <code>elementor_clone_page</code> relying on a generic capability instead of an object-level check, so a contributor reads any private or draft page and publishes it without holding the publish capability. WPScan rates the first Medium and the second Low. Both are fixed in 1.5.10.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three researchers, three missing checks, one plugin, one week. The preview token was the dramatic one. These two are the same omission made quietly: an ability that checks who you are and never what you are asking for.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Subscribers read unpublished posts, and contributors copy private or draft pages and publish them without the capability to publish.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/474153af-b440-4e89-a27b-131e94c4b63e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: Royal MCP 1.4.38-1.5.9 subscriber+ non-public post disclosure via wp_search (CVE-2026-111960)</a><a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/ffdf84e4-dce3-4768-91c9-138256c3064c/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: Royal MCP &lt; 1.5.10 contributor+ private content disclosure and publish_posts bypass (CVE-2026-111961)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 25</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-177" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="filesystem ssrf credential-theft data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Official Reference Servers Ship Nine Advisories in Four Days and Not One Carries a CVE <a href="#incident-177" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Between October 25 and October 28, 2026, cliffhall published nine security advisories on <code>modelcontextprotocol/servers</code>, eight of them on October 25, and every one says &quot;No known CVE&quot;. The scores below are each advisory&#39;s own, under CVSS 3.1 except where noted. The worst is GHSA-mvf5-2qqv-rg3c, Moderate at 6.5, where <code>@modelcontextprotocol/server-everything</code> combines an any-origin CORS policy, a default bind to every interface, no authentication on any route, and a <code>get-env</code> tool that returns the entire process environment unfiltered and cannot be disabled; the reporter&#39;s proof of concept pulled 47 environment variables from a cross-origin page. Four are in <code>server-filesystem</code>: a case-variant path that slips the symlink check and creates files outside the allowed directories (GHSA-4gxg-m5jx-9j57, 5.3), a trailing quote or space that is stripped for the containment check and left in place for the operation, so the check passes on one path and the write lands on a sibling outside the tree (GHSA-7rgq-gccq-qphx, 4.2), a parent-directory fallback TOCTOU permitting writes outside the tree (GHSA-53mr-w429-c597, 2.2), and <code>list_directory_with_sizes</code> following symlinks out of it (GHSA-hhwq-wxfx-cj7g, 3.1). Three are in <code>mcp-server-fetch</code>: the private-address guard resolving the IDNA 2003 spelling of a hostname while httpx connects to the IDNA 2008 one (GHSA-v38p-wf49-hx2j, 6.3 under CVSS 4.0), the non-public network list omitting the well-known NAT64 prefix <code>64:ff9b::/96</code> so <code>64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe</code> reads as public (GHSA-66qx-2266-pmj6, 3.1), and robots.txt checked on the first URL and never again across redirects (GHSA-3jqg-r5pp-rmp2, 3.1). The ninth, on October 28, is GHSA-w766-5x43-whvw: a nested <code>.git</code> file inside an allowed directory redirects <code>mcp-server-git</code> to a repository outside its <code>--repository</code> confinement.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/sad-pablo-escobar.jpg" alt="escobar waiting alone: nine advisories, not one CVE between them" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>escobar waiting alone: nine advisories, not one CVE between them</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">These are the reference implementations. They are what people read to find out what an MCP server is supposed to look like. Filed High against a set where no advisory badges itself above Moderate, because the worst of the nine hands a passing web page the server&#39;s whole environment, and an API key leaving the machine is not an information disclosure. Five of the nine close with the same housekeeping note: npm ranks the fixed 2.0.1 below the date-stamped 2026.8.31 release, so the upgrade is never offered and has to be asked for by name.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A developer running the everything server over HTTP hands their whole environment, API keys and database credentials included, to any web page they happen to have open. The filesystem and fetch servers between them write outside their allowed directories and reach loopback, private ranges and the cloud metadata address.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/security/advisories/GHSA-mvf5-2qqv-rg3c" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-mvf5-2qqv-rg3c: Cross-Origin Environment Variable Exfiltration in @modelcontextprotocol/server-everything</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">modelcontextprotocol/servers security advisories, with the publication date of all nine</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/security/advisories/GHSA-4gxg-m5jx-9j57" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-4gxg-m5jx-9j57: Case-variant path bypasses symlink check in server-filesystem</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/security/advisories/GHSA-v38p-wf49-hx2j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-v38p-wf49-hx2j: fetch private-address guard bypass via IDNA hostnames</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/security/advisories/GHSA-w766-5x43-whvw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-w766-5x43-whvw: mcp-server-git nested gitdir file bypasses --repository confinement</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-163" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="aws authentication authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-115413, CVE-2026-115845</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Ships Two MCP Server Bulletins in Four Days <a href="#incident-163" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS published two MCP security bulletins in one week. Bulletin 2026-149, on October 23, 2026, covers CVE-2026-115845, an incomplete list of disallowed inputs in the Python code validator of the <code>run_script</code> tool in AWS MCP Server, which lets an authenticated user run arbitrary code inside their own isolated session. AWS fixed that one service-side with no customer action. Bulletin 2026-152, on October 27, covers CVE-2026-115413 in the AWS API MCP Server, credited to Yuval Elbar. In OAuth mode the server validates a bearer token&#39;s signature, issuer and expiry, but not the claim identifying the application the token was issued to, so a token the operator&#39;s identity provider minted for a different application in the same tenant is accepted. Affected versions run from 1.3.0 up to 1.6.0, which adds an expected audience or client ID setting. AWS scores it 7.3 under CVSS 4.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/they-dont-know.png" alt="man alone at a party holding a drink: they don't know this token was issued to a different application" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>man alone at a party holding a drink: they don&#39;t know this token was issued to a different application</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Signature, issuer, expiry, and then it stops reading. The audience claim exists to answer exactly the question the server skipped, and the same omission turned up in the official Python and TypeScript SDKs the same month.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A token issued to a different application in the same tenant drives AWS CLI operations with whatever credentials the MCP server host has configured.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-149-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-149: Issue with AWS MCP Server run_script validator (CVE-2026-115845)</a><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-152-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-152: Issue with AWS API MCP Server OAuth token validation (CVE-2026-115413)</a><a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-4prq-p2x5-w9fh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-4prq-p2x5-w9fh: Missing token audience validation in AWS API MCP Server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-164" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection rce prompt-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-117558, CVE-2026-117559, CVE-2026-117560, CVE-2026-117561, CVE-2026-117567, CVE-2026-117568, CVE-2026-117569, CVE-2026-117570, CVE-2026-117573</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> One Researcher Reports Nine MCP Flaws in Four Days, Eight of Them the Same Shell Injection <a href="#incident-164" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Between October 23 and October 26, 2026, Nguyễn Văn Tiến opened public security issues against nine unrelated MCP servers, each carrying a proof of concept. VulnCheck assigned CVEs to all nine on October 27, scoring seven of them 8.5 under CVSS 4.0. The bug is the same every time: a tool parameter reaches a shell. ai-sdlc interpolates <code>repo</code> into <code>gh issue view</code> through <code>execSync</code> (CVE-2026-117558). DroidMind&#39;s shell allowlist falls to <code>$(...)</code> and backtick substitution (CVE-2026-117559). The rest run through ATXP&#39;s Gemini CLI extension in <code>runAtxp()</code> (CVE-2026-117560), Codefriar sf-mcp via Salesforce CLI flag values (CVE-2026-117561), esp-mcp via tool parameters (CVE-2026-117567), mcp-ffmpeg via <code>videoPath</code> and <code>outputDir</code> (CVE-2026-117568), Kali-GPT in <code>execute_tool()</code> (CVE-2026-117569) and hagent via the <code>args</code> parameter of its Arduino and ESP32 tools with <code>shell=True</code> (CVE-2026-117573). The ninth is the odd one out: nmap-mcp&#39;s <code>scan_vulnerabilities</code> allowlist lets callers pass blocked NSE categories such as <code>exploit</code> and <code>brute</code> (CVE-2026-117570, scored 4.8 under CVSS 4.0).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/ancient-aliens.jpg" alt="giorgio tsoukalos gesturing: nine unrelated projects, eight tool arguments, one shell" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>giorgio tsoukalos gesturing: nine unrelated projects, eight tool arguments, one shell</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Nine projects, nine maintainers, one idea: the argument the model picked is a string, and strings go in shells. Eight of the nine are exactly that. The ninth at least had an allowlist, and then let the caller name the categories it was meant to block.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any MCP client, or any prompt injection that reaches one, runs shell commands on eight of the nine: as the server user on seven, and on the connected Android device through DroidMind. On nmap-mcp it runs the intrusive and brute-force scans the allowlist was written to stop.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ai-sdlc-framework/ai-sdlc/issues/1970" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ai-sdlc issue 1970: Command Injection in check_issue and check_pr_status MCP tools via repo parameter</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/ai-sdlc-mcp-server-through-0.31.2-command-injection-via-check-issue-and-check-pr-status" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: ai-sdlc MCP server through 0.31.2 command injection (CVE-2026-117558)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/droidmind-through-0.4.0-command-injection-via-shell-allowlist-bypass" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: DroidMind through 0.4.0 command injection via shell allowlist bypass (CVE-2026-117559)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/atxp-gemini-cli-extension-through-1.0.0-os-command-injection-via-mcp-tools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: ATXP Gemini CLI extension through 1.0.0 OS command injection via MCP tools (CVE-2026-117560)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/sf-mcp-through-1.3.2-os-command-injection-via-salesforce-cli-flag-values" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: sf-mcp through 1.3.2 OS command injection via Salesforce CLI flag values (CVE-2026-117561)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/esp-mcp-through-commit-28afddf-os-command-injection-via-mcp-tool-parameters" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: esp-mcp through commit 28afddf OS command injection via MCP tool parameters (CVE-2026-117567)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-ffmpeg-through-1.0.5-os-command-injection-via-videopath-and-outputdir" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-ffmpeg through 1.0.5 OS command injection via videoPath and outputDir (CVE-2026-117568)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/kali-gpt-through-commit-a608c74-os-command-injection-via-mcp-scanner-tool-parameters" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Kali-GPT through commit a608c74 OS command injection via MCP scanner tool parameters (CVE-2026-117569)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/nmap-mcp-through-commit-3636afa-permissive-nse-script-allowlist-in-scan-vulnerabilities" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: nmap-mcp through commit 3636afa permissive NSE script allowlist in scan_vulnerabilities (CVE-2026-117570)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/hagent-through-commit-761eec6-os-command-injection-via-arduino-and-esp32-mcp-tools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: hagent through commit 761eec6 OS command injection via Arduino and ESP32 MCP tools (CVE-2026-117573)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-165" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem file-overwrite advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-106484</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Chrome DevTools MCP Validates the Output Path, Then Changes It <a href="#incident-165" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">OrKoN published GHSA-5hw5-4q87-m6x9 on October 22, 2026 against <code>chrome-devtools-mcp</code>, credited to thesmartshadow. The server checks a caller-supplied output path against the configured MCP roots, then rewrites the file extension, <code>.txt</code> to <code>.png</code> for instance, and writes to the new path without checking it again. If the rewritten path is a symlink inside a root that points outside it, the tool output overwrites the file on the far end. The affected sink is the shared <code>McpContext.saveFile</code>, used by <code>take_screenshot</code> and the other extension-enforcing output tools. Submitting the final path directly is rejected, so the bypass exists only because the rewrite happens after validation. Versions 1.1.0 through 1.4.0 are affected and 1.5.0 is patched. It is tracked as CVE-2026-106484. The advisory calls it an incomplete-fix variant of CVE-2026-53766.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The path that got validated and the path that got written were never the same string. Second time this boundary has been patched in this server, and it was the first fix that made the rewrite worth looking at. Filed Medium rather than High because a symlink has to be planted inside a root first, and what lands outside it is tool output.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A caller who can leave a symlink inside an MCP root, including through a prompt-injected agent, overwrites files outside the root with tool output.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-5hw5-4q87-m6x9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-5hw5-4q87-m6x9: MCP roots bypass via extension-rewritten symlink output path</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2mw5-2f2c-v2g3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-2mw5-2f2c-v2g3: Insufficient policy enforcement in chrome-devtools-mcp (CVE-2026-106484)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="file-overwrite" aria-pressed="false">file-overwrite</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-152" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="ssrf authorization advisory protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Official Rust SDK&#39;s SSRF Filter Reads the Hostname, Not Where It Resolves <a href="#incident-152" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">DaleSeo published GHSA-5p52-q7pq-9fq2 on October 22, 2026 against the <code>rmcp</code> crate 2.0.0 through 3.6.0, fixed in 3.7.0, rated Moderate with no CVE. It is an incomplete fix of GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9, the June advisory this site carries as id 111. That fix put a same-origin requirement on the <code>resource_metadata</code> path and left the <code>authorization_servers</code> path guarded by a hostname denylist that compares text. The denylist catches literal loopback addresses, literal private ranges and known cloud metadata hostnames, so <code>127.0.0.1</code> is refused and a DNS name that resolves to <code>127.0.0.1</code> is not. The advisory also records bracketed IPv6 literals going unrecognised as internal, and the token and registration endpoints carrying no host check at all, which leaves redirects and IP-literal URLs reaching internal addresses by POST. Version 3.7.0 applies the address policy at the DNS resolver so it tests resolved addresses. Reporters: ndreno, arpe1618, Hama1cco, zachary-satterly, neyugNat and president-xd.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/two-guys-on-a-bus.jpg" alt="two guys on a bus: literal 127.0.0.1 refused, a name pointing at it waved through" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>two guys on a bus: literal 127.0.0.1 refused, a name pointing at it waved through</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">June&#39;s fix and this one are the same bug with the check moved. Matching a hostname against a list of bad hostnames tells you what the string says, and the string is the attacker&#39;s to choose; DNS decides where the packet actually goes. The resolver-level policy in 3.7.0 is what the June patch needed to be.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A malicious MCP server advertises an authorization server whose DNS record points inside the client&#39;s network, and the client sends requests there. The attacker does not see the responses, so it is blind.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5p52-q7pq-9fq2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Rust SDK GHSA-5p52-q7pq-9fq2: OAuth authorization-server metadata SSRF filter checks host strings, not resolved addresses</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Rust SDK GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9: rmcp OAuth client fetches server-controlled resource_metadata URLs</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-166" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-119025</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCP Inspector Hands a Remote Server&#39;s OAuth URL Straight to PowerShell <a href="#incident-166" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The official <code>modelcontextprotocol/inspector</code> project published GHSA-q978-78c7-phqw on October 21, 2026, credited to Eilon Cohen of Pillar Security. A remote MCP server answers with a 401 and then serves OAuth discovery documents in which it chooses the <code>authorization_endpoint</code> value. Inspector passes that URL to the <code>open</code> package, which on Windows and on WSL outside a container places it inside a PowerShell double-quoted string and runs it with <code>-EncodedCommand</code>. PowerShell evaluates <code>$(...)</code> subexpressions inside such strings and URL serialization leaves the needed characters intact, so the server&#39;s URL executes. Auto-open is on by default, so this fires during connection, with no prompt, no tool call and no model involvement. CVSS 8.8 under 3.1 on the advisory, affecting 2.0.0 through 2.11.0 and fixed in 2.12.0. VulnCheck assigned CVE-2026-119025 against the underlying <code>open</code> package on October 29 and scored it 7.7 under CVSS 4.0. macOS and Linux are not affected.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/scooby-doo-mask-reveal.jpg" alt="scooby doo mask reveal: the oauth discovery document from the server you were inspecting" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>scooby doo mask reveal: the oauth discovery document from the server you were inspecting</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Inspector exists so you can poke at MCP servers you have no reason to trust. The one thing it should never do with what they send back is run it. <code>open</code> has been putting URLs into PowerShell strings since 7.2.0, which is a long time for a URL to have been a shell string.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A server you point Inspector at runs commands as you on Windows and WSL, before you click anything in the tool.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector/security/advisories/GHSA-q978-78c7-phqw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-q978-78c7-phqw: A remote MCP server can execute arbitrary code through the OAuth authorization URL</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/open-7.2.0-before-11.0.0-os-command-injection-via-powershell-on-windows" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: open 7.2.0 before 11.0.0 OS command injection via PowerShell on Windows (CVE-2026-119025)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-153" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="denial-of-service advisory protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> rmcp Caches Every Tool Name It Is Sent, Including the Ones It Rejects <a href="#incident-153" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">DaleSeo published GHSA-4432-vg92-fqh6 on October 20, 2026 against <code>rmcp</code> 3.0.0 before 3.6.0, rated High at 7.5 under CVSS 3.1 with no CVE, crediting manus-use, manus-pi and NovelliSimone. The Streamable HTTP service cached the result of <code>ServerHandler::get_tool</code> in a process-wide map keyed by the tool name taken from the incoming <code>tools/call</code> request, with no size limit and no eviction, and it stored names matching no tool at all. The entry was created during header validation, so a request the server went on to reject with HTTP 400 had already added one. Servers receiving requests at protocol version 2026-07-28 or later are affected, the cache arrived in 3.0.0, and 3.6.0 removes it so the transport calls <code>get_tool</code> per request. Lowering <code>max_request_body_bytes</code> slows the growth without stopping it.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">CVSS 7.5, filed Medium here, because a crash is the whole prize. The detail worth keeping is which code path fills the map: the request got far enough to be rejected with a 400 and still paid for its entry. Refusing to answer a request and remembering it forever is an odd pair of behaviours to ship together.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller sends requests carrying distinct invented tool names until the process runs out of memory. Nothing is read or written.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-4432-vg92-fqh6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Rust SDK GHSA-4432-vg92-fqh6: Unauthenticated unbounded tool_schemas cache growth in rmcp Streamable HTTP server transport</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-155" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication privilege-escalation web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-111958</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Royal MCP&#39;s Preview Link Token Is an Administrator Session for the Entire REST API <a href="#incident-155" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Raphael P. Cigana disclosed CVE-2026-111958 through WPScan on October 20, 2026 against the Royal MCP WordPress plugin before 1.5.7. The plugin does not limit a preview-link token to the single preview it was issued for, so any party holding a valid token is authenticated as the user who owns it, an administrator in the default configuration, across the whole REST API and with no nonce required. Version 1.5.7 fixes it. WPScan scores it 7.5 under CVSS 3.1 and is holding the proof of concept until November 20, 2026 to give sites time to update.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A preview token exists so one person can look at one unpublished thing. This one authenticates its bearer as its owner everywhere, which makes the most casually shared artifact the plugin issues also the most privileged. The embargoed proof of concept is the right call and does not change the disclosure date.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A preview link that leaks is a full site takeover. Preview links are made to be shared, and they travel through tickets, chat and email as a matter of routine.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/014f061c-54f3-491e-b4a0-85cfa6fb178b/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: Royal MCP &lt; 1.5.7, Authentication Bypass via Preview Link Token (CVE-2026-111958)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-156" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="credential-theft ssrf authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-115750, CVE-2026-115751</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-from-openapi Forwards Your Authorization Header Wherever the Spec Redirects <a href="#incident-156" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">frontegg-david published GHSA-ff5q-5r4v-fw4v on October 20, 2026 against the npm package <code>mcp-from-openapi</code> at 2.8.0 and earlier, fixed in 2.9.0, crediting zachariah-mithani, who found both issues with the AploSec scanner and reported them on October 16. The package&#39;s manual redirect loop re-sends caller-supplied headers, <code>Authorization</code> and <code>Cookie</code> among them, to whatever origin it is redirected to; the platform <code>fetch</code> strips <code>Authorization</code> across origins and this loop does not. The second finding is that <code>ssrf.blockedHosts</code> matching is an exact string comparison with no normalization, so a trailing dot appended to a hostname evades a denylist entry. The maintainer scores the pair 3.7 under CVSS 3.1 and the advisory says it has no known CVE. VulnCheck assigned CVE-2026-115750 and CVE-2026-115751 and scores each 6.3 under CVSS 4.0 and 3.7 under 3.1, matching the maintainer wherever the two are comparable.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The redirect leak is the same finding the official TypeScript and Python SDKs both patched on October 2, and <code>rmcp</code> on July 2. What the platform gives you for free is narrower than it looks: <code>fetch</code> drops <code>Authorization</code> across origins, which is why the SDK advisories are about custom headers and 307 bodies instead. This loop is hand-rolled, so it re-sent the one header <code>fetch</code> already had covered.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A spec host that is hostile, or that merely carries an open redirect, collects the bearer token the caller handed to `fromURL`. The trailing-dot bypass is a defence-in-depth gap rather than a route to data on its own.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/agentfront/mcp-from-openapi/security/advisories/GHSA-ff5q-5r4v-fw4v" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-from-openapi GHSA-ff5q-5r4v-fw4v: Caller headers forwarded to cross-origin redirect targets; blockedHosts bypass with trailing dot</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-from-openapi-from-2.5.0-before-2.9.0-credential-leak-via-cross-origin-redirects" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-115750: credential leak via cross-origin redirects</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-from-openapi-2.2.0-before-2.9.0-ssrf-blockedhosts-bypass-via-trailing-dot-hostname" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-115751: ssrf.blockedHosts bypass via trailing-dot hostname</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-115750" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-115750 (VulnCheck scores, CVSS 4.0 and 3.1)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-115751" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-115751 (VulnCheck scores, CVSS 4.0 and 3.1)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 19</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-178" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-121419</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> SurrealDB&#39;s MCP Session Fingerprint Is the Access Method, Not the User <a href="#incident-178" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">rushmorem published GHSA-pgh9-49q4-hqcx on October 19, 2026 against SurrealDB 3.1.0 before 3.3.0, rated High at 8.1 under CVSS 3.1 and crediting Yanhaoxi. The MCP HTTP transport checks on every request that the caller&#39;s identity matches the identity bound to the session. For JWT and Bearer access methods defined with <code>DEFINE ACCESS</code>, that identity is the name of the access method rather than the individual user, so every user of one method produces the same fingerprint and the check cannot tell two of them apart. An attacker holding any valid token for the method, including one with the lowest role, plus the victim&#39;s <code>mcp-session-id</code>, runs MCP tools with the victim&#39;s roles and permissions. The reporter reproduced it on the released 3.2.0 binary and on master by writing to a record their own permissions forbid. Accounts authenticated through the user and password path are unaffected. VulnCheck filed CVE-2026-121419 on November 3 and scores it 8.6 under CVSS 4.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A session check that cannot distinguish two sessions is a session check the way a lock keyed to &quot;door&quot; is a lock. The token already carries a per-user claim; the fingerprint was built from the wrong field.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any authenticated user of a shared access method who gets hold of a session id takes over that session and acts with the other user&#39;s permissions.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/surrealdb/surrealdb/security/advisories/GHSA-pgh9-49q4-hqcx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-pgh9-49q4-hqcx: MCP session hijack via BoundSubject collision in SurrealDB</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/surrealdb-3.1.0-before-3.3.0-mcp-session-hijack-via-boundsubject-collision" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: SurrealDB 3.1.0 before 3.3.0 MCP session hijack via BoundSubject collision (CVE-2026-121419)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 16</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-150" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="rce command-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> FastMCP&#39;s Client Executes Python From a Malicious Server&#39;s Output Schema <a href="#incident-150" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">zzstoatzz published GHSA-gpgx-58w2-xwwf on October 16, 2026 against the <code>fastmcp</code> PyPI package, rated Critical, with no CVE, crediting jlmitra-ampl. A malicious MCP server returns a successful structured tool result whose output schema carries a self-referencing <code>title</code>. FastMCP&#39;s schema type converter passes that title to <code>ForwardRef</code> without the sanitization the rest of the same file applies, and the client evaluates it as Python. The surrounding exception handler only logs, so the tool call still reads as a success. Affected versions are 2.10.0 up to but not including 3.4.8, and 4.0.0a1 up to but not including 4.0.11, fixed in 3.4.8 and 4.0.11, with no fixed 2.x release. The advisory records that the same converter also processes server-controlled form elicitation schemas, which the reporter flagged for separate assessment, and bounds the impact by the client process&#39;s filesystem, network and credential access.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/whisper-goosebumps.jpg" alt="a whisper into an ear, then goosebumps: one schema title, evaluated" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>a whisper into an ear, then goosebumps: one schema title, evaluated</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The direction is the story. This is not a server being attacked, it is a client being taken over by the server it was pointed at, and people add MCP servers by pasting a config snippet. The logging is the part that stings: the one signal that something went wrong was written to a log and the call returned normally.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any MCP server a FastMCP client connects to runs code in the client process, with that process&#39;s files, network reach and credentials. The only workaround offered is to connect clients to trusted servers.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/jlowin/fastmcp/security/advisories/GHSA-gpgx-58w2-xwwf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FastMCP GHSA-gpgx-58w2-xwwf: Remote code execution in FastMCP client when parsing a server-supplied tool output schema</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 16</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-151" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="advisory authorization authentication denial-of-service"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> FastMCP Publishes Sixteen Security Advisories in Five Days <a href="#incident-151" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">FastMCP published sixteen advisories between October 16 and October 20, 2026: thirteen on October 16, one on October 18 and two on October 20. One is Critical, five are High, nine are Moderate and one is Low, and none of the sixteen carries a CVE. The Critical one is the client-side code execution carried here as id 150, which is why this entry is filed High. Two are incomplete fixes of earlier work. GHSA-99xm-8q53-5c8p reopens CVE-2026-32871, OpenAPI path parameters set to <code>..</code> reaching other backend routes, and GHSA-57vm-5fr9-3r5x reopens CVE-2025-64340, command injection in the claude-code and gemini-cli installers on Windows. The authorization findings cluster: <code>ToolInjectionMiddleware</code> bypassing per-tool authorization (GHSA-mhwm-p596-p7q4), component manager enable and disable routes skipping the server auth provider when <code>required_scopes</code> is unset (GHSA-63pr-7fpf-v477), post-transform component authorization skipped on direct calls (GHSA-7xgj-8392-5q4r), and <code>host_origin_protection</code> silently ignored on the SSE transport (GHSA-6868-q46w-rmjv). The resource-exhaustion pair is an unbounded CIMD cache in the OAuth proxy (GHSA-2qh8-g8rv-3mx5) and unknown JWT key IDs each triggering a JWKS fetch at the identity provider (GHSA-p8g9-xjch-9mxg).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/charlie-conspiracy.jpg" alt="charlie at the conspiracy board: sixteen fastmcp advisories, five days, zero cves" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>charlie at the conspiracy board: sixteen fastmcp advisories, five days, zero cves</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Sixteen advisories in five days reads as a framework that finally got a thorough audit, and the two incomplete fixes suggest whoever did it went back to check whether the previous patches held. <code>host_origin_protection</code> being ignored on SSE is the one to read twice. The setting exists, operators set it, and that transport never looked.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>FastMCP is one of the most widely deployed MCP server frameworks. The sixteen land across the OAuth proxy, the OpenAPI provider, the authorization middleware, the skill loader and the installers, so no single upgrade note covers an operator&#39;s exposure.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/jlowin/fastmcp/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FastMCP security advisories (repository listing)</a><a href="https://github.com/jlowin/fastmcp/security/advisories/GHSA-99xm-8q53-5c8p" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FastMCP GHSA-99xm-8q53-5c8p: OpenAPI path parameters set to &quot;..&quot; reach other backend routes (incomplete fix for CVE-2026-32871)</a><a href="https://github.com/jlowin/fastmcp/security/advisories/GHSA-57vm-5fr9-3r5x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FastMCP GHSA-57vm-5fr9-3r5x: Incomplete fix for CVE-2025-64340, command injection in claude-code and gemini-cli installers</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 16</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-154" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-71563</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Apache Answer&#39;s MCP Tool Returns Answers to Hidden, Deleted and Unreviewed Questions <a href="#incident-154" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The Apache Software Foundation announced CVE-2026-71563 on oss-security on October 16, 2026, crediting Han, JunGyu and VK of AzeFox Foundations. Apache Answer through 2.0.2 exposes an MCP answer retrieval tool that does not check the state of the question an answer belongs to, so a caller holding a global API key reads answer content attached to questions that are hidden, deleted or still pending review. Version 2.0.3 fixes it. The Foundation rates the issue Moderate.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Hidden and deleted are states the question carries and the answer does not. The tool asks for answers, gets answers, and nothing along that path ever consults the parent. Moderation that only applies to the route a human would have taken.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A global API key, which on a typical deployment belongs to an integration rather than a moderator, reads content the forum&#39;s own moderation has taken down.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.openwall.com/lists/oss-security/2026/10/16/27" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">oss-security: CVE-2026-71563, Apache Answer, MCP Answer Retrieval Exposes Content from Non-Public Questions</a><a href="https://lists.apache.org/thread/msodd7g2r6xdn9wpkpdp04m9g1jb2xtl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apache Answer announcement thread</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-139" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="data-leak cross-tenant protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-111770</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The MCP TypeScript SDK&#39;s Cross-Client Leak Returns in the Package That Replaced It <a href="#incident-139" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">maxisbey published GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp on October 15, 2026 against <code>@modelcontextprotocol/server</code> 2.0.0 through 2.2.x, fixed in 2.3.0, crediting DavidCarliez and legitu1337. A server reached over HTTP that shares one stateless transport, or one <code>McpServer</code> or <code>Server</code> instance, across clients matches messages to connections by JSON-RPC <code>id</code> alone. Every SDK client numbers its requests from zero, so the ids collide and one client&#39;s responses and notifications travel down another client&#39;s connection. 7.7 under CVSS 3.1, and it picked up CVE-2026-111770 on October 20, 2026. The advisory states that <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> 1.26.0 and later are not affected, which is the version that fixed the same bug in February as GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7. It also states that the README examples for the Hono, Express, Fastify and Node packages showed the shared-instance pattern until this release. Upgrading is not enough on its own: 2.3.0 makes a second <code>connect()</code> fail with <code>ALREADY_CONNECTED</code>, so callers have to move to a per-request or per-session server.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/aj-styles-undertaker.jpg" alt="aj styles laughing with the undertaker behind him: the february fix, and version 2.0" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>aj styles laughing with the undertaker behind him: the february fix, and version 2.0</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">February&#39;s advisory fixed this in <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> at 1.26.0. The 2.x rewrite put the id collision back, and the quickstart examples taught the pattern that triggers it. Eight months to relearn that both clients start counting at zero.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Concurrent users of a shared-instance HTTP MCP server receive each other&#39;s tool results, progress notifications and sampling requests. No attacker positioning is required, only ordinary concurrent traffic.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp: Shared transport or server instances delivered one HTTP client&#39;s messages to another</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7: Sharing server/transport instances can leak cross-client response data</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp (CVE-2026-111770)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-140" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-111768</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The TypeScript SDK Caps Its Request Bodies, Eight Weeks After Java and Two After Python <a href="#incident-140" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">maxisbey published two resource-limit advisories against the official TypeScript SDK on October 15, 2026. GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453 picked up CVE-2026-111768 on October 20, 2026; its sibling still carries none. GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq, 7.5 under CVSS 3.1, is HTTP server transports and handlers that cap neither POST body size nor JSON-RPC batch length; <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> 1.30.1 adds a 4 MiB body limit and a 100-message batch cap, and the advisory notes that <code>express.json()</code> does not help because it does not limit batch length. GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453, also 7.5 under CVSS 3.1, is the follow-on: one request inside that 4 MiB limit still exhausts the heap, because validation walks every array element and object key, so 1.32.0 adds a <code>maxToolInputElements</code> option that is off by default. The site carries the same server-side unbounded read in the Java SDK as id 149, published August 19, and in the Python SDK as id 148, published September 30. Credits on the TypeScript pair run to eight reporters, among them cybercraftsolutionsllc, hewei-gikaku, shmulc8 and root-Brainoverflow.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/where-monkey.png" alt="three orangutans in silence: nobody mentioned the unbounded read to the other two" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>three orangutans in silence: nobody mentioned the unbounded read to the other two</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">CVSS 7.5 four times across three SDKs, all Medium here, because a crash is all the attacker gets. The calendar is the better number. Java capped the body on August 19, Python on September 30, TypeScript on October 15, three teams reading the same specification and finding the same missing limit one at a time. The second TypeScript fix ships disabled, so the patched default still validates until it runs out of memory.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller who can reach a Streamable HTTP server sends one oversized body, or one deeply nested tool argument under the size limit, and the process dies with a heap out-of-memory error. Stdio deployments are unaffected.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq: HTTP server transports and handlers did not limit request body size or JSON-RPC batch length</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453: One HTTP request within the default size limit could exhaust server memory during validation</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453 (CVE-2026-111768)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-142" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection rce advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-108953</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mac-shell-mcp Allows `pwd`, and Everything After the Semicolon <a href="#incident-142" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-108953 on October 12, 2026 against mac-shell-mcp before 2.0.0, credited to Bandana of HackWitHer and APIsec Research Labs. The server keeps a whitelist of commands an MCP client may run without an approval prompt. The whitelist decides on the command, and the arguments go to the shell, so calling an approved command such as <code>pwd</code> with an argument of <code>;</code> followed by a second command runs the second command too. CVSS 8.5 at VulnCheck, vector <code>AV:L/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:H</code>. Fixed in 2.0.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/is-this-a-pigeon.jpg" alt="anime man pointing at a butterfly: the whitelist deciding that pwd ; curl is pwd" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>anime man pointing at a butterfly: the whitelist deciding that pwd ; curl is pwd</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">An allowlist that reads the first token and hands the rest to a shell is not an allowlist. The approval prompt it suppresses is the whole product.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any MCP client, or any injected prompt that reaches one, runs arbitrary commands as the server user, with none of the approval prompts the whitelist exists to trigger.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mac-shell-mcp-before-2.0.0-os-command-injection-via-execute-command-arguments" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mac-shell-mcp before 2.0.0 OS command injection via execute_command arguments (CVE-2026-108953)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-143" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="prompt-injection authorization web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-109331, CVE-2026-109332</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Network-AI&#39;s Agent Hook Checks the First Field It Finds and Stops Looking <a href="#incident-143" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published two Network-AI advisories on October 12, 2026, both fixed in 5.15.2. CVE-2026-109331 (7.5), credited to manus-use, is the <code>ClaudeHookBridge</code> PreToolUse hook, which matches its deny patterns against the first candidate field present in a tool call. An agent under prompt injection puts the payload in a field the matcher never reaches, <code>Write</code> content or an MCP script parameter, next to a benign <code>file_path</code> or <code>command</code>, and the deny list clears it. CVE-2026-109332 (2.3) is <code>McpSseServer._handleRequest</code> in <code>lib/mcp-transport-sse.ts</code>, which reflects any localhost <code>Origin</code> into <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> whatever interface the server bound, so a page the victim visits reads unauthenticated endpoints such as <code>/health</code> cross-origin from a non-loopback deployment.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imagination-spongebob.jpg" alt="spongebob making a rainbow: the deny patterns, applied to whichever field came first" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>spongebob making a rainbow: the deny patterns, applied to whichever field came first</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The deny patterns work exactly as written. They are reading a different field than the one the command comes out of, which is the failure mode every allow-or-deny layer bolted onto an agent will have until it reads the whole call.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>The hook sold as the guardrail between an injected agent and the filesystem passes web shells and scripts through, as long as an innocuous field comes first in the call.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/network-ai-5.14.0-before-5.15.2-deny-pattern-bypass-via-claudehookbridge-extractfulltarget" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Network-AI 5.14.0 before 5.15.2 deny-pattern bypass via ClaudeHookBridge extractFullTarget (CVE-2026-109331)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/network-ai-before-5.15.2-cors-origin-validation-error-in-mcp-sse-transport" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Network-AI before 5.15.2 CORS origin validation error in MCP SSE transport (CVE-2026-109332)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 11</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-141" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication advisory credential-theft denial-of-service"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-108865, CVE-2026-108861, CVE-2026-108859, CVE-2026-108856, CVE-2026-108855, CVE-2026-108854, CVE-2026-108851, CVE-2026-110616</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Eleven-Advisory Researcher Files Eight More in Four Days <a href="#incident-141" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">HaiND, filing as hieuPenguinnn, followed the eleven-advisory run of October 10 and 11 with eight more through VulnCheck. Seven are dated October 11. CVE-2026-108865 (8.8) is an OAuth2 server in AmoyLab Unla that never authenticates a resource owner, so anyone can register a client, take a code from <code>/authorize</code> and exchange it at <code>/token</code> for a token good for the gateway&#39;s OAuth2-protected MCP prefixes, its proxied upstream APIs and its injected credentials. CVE-2026-108859 (8.7) is <code>mcp-go</code>&#39;s <code>StreamableHTTPServer.ServeHTTP</code> reading POST bodies through <code>io.ReadAll</code> before validating anything. CVE-2026-108861 (5.3) is an Odoo MCP <code>execute_method</code> tool that reaches <code>read</code> and <code>search_read</code> with denied field names and gets their values back. CVE-2026-108851 (4.8) is phpMyFAQ&#39;s <code>faq_search</code> tool calling <code>Search::searchDatabase()</code>, which applies no user or group permission check. CVE-2026-108856, CVE-2026-108855 and CVE-2026-108854 (2.3 to 5.3) are three authorization failures around UnicomAI Wanwu&#39;s AppKeys, the first of which mints a key against another user&#39;s MCP server and opens sessions with the victim&#39;s upstream authentication. The eighth, CVE-2026-110616 (9.2) on October 14, is NullHub shipping an <code>auth_token</code> that defaults to null and is never assigned, leaving the <code>/api/*</code> management API open to anyone reaching a non-loopback listener.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Nineteen MCP advisories in five days from one person at one institute. The projects share no language, no vendor and no deployment model. What they share is that someone finally looked.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Three of the eight need no credentials at all: a token minted out of Unla&#39;s OAuth2 server, NullHub&#39;s management API, which configures agent instances, provider credentials, cron jobs and MCP servers, and the memory exhaustion in mcp-go. The other five are ordinary users reading or revoking things that belong to other tenants.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/amoylab-unla-through-0.10.0-oauth2-authentication-bypass-via-authorize" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: AmoyLab Unla through 0.10.0 OAuth2 authentication bypass via /authorize (CVE-2026-108865)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-go-through-1.2.1-denial-of-service-via-unbounded-post-body-buffering" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-go through 1.2.1 denial of service via unbounded POST body buffering (CVE-2026-108859)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/odoo-mcp-1.0.0-through-1.3.2-information-disclosure-via-execute-method-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Odoo MCP 1.0.0 through 1.3.2 information disclosure via execute_method tool (CVE-2026-108861)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/phpmyfaq-through-4.1.10-missing-authorization-via-mcp-server-faq-search-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: phpMyFAQ through 4.1.10 missing authorization via MCP server faq_search tool (CVE-2026-108851)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/unicomai-wanwu-through-0.6.5-authorization-bypass-via-v1-appspace-app-key-appkey-minting" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: UnicomAI Wanwu through 0.6.5 authorization bypass via AppKey minting (CVE-2026-108856)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/unicomai-wanwu-through-0.6.5-missing-authorization-via-delete-v1-appspace-app-publish" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: UnicomAI Wanwu through 0.6.5 missing authorization via the unpublish endpoint (CVE-2026-108855)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/wanwu-before-0.6.3-idor-appkey-deletion-via-delete-v1-appspace-app-key" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Wanwu before 0.6.3 IDOR AppKey deletion via DELETE /v1/appspace/app/key (CVE-2026-108854)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/nullhub-through-2026.5.29-missing-authentication-for-api-management-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: NullHub through 2026.5.29 missing authentication for the /api/* management API (CVE-2026-110616)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-135" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf authorization advisory credential-theft denial-of-service filesystem"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-108756, CVE-2026-108721, CVE-2026-108716, CVE-2026-108714, CVE-2026-108604, CVE-2026-108591, CVE-2026-108586, CVE-2026-108585, CVE-2026-108583, CVE-2026-108582, CVE-2026-108115</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> One Researcher Files Eleven MCP Advisories in Two Days Across Eleven Unrelated Projects <a href="#incident-135" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published eleven MCP advisories credited to HaiND of the Post and Telecommunication Institute of Technology, seven on October 10 and four on October 11, 2026, against eleven projects sharing nothing but the protocol. CVE-2026-108714 is the official MCP Kotlin SDK installing Ktor WebSockets with no <code>maxFrameSize</code>, so a small frame header declaring a payload near 2 GiB forces the allocation. CVE-2026-108716 is <code>mcp-remote</code> sending client secrets and receiving tokens over plain HTTP in its device-code grant whenever the discovered endpoints are non-loopback <code>http</code> URLs. CVE-2026-108585 is argocd-mcp passing an unvalidated <code>applicationName</code> into a URL, so a <code>../repositories/</code> value turns <code>delete_application</code> into an authenticated DELETE against repositories, clusters or projects. The rest run through Abilityai Trinity (CVE-2026-108756), Open Computer Use (CVE-2026-108721), Tabularis (CVE-2026-108604), InnoShop (CVE-2026-108591), 1MCP Agent (CVE-2026-108586), zotero-mcp (CVE-2026-108583), GenOffice (CVE-2026-108582) and Kortix Suna (CVE-2026-108115). VulnCheck&#39;s scores across the eleven run from 2.3 to 8.7.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yall-got-any-more.jpg" alt="tyrone biggums asking vulncheck for more MCP advisories on day two" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>tyrone biggums asking vulncheck for more MCP advisories on day two</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The 8.7 is the Kotlin SDK one and it is availability only, so it does not lift the batch. A server that runs out of heap has given the attacker a crash and nothing else. The number worth looking at is eleven, filed by one researcher at one institute in two days, against projects whose only shared property is that each of them decided separately what an MCP tool argument is allowed to mean.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Prompt-injected agents bypass read-only gates, delete Argo CD repositories, read `.env` files off disk and reach cloud metadata endpoints, and an on-path attacker collects mcp-remote&#39;s OAuth client secret along with its access and refresh tokens.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-kotlin-sdk-through-0.15.0-memory-exhaustion-via-application-mcpwebsocket" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: MCP Kotlin SDK through 0.15.0 memory exhaustion via Application.mcpWebSocket (CVE-2026-108714)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-remote-0.8.0-through-0.14.3-cleartext-credential-transmission-via-device-code-oauth-grant" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-remote 0.8.0 through 0.14.3 cleartext credential transmission via device code OAuth grant (CVE-2026-108716)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/argocd-mcp-through-0.9.0-path-traversal-via-delete-application-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: argocd-mcp through 0.9.0 path traversal via delete_application tool (CVE-2026-108585)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/abilityai-trinity-through-0.9.5-missing-authorization-in-telegram-binding-routes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Abilityai Trinity through 0.9.5 missing authorization in Telegram binding routes (CVE-2026-108756)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/open-computer-use-through-1.0.0-denylist-bypass-via-case-variant-bundle-id" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Open Computer Use through 1.0.0 denylist bypass via case-variant bundle ID (CVE-2026-108721)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/tabularis-through-0.27.0-read-only-bypass-via-mcp-run-query-select-classification" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Tabularis through 0.27.0 read-only bypass via MCP run_query SELECT classification (CVE-2026-108604)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/innoshop-0.9.2-local-file-disclosure-via-ai-core-mcp-file-upload-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: InnoShop 0.9.2 local file disclosure via AI Core MCP file_upload tool (CVE-2026-108591)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/1mcp-agent-0.20.0-through-0.39.0-oauth-tag-scope-bypass-via-negated-tag-filter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: 1MCP Agent 0.20.0 through 0.39.0 OAuth tag-scope bypass via negated tag filter (CVE-2026-108586)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/zotero-mcp-0.10.0-through-0.14.1-ssrf-via-zotero-add-by-url-tool" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: zotero-mcp 0.10.0 through 0.14.1 SSRF via zotero_add_by_url tool (CVE-2026-108583)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/genoffice-through-0.11.505-insecure-permissions-in-http-mcp-server-file-store" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: GenOffice through 0.11.505 insecure permissions in HTTP MCP server file store (CVE-2026-108582)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/kortix-suna-0.10.7-before-0.13.52-ssrf-guard-bypass-via-ipv6-6to4-addresses" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Kortix Suna 0.10.7 before 0.13.52 SSRF guard bypass via IPv6 6to4 addresses (CVE-2026-108115)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-136" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization credential-theft advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-108671</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> JeecgBoot&#39;s Permission Check on Its MCP Config Endpoint Is Commented Out <a href="#incident-136" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-108671 on October 10, 2026 against JeecgBoot through 3.9.5, credited to Yaqi Chao. The permission check on <code>queryById</code> in <code>AiragMcpController</code> is present in the source and commented out, so any authenticated user can read MCP server configurations. The record ids come from the <code>/airag/app/queryById</code> endpoint, which is not guarded either, and the configurations hand back MCP endpoint URLs, request headers and the outbound authentication tokens that go with them. VulnCheck scored it 7.1 under CVSS 4.0 and 6.5 under 3.1.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/trophy-case.jpg" alt="gesturing at the empty case where the queryById permission check used to run" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>gesturing at the empty case where the queryById permission check used to run</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The check is sitting in the file with two slashes in front of it. Whatever the reason, it shipped that way, and the endpoint handing out the record ids you need to use it was never guarded at all.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any authenticated JeecgBoot user collects the MCP endpoints the platform talks to and the tokens it authenticates to them with.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/jeecgboot-through-3.9.5-missing-authorization-via-airag-airagmcp-querybyid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: JeecgBoot through 3.9.5 missing authorization via /airag/airagMcp/queryById (CVE-2026-108671)</a><a href="https://github.com/jeecgboot/JeecgBoot/blob/e3b9dc0aefe1943d9772b026f64ed671a7c82802/jeecg-boot/jeecg-boot-module/jeecg-boot-module-airag/src/main/java/org/jeecg/modules/airag/llm/controller/AiragMcpController.java#L164-L173" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JeecgBoot AiragMcpController.java, the commented-out permission check</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-179" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization credential-theft protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-123272, CVE-2026-123271</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> One Researcher Finds the Same Resource Indicator Hole in Two Go MCP Clients Two Days Apart <a href="#incident-179" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Theodoros Lampropoulos reported the same OAuth flaw to two unrelated Go projects. On October 9, 2026 ezynda3 published GHSA-xf6j-693p-wqxx against <code>mcp-go</code> v0.48.0 through v1.2.0, rated High: the client fetches Protected Resource Metadata from the MCP server&#39;s well-known path, applies RFC 9728 section 3.3&#39;s check that the declared resource matches the addressed one only to metadata advertised through a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> parameter, and then uses the declared resource unvalidated as the RFC 8707 <code>resource</code> parameter in dynamic client registration, the authorization request, the token exchange and every refresh. On October 11 diillson published GHSA-95vg-3r9c-2v5q against ChatCLI 1.159.0 through 1.215.1, rated High at 8.0 under CVSS 3.1, with the same shape in the <code>/mcp login</code> flow. In both, a malicious server declares a different resource on the same authorization server, receives the token the user consents to, and replays it there. Fixed in mcp-go 1.2.1 and ChatCLI 1.215.2. VulnCheck filed CVE-2026-123272 and CVE-2026-123271 on November 5, scoring the ChatCLI one 5.1 under CVSS 4.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The resource indicator exists precisely so a token issued for one MCP server cannot be spent at another. Two independent Go clients implemented the check on the path where the server shouts the metadata URL at you in a 401, and skipped it on the path where the server volunteers it. ChatCLI&#39;s advisory lists four conditions that all have to hold, so this is a real finding through a narrow door.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A hostile MCP server walks away with a bearer token minted for a different service behind the same authorization server, while the user sees an ordinary consent screen.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/mark3labs/mcp-go/security/advisories/GHSA-xf6j-693p-wqxx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xf6j-693p-wqxx: mcp-go OAuth client does not validate the resource declared at the well-known path</a><a href="https://github.com/diillson/chatcli/security/advisories/GHSA-95vg-3r9c-2v5q" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-95vg-3r9c-2v5q: ChatCLI MCP OAuth login uses the server-supplied protected resource as the RFC 8707 resource indicator</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/chatcli-before-1.215.2-origin-validation-error-via-unvalidated-mcp-oauth-resource-indicator" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: ChatCLI before 1.215.2 origin validation error via unvalidated MCP OAuth resource indicator (CVE-2026-123271)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-123272" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-123272, the identifier assigned to the mcp-go advisory</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-180" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="prompt-injection llm-manipulation supply-chain advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-54140</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> OpenHuman Puts Remote Tool Descriptions in the Model&#39;s Context and Scans Only What You Type <a href="#incident-180" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">senamakel published GHSA-g6j9-89xv-v752 on October 9, 2026 against OpenHuman before 0.57.13, rated High at 8.3 under CVSS 3.1 and crediting licitrasimone. The <code>McpRemoteTool</code> records that <code>list_tools()</code> builds in <code>mcp_client/registry.rs</code> preserve the name, <code>title</code>, <code>description</code> and parameter definitions a configured MCP server returns, and none of it is sanitised, escaped or length-bounded before it reaches the agent&#39;s tool-use context. <code>filter_allowed_tools()</code> matches tool names against allow and deny lists and never inspects description content, and the prompt-injection detector in <code>prompt_injection/detector.rs</code> runs only on the user-message path, so it never sees a tool definition. The reporter built a minimal malicious stdio MCP server whose tool description carries hidden instructions, and showed them stored at <code>tools/list</code> and active for the rest of the session. The advisory names community registries such as Smithery and mcp.run as places a poisoned server could come from. Fixed in 0.57.13.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/something-of-a-scientist.jpg" alt="norman osborn, pleased with himself: something of a system prompt" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>norman osborn, pleased with himself: something of a system prompt</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The injection detector reads the user&#39;s messages. The untrusted text arrives in the tool manifest, which is the one channel an agent has to trust before it can do anything at all. Filtering on tool names and not on tool descriptions is checking the envelope and posting the letter.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any user with one hostile or compromised MCP server configured has that server&#39;s instructions acted on as if the model had thought of them, for the whole session and with no added delay to give it away.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/tinyhumansai/openhuman/security/advisories/GHSA-g6j9-89xv-v752" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-g6j9-89xv-v752: MCP tool description injection, untrusted server descriptions execute in agent LLM context without sanitization</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-144" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design advisory llm-manipulation"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToFu Fuzzes 273 MCP Servers and Verifies Bugs in 343 Tools <a href="#incident-144" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Md Nazmul Islam Ananto, Pierre Prevost-Avenel, Colin Smith, Kyungtae Kim, Taegyu Kim and Arslan Khan submitted <em>ToFu: Exploiting and Fuzzing the LLM-Orchestrator-Tool Trust Boundary</em> to arXiv on October 9, 2026. The framework derives semantic security checks from the metadata a tool already publishes, adds a CWE-guided sanitizer corpus, and generates a fuzzing harness and an oracle per tool rather than per server. Run against 273 MCP servers exposing 3,405 tools, it reports 253 verified semantic security bugs in 118 servers, which the abstract counts the other way round as bugs in 343 tools across those same 118 servers. The paper&#39;s argument is about the boundary rather than the bug count: the orchestration layer does not enforce the input semantics a tool documents, and arguments the model generated get treated as trusted.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">343 tools whose own schema was decoration, and 253 bugs behind them. The orchestrator is the part that was supposed to enforce the contract, and it is the part that does not.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>118 of the 273 open-source MCP servers tested carry at least one tool that does not enforce the input contract it advertises, and 343 individual tools do not. The paper is a preprint and has not been peer reviewed.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13651" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2610.13651, ToFu: Exploiting and Fuzzing the LLM-Orchestrator-Tool Trust Boundary</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 07</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-133" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="credential-theft ssrf authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-76286</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Splunk&#39;s MCP Server Sends Your Platform Token to Whatever URL the Tool Was Pointed At <a href="#incident-133" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Splunk published SVD-2026-1004 on October 7, 2026 for CVE-2026-76286, affecting Splunk MCP Server below 1.2.1. A user holding <code>mcp_tool_admin</code> configures a custom API tool with a destination URL. When a user holding <code>mcp_tool_execute</code> runs that tool, the server sends the running user&#39;s Splunk platform authentication token to that URL. Whoever controls the destination captures the token and acts as the user who ran the tool. Splunk scored it 5.3, classified it CWE-918, credited Kuniyoshi Noguchi, and fixed it in 1.2.1.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two capabilities, two different people, and the token travels from one to the other with nothing in between asking whether that URL should be getting it. Medium because the privileges it takes are real ones, not because a platform token walking out the door is a small thing.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>The configured destination receives a live Splunk platform token belonging to whoever invoked the tool, and can use it for that user&#39;s data and actions.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://advisory.splunk.com/advisories/SVD-2026-1004" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Splunk SVD-2026-1004: Splunk MCP Server could send a user&#39;s authentication token to a tool-configured URL (CVE-2026-76286)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 07</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-134" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authorization advisory authentication"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToolHive&#39;s Filters Fail Open: Send a POST That Will Not Parse and the Middleware Waves It Through <a href="#incident-134" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">ToolHive published two more advisories on October 7 and 8, 2026. GHSA-g7gq-2r4g-3qw8, scored 7.1, covers a POST with a non-JSON Content-Type. Where Cedar authorization is not enabled, the validating webhook is never invoked, so an authenticated client calls methods and tools the webhook would have denied, mutating webhooks never apply their rewrites, and shared, per-user and per-tool rate limits are all exceeded. The audit log records only a generic event with no MCP method or tool name, so none of it shows up there. The same path skips the duplicate-member JSON check added for GHSA-hxx3-f8cg-r974. GHSA-rxgh-62mp-jq63 is the sibling: when the tool-call filter cannot decode the body, the tool allow-list is skipped, and so are tool name overrides, because the rewrite sits in the same branch. Both fixed in 0.51.4, and neither carries a CVE.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/futurama-fry.jpg" alt="fry squinting at a Content-Type the filter could not read and allowed anyway" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>fry squinting at a Content-Type the filter could not read and allowed anyway</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Both bugs are the same sentence: the component that decides whether to enforce the policy is the component that failed to parse the request. <code>rdimitrov</code> is credited as the finder on the first one, which is a maintainer auditing his own middleware, and that is the most encouraging detail in this entry.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated client reaches tools the operator removed from the allow-list and methods the webhook would deny, at whatever rate it likes, and the audit log shows nothing useful afterwards.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-g7gq-2r4g-3qw8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToolHive GHSA-g7gq-2r4g-3qw8: webhook and rate-limit middlewares are bypassed by a POST with a non-JSON Content-Type</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-rxgh-62mp-jq63" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToolHive GHSA-rxgh-62mp-jq63: tool allow-list is skipped when the tool-call filter cannot decode the body</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 06</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-131" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain ai-supply-chain protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> OX Security Indexes 15,465 Public MCP Servers and Finds Six Sitting on Expired Domains <a href="#incident-131" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">OX Security published a census of 15,465 publicly indexed MCP servers drawn from five MCP registries, deduplicated to 5,095 unique hostnames. 15.6 percent of those hostnames resolve to infrastructure outside the United States, 19 of them in China and 18 in Russia. About 0.45 percent route through consumer tunneling services, mostly ngrok&#39;s free tier, which means the server is running on somebody&#39;s own machine. 2.3 percent no longer resolve at all, and six of those sit on lapsed domains that can be registered for between four and twelve dollars a year. Moshe Siman Tov Bustan, who leads the research team, wrote the public summary on October 6, 2026.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A registry entry is a promise that a hostname still means what it meant when somebody added it. Four dollars buys the other end of that promise. The report&#39;s own line about marketplaces is the one to keep: <em>security is a recommendation, not a policy.</em></div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Six listed MCP servers can be taken over by anyone willing to pay a domain registration fee, and agents still configured against them will keep connecting and sending whatever they were going to send.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ox.security/ebooks/15465-mcp-servers-0-governance/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OX Security: 15,465 MCP Servers, 0 Governance</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/welcome-to-jungle-what-we-found-inside.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welcome to the Jungle: What We Found Inside 15,465 Public MCP Servers, by Moshe Siman Tov Bustan of OX Security, October 6, 2026</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 06</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-132" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication credential-theft authorization advisory privilege-escalation"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-105138, CVE-2026-105139, CVE-2026-105140</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Obot&#39;s CLI Login Mints an API Key and Hands It to Whoever Sent the Link <a href="#incident-132" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Obot published four advisories on October 6 and 7, 2026. The worst is GHSA-2qcm-3gv6-2mfc, scored 8.1: an unauthenticated attacker starts a CLI login, sends the resulting link to a signed-in user, and receives that user&#39;s API key when they open it, with scopes and lifetime the attacker chose. CVE-2026-105138, scored 6.5, left static configuration values on MCP catalog entries readable by every user who could see the entry, which Obot&#39;s own advisory describes as the API keys and tokens for the services behind those MCP servers. CVE-2026-105139, scored 4.3, let a user read resources and use prompts from vMCP components their profile did not grant, the profile holding as a boundary for tools and nothing else. CVE-2026-105140, scored 4.2, is a race in auth provider group refreshes that could restore a group membership just revoked in the identity provider, for about one refresh interval. Reporters were daniel-mertz and xfscott. The patch release differs per advisory: the CLI login flaw is fixed in 0.26.0, the catalog and vMCP flaws in 0.26.2, and the group refresh race in 0.25.6 and 0.26.1, with 0.26.0 itself listed as affected by that last one.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Obot has been here before, for an authorization fix that covered <code>/mcp-connect/</code> and left <code>/mcp-connect-composite/</code> open, and the question is the same one again: who does this request belong to. The catalog entry is worse than it scores. Obot says plainly that upgrading moves the secrets into protected storage and cannot undo the exposure that already happened.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One opened link gives an attacker a persistent API key carrying the victim&#39;s access to the Obot API, the LLM gateway and every MCP server behind it. If the victim is an admin, so is the attacker.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-2qcm-3gv6-2mfc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Obot GHSA-2qcm-3gv6-2mfc: CLI login flow could be used to steal another user&#39;s API key</a><a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-q5wf-87f5-cxgq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Obot GHSA-q5wf-87f5-cxgq: static configuration values on catalog entries were readable by users with access to the entry (CVE-2026-105138)</a><a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-xhpw-65qw-wj6m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Obot GHSA-xhpw-65qw-wj6m: vMCP profiles did not restrict prompts and resources (CVE-2026-105139)</a><a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-929v-v9hq-5xhr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Obot GHSA-929v-v9hq-5xhr: group membership removed in the identity provider could be briefly restored (CVE-2026-105140)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 05</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-130" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem credential-theft advisory authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-89039</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Grafana&#39;s mcp-k6 Reads Any File You Name, Unless You Prefix It With @ <a href="#incident-130" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Grafana published CVE-2026-89039 on October 5, 2026 against mcp-k6, its MCP server for k6 load testing. A caller who can invoke the <code>convert_playwright_script</code> prompt passes a bare filesystem path as the <code>playwright_script</code> argument and receives the contents of any file the server process can read, including SSH keys and cloud credentials in that user&#39;s home directory. The server does apply a working-directory restriction, but only to arguments prefixed with <code>@</code>, and that restriction can itself be escaped with a symbolic link inside the working directory pointing outside it. Grafana scored it 6.5, filed it Medium, and fixed it in 0.7.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/squidward-window.jpg" alt="squidward at the blinds watching a bare path skip the @-only working-directory check" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>squidward at the blinds watching a bare path skip the @-only working-directory check</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The path check exists. It was wired to the one argument form that looks like it needs checking, and the bare path walks straight past it. Filed High against Grafana&#39;s 6.5 because what the attacker leaves with is the contents of <code>~/.ssh</code>, which is not an information leak so much as a credential handover.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone able to reach the prompt reads arbitrary files as the user running the server, SSH private keys and cloud credential files included.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://grafana.com/security/security-advisories/cve-2026-89039" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grafana security advisory CVE-2026-89039: arbitrary file read via the convert_playwright_script prompt in mcp-k6</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-128" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication credential-theft protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-111767, CVE-2026-111769, CVE-2026-111771, CVE-2026-116684, CVE-2026-116685</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Both Official MCP SDKs Followed Cross-Origin Redirects With the Credentials Still Attached <a href="#incident-128" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The TypeScript and Python SDK maintainers published matching advisories on October 2 and October 5, 2026. GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw and GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j cover HTTP client transports and OAuth client providers that followed redirects to any origin. A redirected transport request handed the receiving origin its custom headers, an <code>X-API-Key</code> or a key set in <code>requestInit.headers</code> being the examples the advisories give, plus the <code>mcp-session-id</code> header in the TypeScript case, and on a 307 or 308 the request body as well. The same flaw applied to the OAuth providers&#39; own discovery, registration and token requests, and the TypeScript advisory names refresh tokens and client secrets as what a redirected token request body can carry, recommending rotation. A lone <code>Authorization</code> bearer header was never exposed, because the HTTP libraries already drop it across origins, and the Python advisory is explicit that a malicious MCP server gains nothing here since it already receives the client&#39;s requests directly. The party who gains is a third origin the client gets sent to. GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq and GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23 are a separate finding: server-side bearer authentication could not itself check whether a token had been issued for the receiving server. A server is affected there only where it uses <code>requireBearerAuth()</code> or <code>verifyBearerToken()</code>, its authorization server issues tokens for other services, and its own <code>verifyAccessToken()</code> does not check the <code>aud</code> claim; a verifier that already validates the audience is not affected, and upgrading alone does not close it because the new <code>expectedResource</code> setting is off by default. TypeScript shipped a third on October 2, GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw, in which the experimental <code>taskStore</code> never recorded which session created a task, so clients sharing one <code>InMemoryTaskStore</code> could list, read and cancel each other&#39;s tasks. Credits go to nickelsec, dodge1218, gdegrange and cipher-creator. The three TypeScript advisories picked up CVEs on October 20, 2026: CVE-2026-111769 for GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw, CVE-2026-111771 for GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq and CVE-2026-111767 for GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw. The two Python advisories picked up theirs on October 27: CVE-2026-116685 for GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j and CVE-2026-116684 for GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/flex-tape.png" alt="phil swift patching rmcp in july while typescript and python keep pouring" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>phil swift patching rmcp in july while typescript and python keep pouring</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The redirect fix is the interesting one, because the Rust SDK shipped it on July 2 as GHSA-9g45-5xwm-f3wc. <code>rmcp</code> spent three months patched while its two most-used siblings kept handing credentials to whoever answered the 302. The task-store bug has the same shape in reverse: Python fixed it on June 5, TypeScript on October 2. One protocol, five SDKs, and each one finds out separately.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A third origin the client is redirected to receives the custom headers of whichever request was redirected, and on a 307 or 308 that request&#39;s body too. On a transport request that means an API key and the session header; on an OAuth token request it means a refresh token or client secret. Separately, a server whose own token verifier does not check the `aud` claim accepts tokens issued for any other service behind the same authorization server.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw: HTTP client transports and OAuth requests followed cross-origin redirects</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Python SDK GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j: HTTP client transports followed cross-origin redirects carrying custom headers and bodies</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq: server bearer authentication accepted tokens issued for another service</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Python SDK GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23: server bearer-token authentication accepts tokens issued for a different resource server</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP TypeScript SDK GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw: experimental tasks were not tied to the session that created them</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-9g45-5xwm-f3wc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Rust SDK GHSA-9g45-5xwm-f3wc: custom HTTP headers leak to cross-origin redirect targets, published July 2, 2026</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw (CVE-2026-111769)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq (CVE-2026-111771)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-111767" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-111767 (GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j (CVE-2026-116685)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23 (CVE-2026-116684)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-129" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization advisory privilege-escalation data-leak web-surface"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-32582, CVE-2026-39599, CVE-2026-104402</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Patchstack Returns to the WordPress MCP Shelf for Three More, and One Has No Patch at All <a href="#incident-129" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Ananda Dhakal published three further WordPress MCP plugin advisories through Patchstack on October 2, 2026, the same day as the last of the four the site already carries. CVE-2026-32582 is broken access control in IATO MCP through 1.12.0, scored 6.5 and fixed in 1.12.1. CVE-2026-39599 is broken access control in WDS MCP Content Manager through 3.10.4, scored 4.3, and Patchstack records no official patch available. CVE-2026-104402 is a different class, sensitive data exposure in Mindio Magic MCP through 0.5.6, scored 4.3, fixed in 0.7.1, with a Subscriber-level account listed as the prerequisite and no mechanism described. Patchstack&#39;s pre-embargo records state the vulnerability class, the affected versions and the fixed version, and carry no technical detail beyond that.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/laughing-leo.png" alt="leo laughing at the fixed-version field on WDS MCP Content Manager" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>leo laughing at the fixed-version field on WDS MCP Content Manager</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Third Patchstack batch the site has carried in three weeks, all from the same researcher. The plugins change every time and the finding never does: software whose entire job is handing a WordPress install to an agent, shipped without checking who is asking. Thin entry because the source is thin, which is the honest way to write up an embargoed database record.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>On IATO MCP and WDS MCP Content Manager, low-privileged WordPress users reach MCP plugin functions their role does not grant, and on WDS there is nothing to upgrade to. Mindio Magic MCP is a Subscriber-level data exposure, which is as specific as its record gets.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/iato-mcp/vulnerability/wordpress-iato-mcp-plugin-1-11-0-broken-access-control-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: IATO MCP &lt;= 1.12.0 broken access control (CVE-2026-32582)</a><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/wds-mcp-content-manager/vulnerability/wordpress-wds-mcp-content-manager-plugin-3-10-4-broken-access-control-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: WDS MCP Content Manager &lt;= 3.10.4 broken access control (CVE-2026-39599)</a><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/mindio-magic-mcp/vulnerability/wordpress-mindio-magic-mcp-plugin-0-5-6-sensitive-data-exposure-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: Mindio Magic MCP &lt;= 0.5.6 sensitive data exposure (CVE-2026-104402)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · october · 01</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-126" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="file-overwrite prompt-injection filesystem advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2025-71427</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Office-PowerPoint-MCP-Server Saves Your Presentation Wherever You Point It <a href="#incident-126" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2025-71427 on October 1, 2026, credited to Sonali Tyagi. Office-PowerPoint-MCP-Server through 2.0.7 takes file paths from its MCP callers and never confines them to the working directory, so an absolute path or a <code>../</code> sequence in <code>save_presentation</code>, <code>open_presentation</code> or the <code>manage_image</code> <code>output_path</code> argument reads or writes anywhere the server process can reach. The advisory describes the practical route in as prompt injection: steer the agent with content it will read, and let it call the tool.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/evil-kermit.jpg" alt="hooded kermit: tell it to save the deck over ~/.ssh and it will" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>hooded kermit: tell it to save the deck over ~/.ssh and it will</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A tool whose whole job is writing a file to a given path, writing it to the given path. The prompt-injection step is nearly decorative. Anything that can already call <code>save_presentation</code> has the filesystem.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any file the server process can write gets overwritten, and any file it can read gets pulled into a presentation. The attacker needs no credentials of their own, only content the agent will act on.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/office-powerpoint-mcp-server-through-2.0.7-path-traversal-via-save-presentation-and-manage-image" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Office-PowerPoint-MCP-Server through 2.0.7 path traversal (CVE-2025-71427)</a><a href="https://github.com/GongRzhe/Office-PowerPoint-MCP-Server/pull/33" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Office-PowerPoint-MCP-Server pull request 33, the path confinement fix</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="file-overwrite" aria-pressed="false">file-overwrite</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-167" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="privilege-escalation rce authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-114612</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> LiteLLM Encrypts an Attacker&#39;s Forged Admin Credential and Then Accepts It Back <a href="#incident-167" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">yuneng-berri published GHSA-7hp6-4w63-5g45 on September 30, 2026, CVE-2026-114612, reported by Haruna38 with discovery credited to Hoa X. Nguyen of OPSWAT Unit 515. LiteLLM reuses one cryptographic context for at-rest secrets and for session tokens. An authenticated internal user submits crafted metadata containing a forged administrator credential, the API-key flow encrypts it and returns the ciphertext, and replaying that ciphertext as a bearer token makes the proxy trust the forged proxy administrator identity. From there the MCP stdio endpoint runs arbitrary commands on the host. Versions 1.87.0 through 1.90.x need <code>EXPERIMENTAL_UI_LOGIN</code> enabled; from 1.91.0 the default configuration is enough. CVSS 9.9 under 3.1 on the advisory. Fixed in 1.100.4, 1.101.3, 1.102.2, 1.103.1 and 1.104.0-rc.2.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/is-this-a-pigeon.jpg" alt="anime man pointing at a butterfly: the proxy reading back its own ciphertext and naming it the administrator" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>anime man pointing at a butterfly: the proxy reading back its own ciphertext and naming it the administrator</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The key that protects stored secrets is also the key that establishes who you are, so the secret store doubles as a credential forge. This is the third time LiteLLM&#39;s MCP surface has landed on this site, and the other two are both in CISA&#39;s exploited catalogue.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any internal user with an account becomes proxy administrator and runs commands on the host through the MCP endpoint.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/security/advisories/GHSA-7hp6-4w63-5g45" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-7hp6-4w63-5g45: Privilege Escalation to Proxy Admin via Cross-Domain Reuse of the Salt Key (CVE-2026-114612)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-125" data-severity="low" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="data-leak web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar low"> <span class="l">[low]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-94486</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Next.js Ships an MCP Endpoint in Its Dev Server, and Any Site You Visit Can Reach It <a href="#incident-125" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Vercel published GHSA-39w2-rjm5-chcv on September 30, 2026, one of seven Next.js advisories published that day. The <code>next dev</code> development server exposes a Model Context Protocol endpoint and does not reliably restrict cross-site requests to it, so a malicious page a developer opens while the server is running can reach the endpoint and read back the project&#39;s location on disk, source code snippets out of error reports, the route inventory and the development logs. Production deployments do not serve the endpoint. Versions 16.0.0 up to 16.3.8 are affected, and 16.3.8 fixes it. Vercel rates it Low at CVSS 2.3.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/two-guys-on-a-bus.jpg" alt="two guys on a bus: production deployments, and whatever is running on your laptop" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>two guys on a bus: production deployments, and whatever is running on your laptop</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The Low rating is fair and the detail under it is the part worth keeping. A framework this widely deployed now starts an MCP endpoint on <code>next dev</code> by default, and the first advisory written against it is that nobody told it which origins may speak to it. NVD then copied this same description onto CVE-2026-94485, which is a separate Next.js bug with no MCP in it at all.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A developer who opens the wrong page while running the dev server hands that page&#39;s operator their project file paths, route list and fragments of its source.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/vercel/next.js/security/advisories/GHSA-39w2-rjm5-chcv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-39w2-rjm5-chcv: information disclosure in the Next.js dev server&#39;s MCP endpoint (CVE-2026-94486)</a><a href="https://github.com/vercel/next.js/releases/tag/v16.3.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Next.js v16.3.8 release</a><a href="https://github.com/vercel/next.js/security/advisories/GHSA-f87g-xv8r-7p7x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-f87g-xv8r-7p7x: the non-MCP sibling advisory NVD gave the same description</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-148" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-109527, CVE-2026-59951</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The MCP Python SDK Reads the Whole Body First and Never Lets a Session Go <a href="#incident-148" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">maxisbey published two advisories against the official Python SDK on September 30, 2026, both CVSS 7.5 and both CWE-770. CVE-2026-109527 (GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc) is every HTTP server transport and OAuth endpoint reading a request body into memory before checking its size, at two to three times the body&#39;s own size, with no credentials needed against the OAuth endpoints or against a server that does not require authentication, which is the default. 1.29.1 and 2.1.0 add one shared 4 MiB limit and a 413, though code that drives <code>StreamableHTTPServerTransport</code> directly rather than through the session manager still gets no limit and has to impose its own. CVE-2026-59951 (GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv) is the other end of the same lifecycle: a stateful Streamable HTTP server never reclaimed a session, a client&#39;s DELETE ended the session without removing its entry, a refused opening request left a registered session with a task still running, and every session kept its parsed initialize parameters. 1.30.0 and 2.2.0 add a 1800 second idle timeout and a 10,000 session cap, both of which can be switched off by passing <code>None</code>. Credits run to razashariff and shmulc8 on the first, and rharang, evilgensec, Madan301 and nickelsec on the second.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two 7.5s and both are Medium here, because availability is all either one buys. The pairing is the point: one advisory is the SDK reading too much at once and the other is the SDK forgetting to put anything down, published the same day by the same maintainer. The session fix ships with a documented off switch, which is how the next one of these gets filed.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can reach the endpoint grows the server&#39;s memory until it dies, either with one oversized body or with a stream of ordinary short requests that each leave a session behind. Only a restart clears the second one.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Python SDK GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc: HTTP server transports and OAuth endpoints read request bodies with no size limit (CVE-2026-109527)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Python SDK GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv: Streamable HTTP sessions were never reclaimed by default and kept their initialize payloads (CVE-2026-59951)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-124" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization privilege-escalation web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-103068, CVE-2026-97239, CVE-2026-97242, CVE-2026-32585</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Patchstack Works Down the WordPress MCP Plugin Shelf and Finds Four Ways In <a href="#incident-124" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Ananda Dhakal of Patchstack published advisories against four unrelated WordPress MCP plugins between September 29 and October 2, 2026. ByteCoreStack MCP Connector for AI Tools is the worst of them: CVE-2026-103068, scored 8.8, takes a subscriber to administrator, and is fixed in 1.2.4. MCP Content Manager Lite through 1.1.0 (CVE-2026-97239, 6.5) is broken access control reachable at the subscriber role and fixed in 1.2.0. WEBO MCP through 3.0.18 (CVE-2026-97242, 6.8) is arbitrary file deletion at the author role and fixed in 3.0.22. Airano MCP Bridge through 2.11.0 (CVE-2026-32585, 6.5) is broken access control at the contributor role, and Patchstack lists no official patch for it.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Four plugins, four vendors, one researcher, four days. Each one bolts an MCP tool surface onto WordPress and then leans on the role system to guard it, and <em>subscriber</em> is a role. The Airano bridge is the one to watch, because it is the one with nothing to upgrade to.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>On a site running the ByteCoreStack connector, anyone who can register as a subscriber takes the site. The other three turn the lowest roles that can log in into file deletion or access to data they were never granted.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/bcs-mcp-manager/vulnerability/wordpress-bytecorestack-mcp-connector-for-ai-tools-plugin-1-2-2-privilege-escalation-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: privilege escalation in ByteCoreStack MCP Connector for AI Tools &lt;= 1.2.2 (CVE-2026-103068)</a><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/mcp-content-manager-lite/vulnerability/wordpress-mcp-content-manager-lite-plugin-1-1-0-broken-access-control-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: broken access control in MCP Content Manager Lite &lt;= 1.1.0 (CVE-2026-97239)</a><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/webo-mcp/vulnerability/wordpress-webo-mcp-plugin-3-0-18-arbitrary-file-deletion-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: arbitrary file deletion in WEBO MCP &lt;= 3.0.18 (CVE-2026-97242)</a><a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/airano-mcp-bridge/vulnerability/wordpress-airano-mcp-bridge-plugin-2-11-0-broken-access-control-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: broken access control in Airano MCP Bridge &lt;= 2.11.0 (CVE-2026-32585)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-123" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-102807</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> OpenClaw&#39;s Read-Only Operators Can Mint a Ticket That Runs Write-Scoped MCP Tools <a href="#incident-123" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-102807 on September 29, 2026, credited to Wentao He. OpenClaw&#39;s <code>mcp.app.view</code> method issues a standalone ticket, and the MCP app view endpoint redeems that ticket to invoke tools without re-checking what the caller was allowed to do. An operator token carrying only <code>operator.read</code> therefore reaches MCP App tools that require <code>operator.write</code>. Versions before 2026.9.4 are affected. OpenClaw&#39;s own advisories do not cover this one; the batch it published on September 11 is a separate set.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/three-headed-dragon.jpg" alt="three-headed dragon: the scope check, the ticket, and the endpoint that took it" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>three-headed dragon: the scope check, the ticket, and the endpoint that took it</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The scope gets checked once and spent twice. <code>mcp.app.view</code> reads the token, decides a read-only caller may look, and hands back a ticket that the next endpoint treats as proof of everything. A capability minted from a permission check outlives the check that justified it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An OpenClaw operator issued a read-only token invokes state-changing MCP tools, which is the entire distinction the two scopes exist to draw.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/openclaw-before-2026.9.4-authorization-bypass-via-mcp-app-standalone-ticket" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: OpenClaw before 2026.9.4 authorization bypass via MCP app standalone ticket (CVE-2026-102807)</a><a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/3bd8ec2b39b5f9e80aef0973f7d17eadc745b8f8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenClaw commit 3bd8ec2, the fix</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 28</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-168" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf filesystem advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-116686</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The MCP Python SDK Client Fetched Whatever `$ref` a Server Put in Its Output Schema <a href="#incident-168" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The official Python SDK published GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j on September 28, 2026, fixed in 1.30.0 and 2.2.0. <code>ClientSession</code>, <code>ClientSessionGroup</code> and <code>Client</code> validated server-declared <code>outputSchema</code> documents with jsonschema&#39;s default resolver, so an unresolved <code>$ref</code> in that schema made the client fetch an HTTP, HTTPS or file URL from its own network and filesystem. Resolution ran synchronously on the event loop with no timeout, so one slow URL stalled every session in the process. It carries CVE-2026-116686, scored 6.5 under CVSS 3.1 on the advisory, and added to the GitHub Advisory Database on October 27. The SDK&#39;s other two advisories from the following week, the cross-origin redirect leak and the bearer-audience check, are on id 128.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The usual mental model for this protocol has the server at risk from the client. Here the server writes a schema and the client goes and fetches whatever it points at, from wherever the client happens to sit.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A server the client connects to makes it send blind requests from the client&#39;s network, open a local file through a `file:` reference (the advisory notes the contents are used only as a schema and never sent back), or stall every session in the process.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Python SDK GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j: client fetched server-chosen $ref URLs while validating tool results</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j (CVE-2026-116686)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-122" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authentication authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToolHive&#39;s OAuth Callback Accepts a Login From a Browser That Never Started the Flow <a href="#incident-122" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Stacklok published GHSA-2gjv-f568-6cxp against ToolHive on September 26, 2026, rated High at CVSS 8.1. The <code>/oauth/callback</code> endpoint ties a callback to a pending authorization using the <code>state</code> parameter alone and never binds that state to the browser that began the flow. An attacker with a registered client starts an authorization, lifts the upstream identity provider URL out of the redirect, and gets a victim to open it. The victim authenticates upstream and the server issues an authorization code for the victim&#39;s identity to the attacker&#39;s redirect URI. The device flow&#39;s verification form has the same gap, with no anti-forgery protection on submission. Versions from 0.8.1 up to 0.51.3 are affected, and the fix sets a random HttpOnly SameSite=Lax cookie at authorization time and requires it at the callback.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body"><code>state</code> proves a callback belongs to some flow somebody started. It was never built to prove the callback belongs to <em>yours</em>, which is what the cookie in the fix is for. This is the fifteenth ToolHive advisory since September 8.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who gets a ToolHive user to open one link receives an authorization code minted against that user&#39;s upstream identity, and trades it for the victim&#39;s access to the MCP servers behind the proxy.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-2gjv-f568-6cxp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-2gjv-f568-6cxp: OAuth callback accepts an upstream login from a browser that did not start the flow</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToolHive security advisories</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-158" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization file-overwrite protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-115477, CVE-2026-115478</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> obsidian-tc Lets a Declined Confirmation Go Ahead Anyway <a href="#incident-158" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">obsidian-tc published two advisories on September 26, 2026, both fixed in 1.31.4. GHSA-737x-cwfr-5vvc, rated High, is a human-in-the-loop failure: on a Streamable HTTP deployment with <code>auth.jwtSecret</code> configured and a client declaring <code>elicitation.form</code> support, a dispatch-gated tool call offered an <code>inputRequired</code> confirmation could still complete after the human declined or cancelled it. GHSA-r3g7-r4hr-m258, rated Moderate, is <code>create_entity</code>, <code>link_entities</code>, <code>rename_entity</code>, <code>unlink_entities</code> and <code>delete_entity</code> regenerating memory notes from SQLite at a computed <code>memory/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> path without reliably preserving note ownership or leaving a failed lifecycle operation consistent. GitHub assigned CVE-2026-115477 and CVE-2026-115478, and NVD published both on October 23, 27 days later. This site carries the project&#39;s July batch as id 147.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Human-in-the-loop is the control the entire agent-safety pitch rests on. Here it renders the prompt, collects the answer and runs the tool either way, which is worse than never asking, because the user watched themselves decline and believed it worked.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>The confirmation prompt meant to stop a destructive vault operation can be answered no and the operation runs regardless. The memory-lifecycle tools can overwrite a note they do not own or leave a half-applied change behind.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-737x-cwfr-5vvc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">obsidian-tc GHSA-737x-cwfr-5vvc: a declined or cancelled human-in-the-loop confirmation could still complete the destructive call over HTTP</a><a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-r3g7-r4hr-m258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">obsidian-tc GHSA-r3g7-r4hr-m258: memory-lifecycle tools could overwrite a note they did not own, or half-commit a lifecycle change</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-737x-cwfr-5vvc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-737x-cwfr-5vvc (CVE-2026-115477)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-r3g7-r4hr-m258" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database GHSA-r3g7-r4hr-m258 (CVE-2026-115478)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="file-overwrite" aria-pressed="false">file-overwrite</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 25</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-113" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication local-network authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-100868</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Penpot&#39;s Own Docker Image Sets PENPOT_MCP_SERVER_HOST to 0.0.0.0 and Puts the Unauthenticated MCP REPL Back <a href="#incident-113" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Penpot fixed an unauthenticated MCP REPL in May. GHSA-22qr-rp27-j9wm, CVE-2026-45805, covered a <code>ReplServer.ts</code> that called <code>this.app.listen(this.port)</code> with no host argument, so Express bound 0.0.0.0:4403 and served an <code>/execute</code> endpoint that ran caller-supplied JavaScript through <code>PluginBridge.executePluginTask()</code>. On September 25, 2026 the maintainers published two more advisories against the same module, both reported by carfeii and both fixed in 2.18.0. GHSA-852x-m8p9-558v says the earlier fix was incomplete: <code>/execute</code> still has no authentication, the REPL ignores remote mode, and the project&#39;s own <code>start-mcp-devenv</code> script and official Docker image both set <code>PENPOT_MCP_SERVER_HOST=0.0.0.0</code>. GHSA-ch2q-6x56-qg5r is the sibling in <code>PluginBridge.ts</code>, where <code>new WebSocketServer({ port: port })</code> never read the host setting at all. NVD assigned CVE-2026-100868 to the WebSocket bridge advisory.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/laughing-leo.png" alt="leo laughing into his drink: the unauthenticated REPL advisory, scored 0.0" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>leo laughing into his drink: the unauthenticated REPL advisory, scored 0.0</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The May fix went over <code>PenpotMcpServer.ts</code>, missed <code>ReplServer.ts</code>, and now the second pass has missed the shipping artifact. Setting the unsafe value in your own Docker image is a thorough way to undo your own patch. Both September advisories carry a CVSS vector whose confidentiality, integrity and availability metrics are all None, scoring 0.0, against the 8.8 the original carried in May.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker on the same network reaches an unauthenticated REPL that executes arbitrary JavaScript as the Penpot server process. The original disclosure demonstrated reading /etc/passwd, spawning shell commands, and dumping environment variables holding database credentials and API keys. Through the WebSocket bridge the same caller impersonates the browser plugin, reads plugin task payloads including code and file contents, and returns forged responses.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/penpot/penpot/security/advisories/GHSA-852x-m8p9-558v" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-852x-m8p9-558v: devenv script and Docker image reintroduce unauthenticated MCP REPL RCE on 0.0.0.0</a><a href="https://github.com/penpot/penpot/security/advisories/GHSA-ch2q-6x56-qg5r" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-ch2q-6x56-qg5r: MCP plugin WebSocket bridge binds to all interfaces regardless of PENPOT_MCP_SERVER_HOST</a><a href="https://github.com/penpot/penpot/security/advisories/GHSA-22qr-rp27-j9wm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-22qr-rp27-j9wm: the May 2026 original, CVE-2026-45805</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-100868" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-100868</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 24</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-114" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization privilege-escalation data-leak web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-96524, CVE-2026-96525, CVE-2026-96526</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCP Server for WordPress Verifies Its Nonce Except When the Attacker Picks the Condition <a href="#incident-114" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Raphael P. Cigana disclosed three flaws in the MCP Server for WordPress plugin (<code>ai-workflow-automation-ai-agent-hub</code>) through WPScan on September 24, 2026, all fixed in 1.8.2. CVE-2026-96524, which WPScan scores 8.8, is the serious one: the plugin does not correctly verify the WordPress REST API nonce on cookie-authenticated requests when a condition an attacker can influence is present, which turns administrator-only actions into cross-site request forgery. CVE-2026-96525 and CVE-2026-96526, which WPScan scores 2.7 apiece, are missing authorization on the workflow REST routes. The first applies no ownership or capability check to workflow create, update and delete, and the second applies no object-level authorization check to a workflow route. WPScan is holding the proof-of-concept details for all three until October 8, 2026.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imagination-spongebob.jpg" alt="spongebob making a rainbow: the REST nonce check, on a condition the attacker gets to pick" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>spongebob making a rainbow: the REST nonce check, on a condition the attacker gets to pick</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A nonce check that holds unless an attacker arranges otherwise is not a nonce check. The two low-scored ones are the ones worth reading twice: this plugin&#39;s entire job is to expose a WordPress site to an agent, and it shipped with the workflow routes open to the lowest role that can log in.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who gets a logged-in administrator to load a crafted page creates a new administrator account. Separately, any Contributor rewrites or deletes site-wide workflow configuration including workflows an administrator built, and reads the title and publication status of every private, draft, pending and scheduled post, page and custom post type on the site.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/d8e97a77-b70f-41f0-8d1e-0d50b6d878c6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: MCP Server for WordPress &lt; 1.8.2, administrator account creation via CSRF (CVE-2026-96524)</a><a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/f814fb88-4198-4ba0-972c-658a1d672289/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: Contributor+ workflow modification and deletion via missing ownership check (CVE-2026-96525)</a><a href="https://wpscan.com/vulnerability/79c3ae87-fc63-42b1-bb68-c7420060f9d4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WPScan: Contributor+ arbitrary post title disclosure via workflows/run REST route (CVE-2026-96526)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 24</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-117" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem data-leak authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-77825</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM ContextForge Confines Log Downloads With str.startswith(), Which the Sibling Directory Also Satisfies <a href="#incident-117" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published a ContextForge MCP Gateway bulletin on September 24, 2026 for CVE-2026-77825, a path traversal in the Admin API log-download endpoint <code>GET /v1/admin/logs/file</code>. The path confinement check uses <code>str.startswith()</code> rather than a boundary comparison, so a filename that resolves into a sibling directory whose absolute path shares the configured <code>LOG_FOLDER</code> string prefix passes the check. IBM scores it 4.9 and the vector requires an authenticated administrator. Versions 1.0.0 through 1.0.8 are affected.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body"><code>/var/log</code> is a prefix of <code>/var/log-backup</code>, and that is the whole bug. Prefix matching is not path containment, which is the reason <code>os.path.commonpath</code> and <code>Path.is_relative_to</code> exist.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated administrator reads .log, .jsonl and .json files from outside the configured log directory.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7289314" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: IBM ContextForge MCP Gateway is affected by path traversal</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-116" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-92874, CVE-2026-92628</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> GitLab Patches Its Own MCP Surface: a Token Scope Bypass and a Search That Answers as the Wrong User <a href="#incident-116" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GitLab shipped 19.4.1, 19.3.3 and 19.2.7 on September 23, 2026, and two of the fixes land on its MCP surface. CVE-2026-92874, which GitLab rates Medium, is incorrect authorization in MCP API scope enforcement: an authenticated user holding an MCP-scoped token could perform actions beyond that token&#39;s intended scope. It affects 18.3 and later, up to the patched releases. CVE-2026-92628, which GitLab rates Low, is a race condition in the <code>gitlab_search</code> MCP tool, where shared state handling could return search results under an incorrect user context. It affects 18.6 and later, on the same boundary. GitLab credits both to its own staff, Amr Taha and Chris Bonk respectively.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yall-got-any-more.jpg" alt="tyrone biggums asking: any more gitlab mcp authorization bugs, any codebase will do" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>tyrone biggums asking: any more gitlab mcp authorization bugs, any codebase will do</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Four days earlier, read-only mode in the third-party <code>@zereight/mcp-gitlab</code> server fell over to a leading comma. Different people, different codebase, same two questions: does this token&#39;s scope actually bind, and whose identity is this request running under. Putting shared mutable state in a search tool is one way to find out.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An MCP-scoped token reaches actions it was never granted. Under the race, one user&#39;s MCP search returns results resolved in another user&#39;s context.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://docs.gitlab.com/releases/patches/patch-release-gitlab-19-4-1-released/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitLab patch release: 19.4.1, 19.3.3, 19.2.7</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-118" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-93529</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> WSP&#39;s MCP Connector for WordPress Is Broken at the Contributor Role, and the Advisory Will Not Say How <a href="#incident-118" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Ananda Dhakal of Patchstack disclosed CVE-2026-93529 on September 23, 2026 against the WSP MCP AI Agents Connector plugin, affecting versions 2.7.0 and earlier and fixed in 2.7.1. Patchstack records it as broken access control reachable at the Contributor role and scores it 6.5. The public record carries no technical specifics beyond that category and Patchstack&#39;s generic description of the class, that users can access pages or perform actions they should not, such as viewing other people&#39;s data. Patchstack rates the likelihood of exploitation as low.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two WordPress plugins whose purpose is handing an agent the keys to a site, disclosed a day apart, both broken at the Contributor role. Withholding the detail until users have patched is reasonable practice and it does leave the interesting half of this one unwritten.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A user holding the lowest publishing role on the site reaches plugin functionality or data the plugin meant to restrict. Patchstack does not say which.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://patchstack.com/database/wordpress/plugin/wsp-mcp-ai-agents-connector/vulnerability/wordpress-wsp-mcp-8211-ai-agents-connector-plugin-2-7-0-broken-access-control-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patchstack: broken access control in WSP MCP AI Agents Connector &lt;= 2.7.0 (CVE-2026-93529)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-121" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection cross-tenant authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-79538, CVE-2026-79537, CVE-2026-79535, CVE-2026-79534</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Traceforce Files Four MCP Advisories in One Day and Three of Them Have Nothing to Upgrade To <a href="#incident-121" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Abhijeet Kumar of Traceforce published four MCP advisories on September 22, 2026. The MetaMCP pair is the serious half. CVE-2026-79538 is command execution in the inspector proxy: the STDIO branch of <code>createTransport</code> behind <code>GET /mcp-proxy/server/stdio</code> takes its process parameters from the request rather than resolving them from a server record, registration is open by default, and the only gate is a logged-in session. CVE-2026-79537 is a cross-tenant session hijack, where <code>getSession</code> keys the session store on the client-supplied <code>mcp-session-id</code> header with no owner or namespace binding, the per-endpoint authorization middleware validates only the endpoint&#39;s owner, and <code>GET /metamcp/health/sessions</code> hands out live session IDs to anonymous callers. CVE-2026-79535 is OS command injection in VoiceMode&#39;s <code>update_config</code> tool, which writes caller-supplied values into <code>voicemode.env</code> escaping only quotes, so backticks and command substitution survive to the next service start. CVE-2026-79534 is directory traversal in mark3labs mcp-filesystem-server, where <code>validatePath</code> falls back to checking only the parent directory when <code>filepath.EvalSymlinks</code> meets a dangling symlink.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/this-is-fine.jpg" alt="dog at the table as the room burns: three of the four shipped with no fix" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>dog at the table as the room burns: three of the four shipped with no fix</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">MetaMCP&#39;s inspector proxy takes the command to run from the request body, which Traceforce&#39;s own write-up points out is CVE-2025-49596 in the official MCP Inspector, filed again in something that proxies for it. The health endpoint is what upgrades the session bug from careless to trivial: it publishes the one value the session store trusts.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who registers a MetaMCP account runs commands inside the application container. An unauthenticated caller reads active session IDs and then drives another tenant&#39;s private MCP tools using that tenant&#39;s own forwarded credentials. Only VoiceMode had a fixed release on the day, in 8.10.2.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.traceforce.ai/security-advisories/cve-2026-79538" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Traceforce: MetaMCP inspector proxy command execution (CVE-2026-79538)</a><a href="https://www.traceforce.ai/security-advisories/cve-2026-79537" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Traceforce: MetaMCP cross-tenant session hijack (CVE-2026-79537)</a><a href="https://www.traceforce.ai/security-advisories/cve-2026-79535" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Traceforce: VoiceMode update_config OS command injection (CVE-2026-79535)</a><a href="https://www.traceforce.ai/security-advisories/cve-2026-79534" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Traceforce: mcp-filesystem-server dangling symlink traversal (CVE-2026-79534)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-120" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authorization authentication credential-theft advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToolHive&#39;s Second Advisory Batch in Two Weeks: an MCP Session Belongs to Whoever Knows Its ID <a href="#incident-120" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Stacklok published six more ToolHive advisories on September 21 and 22, 2026, thirteen days after the batch of eight that landed on September 8. GHSA-hqg7-qjgg-q779 leads: the direct streamable proxy and the transparent proxy both authenticate each HTTP request and neither checks that the requester owns the MCP session named in it, so an authenticated user who learns another user&#39;s session ID reads session-scoped backend responses, injects messages, evicts the victim&#39;s SSE connection and deletes the session, all while holding a valid token of their own. GEONWOOHAN reported it and the advisory states it has no known CVE. The rest of the batch covers resource subscriptions and <code>completion/complete</code> skipping per-resource and prompt authorization in the direct proxies, ambiguous JSON members getting past the MCP authorization and argument policies, the legacy HTTP+SSE proxy confusing JSON-RPC responses between authenticated sessions, and the OAuth client secret written to the local run-state file in cleartext.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/where-monkey.png" alt="three orangutans in silence: nobody asked who owned the session" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>three orangutans in silence: nobody asked who owned the session</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The September 8 batch was authorization wired up wrong. This one is authorization that runs and asks the wrong question. <code>completion/complete</code> is the one to keep: the autocomplete path reaches prompts and resources that every other path denies, because nobody thinks of autocomplete as a way to read things.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated ToolHive user who learns another user&#39;s MCP session ID reads that session&#39;s responses, writes into it and kills it. Several of the authorization policies an operator configures are reachable around rather than through.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-hqg7-qjgg-q779" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-hqg7-qjgg-q779: MCP sessions are not bound to authenticated principals across ToolHive proxies</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-8rgq-f53x-rcp6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-8rgq-f53x-rcp6: completion/complete bypasses prompt and resource authorization in direct proxies</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-wm2j-ch74-276r" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-wm2j-ch74-276r: legacy HTTP+SSE proxy confuses JSON-RPC responses across authenticated sessions</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToolHive security advisories, showing the six published on September 21 and 22</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-115" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="llm-manipulation prompt-injection authentication data-exfiltration advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-18875</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM&#39;s Payments Product Lets Anyone Write the Runbooks That Steer Its MCP Tool Calls <a href="#incident-115" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published a Financial Transaction Manager bulletin on September 21, 2026 carrying 47 CVEs, exactly one of them on the MCP surface. CVE-2026-18875 is RAG poisoning in the FTM AI agent server: <code>api.vectordb.runbooks.js:51</code> accepts a runbook upsert without authentication, so anyone who can reach the endpoint writes content into the vector database the agent retrieves from. IBM scores it 7.3 and describes the consequence as steering the product&#39;s AI-driven MCP tool calls. FTM 4.x is affected.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/scooby-doo-mask-reveal.jpg" alt="the mask coming off: the runbook the payments agent retrieved and trusted" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the mask coming off: the runbook the payments agent retrieved and trusted</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Most prompt-injection entries here end with an agent doing what some untrusted text told it. This one starts there, and the untrusted text arrives through an unauthenticated write to the retrieval store, in a product whose job is moving money. It is one line in a bulletin of 47.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated attacker plants content in the knowledge base a payments agent reads, and IBM states the result can be unauthorized payment actions or exfiltration of payment data.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7288641" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: IBM Financial Transaction Manager (FTM) is Impacted by Multiple Vulnerabilities</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 19</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-106" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authorization denial-of-service prompt-injection authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> A Leading Comma Turns the GitLab MCP Server&#39;s Read-Only Mode Back Into Write Access <a href="#incident-106" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The maintainer of <code>@zereight/mcp-gitlab</code> published two advisories on September 19, 2026, both against versions before 2.1.30 and neither carrying a CVE. GHSA-64mg-3vx9-g74j, rated 8.1, concerns <code>execute_graphql</code>: the tool decides whether a query writes by inspecting how it starts, and a query beginning with a comma slips past the write detector while GitLab still runs it as a mutation. The same handler also skips the project-scope check every other project-scoped tool applies, so <code>GITLAB_ALLOWED_PROJECT_IDS</code> does not constrain it either. GHSA-24vp-rch2-gjqq, rated 7.5, is session exhaustion: <code>validateToken</code> confirmed that a token looked like a token, 20 or more characters in the right character set, and allocated a session before asking GitLab whether it was real. Fabricated tokens fill the default pool of 1,000 sessions and legitimate clients get 503s for the hour-long session timeout.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/evil-kermit.jpg" alt="hooded kermit: put a comma in front of it and the read-only check looks away" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>hooded kermit: put a comma in front of it and the read-only check looks away</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Read-only mode was a guess about the first character of a string. <code>,mutation{...}</code> is not a clever bypass; it is a guard and a parser disagreeing about what a query looks like, which is the only thing that ever goes wrong in this class of bug.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A semi-trusted client or a prompt-injected agent writes to GitLab projects the operator believed were read-only, and to projects outside the configured allow-list, using the server&#39;s own token. Separately, anyone can hold the server&#39;s session pool closed for an hour with tokens they invented.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/zereight/gitlab-mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-64mg-3vx9-g74j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-64mg-3vx9-g74j: execute_graphql defeats read-only mode and GITLAB_ALLOWED_PROJECT_IDS</a><a href="https://github.com/zereight/gitlab-mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-24vp-rch2-gjqq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-24vp-rch2-gjqq: unauthenticated session exhaustion denies service to legitimate clients</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-107" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-103758</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Obot&#39;s Authorization Fix Blocked /mcp-connect/ and Left /mcp-connect-composite/ Open <a href="#incident-107" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Obot published GHSA-6fwv-3h4c-37j9 on September 17, 2026, rated 8.1 and reported by arpitjain099. In May the project closed GHSA-vw82-7fv8-r6gp, a Critical bypass that let any authenticated user drive any registered MCP server, by adding <code>/mcp-connect/</code> to the UI fallback deny list. The composite route is <code>/mcp-connect-composite/{mcp_id}</code> and it reaches the same <code>mcpGateway.Proxy</code> handler, but the deny entry carries a trailing slash and the prefix check never strips it, so the composite path does not match and is not denied. Versions 0.21.1 through 0.24.1 are affected. The advisory lists no patched version.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/flex-tape.png" alt="flex tape over one route while the other one keeps pouring" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>flex tape over one route while the other one keeps pouring</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two routes, one handler, and a deny list that knows about one of them. Four months of believing the May bypass was closed came down to a trailing slash in a string comparison.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any authenticated user holding only the Basic role connects to MCP servers they were never granted, calling their tools and reading their data through the composite gateway route.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-6fwv-3h4c-37j9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-6fwv-3h4c-37j9: UI-fallback deny list covers /mcp-connect/ but not /mcp-connect-composite/</a><a href="https://github.com/obot-platform/obot/security/advisories/GHSA-vw82-7fv8-r6gp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-vw82-7fv8-r6gp: the May authorization bypass this one reopens</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/obot-0.21.1-through-0.24.1-authorization-bypass-via-mcp-connect-composite-route" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Obot 0.21.1 through 0.24.1 authorization bypass via the /mcp-connect-composite/ route (CVE-2026-103758)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-105" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization cross-tenant data-leak privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-91998</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Casdoor&#39;s /api/mcp Hands Any Application&#39;s Credentials Every Organization&#39;s User Table <a href="#incident-105" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">George Chen disclosed CVE-2026-91998 through VulnCheck on September 15, 2026. Casdoor is an open-source identity and access management server, and its <code>/api/mcp</code> endpoint authorizes a caller by application credentials without binding that caller to an organization. Any valid <code>clientId</code> and <code>clientSecret</code> pair, from any application registered on the instance, reaches user administration across every organization on it. Casdoor through 4.4.0 is affected and the advisory names no fixed version. VulnCheck scores it 9.4 under CVSS 4.0 and 9.9 under 3.1.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The entire job of an identity server is deciding who may touch which tenant. This one asks for a credential, is handed one, and stops asking.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One application&#39;s credentials enumerate every organization&#39;s user records, including email addresses and password salts, and can create, modify, or delete administrator accounts in any organization on the instance.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/casdoor-through-4.4.0-cross-organization-user-administration-via-api-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory: Casdoor through 4.4.0 cross-organization user administration via /api/mcp (CVE-2026-91998)</a><a href="https://github.com/geo-chen/oss/blob/main/casdoor.md#finding-2-application-credential-authorization-bypass-in-apimcp-enables-unrestricted-cross-organization-user-administration" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">George Chen: application credential authorization bypass in /api/mcp</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-91998" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-91998</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 14</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-108" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication command-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-90898</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Bifrost Runs Whatever Program You Register as an MCP Client, and Auth Ships Off <a href="#incident-108" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">JFrog Security Research disclosed CVE-2026-90898 on September 14, 2026, credited to Yuval Moravchick. Bifrost registers MCP clients through its management API. A stdio client is a command plus arguments, and the gateway starts that program the moment the client is added, with no MCP handshake first. <code>governance.auth_config.is_enabled</code> defaults to false, which makes every caller a local admin, so one unauthenticated <code>POST /api/mcp/client</code> runs a program as the Bifrost process user. The 1.6.x line through 1.6.11 and <code>transports/v2.0.0</code> are affected. <code>transports/v2.1.0</code> refuses an unauthenticated stdio registration with a 403. NVD scores it 9.8.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Registering a client and executing it are one operation here, and the thing standing between them is a boolean that ships false. The remediation advice tells you what the vendor thinks the odds are that someone already found this.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A single unauthenticated HTTP request executes arbitrary commands on the gateway host. JFrog&#39;s guidance is to treat any instance that ran with authentication disabled as compromised and to rotate virtual keys and provider credentials.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://research.jfrog.com/vulnerabilities/bifrost-is-vulnerable-to-unauthenticated-remote-code-execution-via-mcp-stdio-client-registration-cve-2026-90898/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JFrog Security Research JFSA-2026-001686326: unauthenticated RCE via MCP stdio client registration (CVE-2026-90898)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-90898" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-90898</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 13</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-109" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata prompt-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Two More IPv6 Spellings Get Past mcp-searxng&#39;s URL Filter <a href="#incident-109" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">On September 13, 2026 the mcp-searxng maintainer published GHSA-q9wf-wwq5-qmjf and GHSA-f653-hpvm-8rfh, both against <code>web_url_read</code>, the tool behind four of the project&#39;s June advisories. The first, rated 6.0, blocks IPv6 unique-local addresses at <code>fc00::/7</code> and never added site-local <code>fec0::/10</code>, deprecated by RFC 3879 in 2004 and still answered by legacy and embedded gear. The second, rated 6.8, recognises the IPv4-mapped form <code>::ffff:&lt;v4&gt;</code> and misses the other IPv4-embedded forms the advisory names: the NAT64 well-known prefix <code>64:ff9b::/96</code>, 6to4 at <code>2002::/16</code>, and the IPv4-compatible and IPv4-translated notations. Both are fixed in 2.2.0 and neither carries a CVE.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Six advisories now against the same function, each one a different way of writing an address the filter already meant to refuse. A deny list of notations is not a control, it is an inventory of the notations somebody has thought of so far.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A URL arriving through prompt-injected content reaches internal HTTP services and returns their responses as markdown. On a host behind a DNS64/NAT64 gateway, which is the normal arrangement in IPv6-only AWS subnets, that includes 169.254.169.254 and whatever IMDSv1 answers without a token.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ihor-sokoliuk/mcp-searxng/security/advisories/GHSA-q9wf-wwq5-qmjf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-q9wf-wwq5-qmjf: web_url_read does not block IPv6 site-local addresses (fec0::/10)</a><a href="https://github.com/ihor-sokoliuk/mcp-searxng/security/advisories/GHSA-f653-hpvm-8rfh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-f653-hpvm-8rfh: SSRF filter bypassed with IPv4-embedded IPv6 (NAT64, 6to4, IPv4-compatible)</a><a href="https://github.com/ihor-sokoliuk/mcp-searxng/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-searxng security advisories, showing the June run against the same tool</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-145" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection rce advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-96620</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic&#39;s Claude Agent SDK Let a Session ID Turn Into an MCP Server <a href="#incident-145" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">OctavianGuzu published GHSA-h4mw-j7qp-8mwm on September 12, 2026 against the Python <code>claude-agent-sdk</code>, crediting moamenmahmood on HackerOne. The SDK passed the <code>resume</code> option to the Claude CLI as its own token with no validation, so a value beginning with <code>-</code> was parsed as a separate flag rather than a session ID. An attacker who controlled that value injected <code>--mcp-config</code>, defined a stdio MCP server of their choosing, and got arbitrary command execution at CLI startup, before the SDK&#39;s permission controls applied. CVSS 9.2 under 4.0, CWE-88. Fixed in 0.2.121, which PyPI shows published on July 17, 2026, almost two months before the advisory and three before NVD published the record as CVE-2026-96620. It is the only advisory the repository has ever published.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Filed High rather than the advisory&#39;s Critical, because reaching it needs an application that feeds untrusted input to <code>resume</code>. The shape needs no conditions at all. A permission system was argued out of the way by a leading dash, in the SDK whose job is to enforce it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An application that passes a user-supplied session ID into `resume` hands the caller command execution on the host running the agent, with the SDK&#39;s approval prompts skipped entirely.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-agent-sdk-python/security/advisories/GHSA-h4mw-j7qp-8mwm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-h4mw-j7qp-8mwm: Argument Injection via resume Option Allows Arbitrary Command Execution (CVE-2026-96620)</a><a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-agent-sdk-python/releases/tag/v0.2.121" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">claude-agent-sdk-python release v0.2.121</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 11</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-119" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization rce command-injection sandbox-escape advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-100596, CVE-2026-100587, CVE-2026-100585, CVE-2026-100573</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> OpenClaw&#39;s Owner Check Is Missing in Four Places, and One of Them Persists an MCP Command That Runs on Load <a href="#incident-119" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">joshavant published four OpenClaw advisories on September 11, 2026, all missing-authorization findings on the MCP surface, and VulnCheck assigned CVEs to them two weeks later on September 25. GHSA-wwx7-573h-pqwc, which the advisory scores 8.8, is the worst of them: an authorized non-owner sender on an external channel runs <code>/mcp set</code> or <code>/mcp unset</code> and persists a Gateway MCP server entry, so an attacker-selected stdio MCP command executes as the OpenClaw process user the next time configuration loads (CVE-2026-100596, reported by yetval). GHSA-pjjr-5qhr-5w6r, also 8.8, is the same shape through the Codex computer-use install command (CVE-2026-100587, wwwvwwvwwwwwvwwvw). GHSA-p5g8-m35v-7m82 lets a non-owner channel member approve or deny a pending Claude Code permission request meant for the owner (CVE-2026-100585, whyiug). GHSA-5m4g-88rg-69pj, the mild one at 3.3 on the same scale, lets a sandboxed coding-agent session list and invoke tools that <code>sandbox.tools.deny</code> excluded, by way of the bundled MCP loopback (CVE-2026-100573, qc9c). The first three are fixed in 2026.7.1 and the fourth in 2026.8.1.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three of the four are one question the code never asked: is the person typing this the owner. The permission-prompt finding is the one to sit with, because a human-in-the-loop approval dialog is the entire safety argument for an agent holding host access, and this one took an answer from whoever else happened to be in the channel.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A channel participant who was never the owner installs plugin code and gets a host process of their choosing executed with the agent&#39;s privileges. On the permission-prompt finding, they answer the consent dialog that exists to stop exactly that.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/security/advisories/GHSA-wwx7-573h-pqwc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-wwx7-573h-pqwc: /mcp set and /mcp unset persist a stdio MCP command for non-owners</a><a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/security/advisories/GHSA-pjjr-5qhr-5w6r" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-pjjr-5qhr-5w6r: Codex computer-use install command skips owner authorization</a><a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/security/advisories/GHSA-p5g8-m35v-7m82" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-p5g8-m35v-7m82: non-owner channel senders resolve Claude Code permission prompts</a><a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/security/advisories/GHSA-5m4g-88rg-69pj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-5m4g-88rg-69pj: MCP loopback lets sandboxed sessions invoke denied tools</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/openclaw-before-2026.7.1-authorization-bypass-via-mcp-configuration" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-100596 (MCP configuration)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/openclaw-before-2026.7.1-authorization-bypass-via-codex-install" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-100587 (Codex install)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/openclaw-before-2026.7.1-authentication-bypass-via-mcp-channel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-100585 (MCP channel bridge)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/openclaw-before-2026.8.1-sandbox-policy-bypass-via-mcp-loopback" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-100573 (MCP loopback sandbox bypass)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-97" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="aws data-leak authorization data-exfiltration advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-87912, CVE-2026-87913</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Security Agent&#39;s MCP Server Uploads Scanned Source to a Bucket Name Anyone Could Register First <a href="#incident-97" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS security bulletin 2026-105, published September 10, 2026, covers CVE-2026-87912 and CVE-2026-87913. The AWS Security Agent plugin in <code>aws-agents-for-devsecops</code> before 1.1.0 and the AWS Security Agent MCP server before 0.2.0 did not verify that the S3 bucket holding a scanned workspace&#39;s source archive belonged to the caller&#39;s own account. The bucket name derives from a publicly known account identifier, so another account could pre-register that name and receive the archive. Nadav Claude Cohen of glow.io reported both through coordinated disclosure. AWS&#39;s stated precaution is to pre-create the bucket in your own account before first use so the predictable name cannot be claimed by someone else. NVD scores CVE-2026-87913 at 5.9.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The mitigation is a race you have to enter deliberately. An agent whose entire job is finding secrets in your code wrote that code to a bucket named after a public identifier, and the advice is to squat your own bucket before a stranger does.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Whoever registers the predictable bucket name first receives the private source archive of each scanned workspace, including credentials and infrastructure state contained in it.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-105-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-105: CVE-2026-87912 and CVE-2026-87913, missing S3 bucket ownership verification</a><a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-3jxw-vj8m-8x77" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-3jxw-vj8m-8x77: AWS Security Agent MCP server missing bucket ownership verification (CVE-2026-87913)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2px6-hhjp-3g5x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-2px6-hhjp-3g5x: AWS Security Agent plugin missing bucket ownership verification (CVE-2026-87912)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-95" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication sandbox-escape filesystem rce ssrf advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-88938</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Knowns Publishes Six Advisories in One Day, and an HTTP Header Picks the Agent&#39;s Working Directory <a href="#incident-95" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The knowns project published six security advisories on September 9, 2026, all reported by uziii2208. The most severe, GHSA-9h2q-r9fh-f98w at CVSS 4.0 10.0, is in the reverse proxy for the bundled OpenCode AI daemon: <code>proxyOpenCode()</code> injected the server&#39;s own <code>activeRoot</code> only when the client-supplied value was empty, so any non-empty value passed through unmodified. A request setting the <code>x-opencode-directory</code> header or the <code>directory</code> query parameter to <code>/root</code>, <code>/etc</code> or <code>~/.ssh</code> relocates the daemon&#39;s file tree, git interface and tool execution there. Two advisories rated 9.4 cover arbitrary file write and persistent code execution through malicious git template imports, where <code>resolveDest()</code> returns absolute paths unmodified, and a chained bootstrap-gate bypass that lets a session swap its own active project root after <code>NewGuardMiddleware</code> exempts <code>project.set</code> without checking session state. GHSA-5cj9-fcqq-g2h7 (CVE-2026-88938) is path traversal in the <code>code.find</code> MCP tool, which sits in every permission preset. A sixth advisory carries two SSRF bugs in the embedding-model test and git-clone import endpoints.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/best-i-can-do.jpg" alt="hands spread across the pawn shop counter: four of the six advisories list no patched version" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>hands spread across the pawn shop counter: four of the six advisories list no patched version</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The 10.0 is not a parser trick or a clever chain. The agent asks the caller which directory to work in and believes the answer, and the fail-open conditional that does it reads as a convenience for the case where nobody said. Four of the six advisories say <em>Patched versions: None</em>, which is its own kind of disclosure.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated request points an autonomous coding agent at any path on the host, and the agent&#39;s ordinary tools then read SSH keys, AWS credentials and .env files or write to shell profiles and cron for persistence. Four of the six advisories list no patched version.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-9h2q-r9fh-f98w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9h2q-r9fh-f98w: Sandbox escape, unauthenticated header injection grants AI agent unrestricted access to host filesystem</a><a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-xjcg-5j3r-m6f9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xjcg-5j3r-m6f9: Malicious git import overwrites arbitrary host files and achieves persistent RCE</a><a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-h73x-698r-qrvg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-h73x-698r-qrvg: Multi-stage workspace context escalation and sandbox escape via bootstrap gate bypass</a><a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-68cq-4rwm-f7jr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-68cq-4rwm-f7jr: Arbitrary file read/write and path traversal to RCE via malicious codegen templates</a><a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-5cj9-fcqq-g2h7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-5cj9-fcqq-g2h7: AI agent sandbox escape via path traversal in code.find MCP tool (CVE-2026-88938)</a><a href="https://github.com/knowns-dev/knowns/security/advisories/GHSA-w7xr-3g68-fgjc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-w7xr-3g68-fgjc: Multiple SSRF via embedding model test and git clone import endpoints</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-96" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection rce aws authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-87911</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Postgres MCP Server&#39;s Read-Only Mode Runs Shell Commands Through COPY TO PROGRAM <a href="#incident-96" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS published security bulletin 2026-104 on September 9, 2026 for CVE-2026-87911, an OS command injection in <code>awslabs.postgres-mcp-server</code> before 1.1.7. The read-only enforcement in the SQL validation component did not block every verb capable of system-level operations, so a <code>COPY ... TO PROGRAM</code> statement cleared the application-layer blocklist and ran on the PostgreSQL host. Exploitation needs the <code>PG_WIRE_PROTOCOL</code> connection method and a database role holding superuser or <code>pg_execute_server_program</code>; least-privilege roles are unaffected because Postgres itself refuses the operation. Mordehai Attia (Corsen AI) and Ryan von Brubeck reported it. The fix shipped in 1.1.7, which is also the release that fixed CVE-2026-85787, a separate read-only bypass AWS disclosed five days earlier in bulletin 2026-101.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Read-only described the validator&#39;s intention rather than its behaviour, twice, in the same component. Filed High against AWS&#39;s 9.6, because the shell only opens when the wire protocol and a superuser-grade role line up and a least-privilege role stops it dead, and this site grades on the realistic default rather than the number. Both bypasses were fixed in 1.1.7 and disclosed in separate bulletins five days apart, so anyone who patched on September 4 was already covered and had no way to know it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Crafted SQL reaching the server in its default read-only mode executes operating-system commands on a self-managed PostgreSQL host at the privilege level of the database process.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-104-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-104: CVE-2026-87911, OS command injection in awslabs postgres-mcp-server</a><a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-fph8-pg5w-78fv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-fph8-pg5w-78fv: postgres-mcp-server read-only enforcement bypass via COPY TO PROGRAM</a><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-101-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-101: CVE-2026-85787, incomplete disallowed-input list in the same SQL validation component</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-99" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication privilege-escalation credential-theft advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-78573</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM ContextForge MCP Gateway Shipped Default Credentials Across Eight Releases <a href="#incident-99" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published a bulletin on September 9, 2026 for CVE-2026-78573, scored 9.8 with vector <code>CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H</code>: ContextForge MCP Gateway v1.0.0 through v1.0.7 allows a remote attacker to gain administrative access through the use of default credentials, filed as CWE-1392. ContextForge is IBM&#39;s MCP aggregation layer, so it sits in front of the MCP servers registered with it and the credentials it stores for them. It arrives a week after the four ContextForge bulletins dated September 2, which covered jq filter validation, cross-session data exposure and two DNS rebinding paths.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A gateway exists so credentials live in one place instead of many. CWE-1392 on the thing holding all of them is the half of that trade nobody puts in the architecture diagram.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated attacker who can reach the gateway takes administrative control of it, and through it the registered MCP servers and the credentials the gateway holds on their behalf.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286834" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: IBM ContextForge MCP Gateway administrative access via default credentials (CVE-2026-78573)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-138" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain credential-theft ai-supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> An npm MCP Package Promised to Auto-Sign Your Micropayments and Mailed Your Private Key Instead <a href="#incident-138" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Tamir Isaschar of Knostic published an analysis of the npm package <code>gadgethumans-mcp</code> on September 8, 2026. The package instructs users to set <code>WALLET_PRIVATE_KEY</code> and says the agent will auto-sign x402 micropayments without any manual steps. It performs no local signing. The key is copied unchanged into an <code>X-402-Wallet</code> HTTP header and sent to the package&#39;s own endpoint on every outbound request for a recognised MCP tool call. <code>viem</code>, <code>@x402/core</code> and <code>@x402/evm</code> are declared in <code>package.json</code> and never imported, which is what signing would have required. Versions 1.0.3 and 1.0.9 ship a byte-for-byte identical <code>index.js</code>, and the repository behind 1.0.3 has since been removed. Knostic recorded 2,327 downloads across July and August 2026 and reported 1.0.9 as still the latest published version on npm on the day of writing. The analysis is static, so the receiving server&#39;s behaviour was not observed.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The manifests never say the key leaves the machine, and the three signing libraries sit in the dependency list importing nothing, doing no work except making the claim plausible to anyone who reads <code>package.json</code> instead of <code>index.js</code>. Knostic could only look at the source, so what the far end does with the keys is not in evidence. That is the one comforting sentence available here.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Every recognised tool call sends a raw wallet private key in a header to a third-party endpoint. Anyone who configured the package should treat that key as spent.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.knostic.ai/blog/when-auto-signing-sends-your-wallet-private-key-to-a-remote-server-a-malicious-mcp-package-on-npm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Knostic: When Auto-Signing Sends Your Wallet Private Key to a Remote Server, a Malicious MCP Package on npm</a><a href="https://adversa.ai/blog/top-mcp-security-resources-october-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adversa AI, Top MCP security resources October 2026, which corroborates the per-tool-call key transmission</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-110" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authentication authorization ssrf advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToolHive Publishes Eight Advisories in One Day, Led by Workloads That Run Unauthenticated While OIDC Is Configured <a href="#incident-110" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Stacklok published eight ToolHive advisories on September 8, 2026. ToolHive&#39;s purpose is running MCP servers in isolated containers behind an authenticating proxy. GHSA-gc9p-rmpx-vv9j, rated 9.1, is the heaviest: a workload created through the API builds two representations of the OIDC settings, and the API path fills in <code>RunConfig.OIDCConfig</code> while passing a literal <code>nil</code> to the middleware builder. The factory sees nil and falls back to the unauthenticated <code>LocalUserMiddleware</code>, so the workload proxy accepts every request without a token. GHSA-h4mf-84xq-q2fc has any POST to a path ending in <code>/sse</code> skip the authz middleware on the transparent proxy and execute policy-denied MCP calls. The rest of the batch covers a permission profile mutated in place, leaking one workload&#39;s host volume mounts into later ones; an unauthorized <code>resources/templates/list</code>; a three-byte UTF-8 BOM defeating the tool allow-list in both directions; blind SSRF in the remote health pingers; and two OAuth credential-handling flaws. The advisory for the nil states that no CVE was assigned to it.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/trophy-case.jpg" alt="timmy's dad gesturing at the empty case: this is where the OIDC middleware goes" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>timmy&#39;s dad gesturing at the empty case: this is where the OIDC middleware goes</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Eight advisories in a day is a team that went looking properly, which is more than most projects on this site manage. The nil is still the one that stings: the configuration was correct, the plumbing dropped it on the floor, and nothing along the way said a word.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An operator who configured OIDC and created workloads through the API was serving those MCP servers to anyone who could reach the proxy, with no token validation at all.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-gc9p-rmpx-vv9j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-gc9p-rmpx-vv9j: workloads created via the API with OIDC configured run unauthenticated</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories/GHSA-h4mf-84xq-q2fc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-h4mf-84xq-q2fc: a POST to a path ending in /sse bypasses the authz middleware</a><a href="https://github.com/stacklok/toolhive/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ToolHive security advisories, showing the eight published on September 8</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-98" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection authorization authentication cross-tenant advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-85025, CVE-2026-78575, CVE-2026-81941</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM&#39;s Langflow Bulletin Carries Eleven CVEs, Three of Them on the MCP Surface <a href="#incident-98" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published a Langflow OSS security bulletin on September 8, 2026 covering eleven CVEs in versions 1.0.0 through 1.11.5, all fixed in 1.11.6. Three of them are MCP. CVE-2026-85025, which IBM scores 9.8, is improper enforcement of public-flow security restrictions and session isolation on publicly shared MCP project endpoints, letting an unauthenticated attacker execute code and access or modify other users&#39; chat sessions. CVE-2026-81941 (8.8) lets an authenticated non-administrative user run operating-system commands by building a flow whose MCP Tools component uses a local stdio subprocess transport, which bypasses both the <code>LANGFLOW_CUSTOM_COMPONENT_ADMIN_ONLY</code> and <code>LANGFLOW_BLOCK_CODE_INTERPRETER_COMPONENTS</code> server-side controls. CVE-2026-78575 (8.8) is command-argument validation in the MCP stdio server configuration: IBM&#39;s description says the Windows validator recognises only one <code>cmd.exe</code> execution switch, so alternate switches clear the command allow-list, and <code>OPENSSL_CONF</code> is absent from the environment blocklist.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/oprah-you-get-a.jpg" alt="oprah gesturing to the audience: publicly shared MCP project endpoints, and the sharing included code execution" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>oprah gesturing to the audience: publicly shared MCP project endpoints, and the sharing included code execution</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two of the three are bypasses of controls Langflow shipped specifically to stop this. <code>LANGFLOW_BLOCK_CODE_INTERPRETER_COMPONENTS</code> is an unusually clear name for a setting that the MCP Tools component does not consult.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller who reaches a shared MCP project endpoint gets code execution and access to other users&#39; sessions. Any authenticated user can reach a shell through the MCP Tools component regardless of the two settings that exist to prevent it.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286666" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: multiple vulnerabilities in Langflow OSS, including CVE-2026-85025, CVE-2026-78575 and CVE-2026-81941</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 05</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-90" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata authorization"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-86122</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Rowboat Checks That Your Custom MCP Server URL Has a Scheme <a href="#incident-90" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-86122 on September 5, 2026, again crediting George Chen, against Rowboat through 0.9.1. Adding a custom MCP server or a webhook runs the URL through a check that validates the scheme and nothing else, and the agent-tools component then fetches it. Any authenticated user can point either at an internal address. VulnCheck scores it 5.3, NVD 5.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three advisories in two days from one researcher, all the same finding in different products: a URL that a user supplies, a fetch that a server performs, and nothing in between. Postgres MCP Pro and OGX were the other two.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated Rowboat user maps the internal network from the server side and reaches cloud metadata endpoints through the custom MCP server and webhook fields.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/rowboat-through-0.9.1-server-side-request-forgery-via-custom-mcp-server" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Rowboat through 0.9.1 server-side request forgery via custom MCP server (CVE-2026-86122)</a><a href="https://github.com/rowboatlabs/rowboat/issues/621" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rowboat issue #621</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-127" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization web-surface local-network advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-102878</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-chrome Matches 127.0.0.1 as a Substring, So 127.0.0.1.evil.com Passes the Origin Check <a href="#incident-127" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">carfeii filed a public issue against hangwin/mcp-chrome on September 4, 2026, describing an origin validation flaw in the native-server HTTP API. The check substring-matches <code>http://127.0.0.1</code>, so an attacker-registered domain such as <code>127.0.0.1.evil.com</code> satisfies it and any web page can make credentialed cross-origin requests to the local server. VulnCheck assigned CVE-2026-102878 and published its own advisory on September 29, crediting carfeii and scoring it 8.6. mcp-chrome-bridge through 1.0.31 is affected.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Prefix-matching an origin is a durable way to lose. The browser tells you the origin honestly; the trouble is that <code>127.0.0.1.evil.com</code> genuinely does start with <code>127.0.0.1</code>, and its owner picks where it resolves.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A page the user happens to visit drives the browser automation tools the bridge exposes, which covers executing scripts, reading page content and taking screenshots.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/hangwin/mcp-chrome/issues/384" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hangwin/mcp-chrome issue 384: CORS origin bypass in native-server HTTP API (CSWSH)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-chrome-bridge-through-1.0.31-cors-origin-bypass" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-chrome-bridge through 1.0.31 CORS origin bypass (CVE-2026-102878)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-157" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization authentication rce advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-102503, CVE-2026-103091</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Google&#39;s Agent Mesh Guards Its MCP Path and Leaves the Ingress Beside It Open <a href="#incident-157" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">aojea published two Critical advisories against Google&#39;s Sovereign Agent Mesh (SAM), GHSA-r6hp-67wp-c7jx on September 4 and GHSA-cgmg-9xf6-rrgw on September 6, 2026, both scored 9.8 under CVSS 3.1 on their own pages, against Google&#39;s own 9.3 under CVSS 4.0 in the CVE records. In the first, a node serves its registered backend services over two libp2p paths and only the MCP protocol path is wrapped in <code>WithBiscuitAuth</code>, so the parallel <code>/libp2p-http</code> ingress reaches the same handlers with no authorization check. For command and stdio-backed MCP servers that is unauthorized execution of backend subprocess capabilities, and for URL-backed ones an unauthenticated proxy into internal services. The advisory notes that a maintainer comment in <code>mcp_service.go</code> already recorded that the URL-backed ingress lacked policy enforcement. The second covers the MCP proxy performing no authorization at all, an unauthenticated <code>/info</code> endpoint publishing OIDC configuration, and a <code>/register</code> endpoint auto-enrolling any caller holding a token from the configured provider, all under a default wildcard policy granting every enrolled member access to every service. Bans keyed on <code>peer_id</code> are evaded by regenerating a keypair. Eli Ainhorn of Noma Security reported the second. Fixed in 0.1.0-alpha.3 and 0.1.0-alpha.9. NVD published the pair on October 19 as CVE-2026-102503 and CVE-2026-103091, 45 and 43 days behind.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/monkey-puppet.jpg" alt="puppet side-eye: the maintainer comment already said the ingress had no policy enforcement" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>puppet side-eye: the maintainer comment already said the ingress had no policy enforcement</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The authorization was written, wired up and enforced on the MCP path. The ingress that reaches the same handlers never got it, and the comment sitting in <code>mcp_service.go</code> means this was known and shipped anyway. The default wildcard policy makes the argument academic: even where the biscuit check runs, it was configured to say yes.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Knowing a control plane URL is enough to enroll in the mesh and call any tool on any node, with read, write and execute on the backend. The advisory says the default deployment configuration is exploitable as shipped.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/google/agentmesh/security/advisories/GHSA-r6hp-67wp-c7jx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Agent Mesh GHSA-r6hp-67wp-c7jx: unauthenticated /libp2p-http ingress bypasses biscuit policy enforcement</a><a href="https://github.com/google/agentmesh/security/advisories/GHSA-cgmg-9xf6-rrgw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Agent Mesh GHSA-cgmg-9xf6-rrgw: insecure default policy and unrestricted enrollment enable full mesh compromise</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-102503" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-102503 (references GHSA-r6hp-67wp-c7jx)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-103091" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-103091 (references GHSA-cgmg-9xf6-rrgw)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-87" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization data-leak filesystem aws advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-85620, CVE-2026-85787</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Two Postgres MCP Servers, One Day, Both SQL Validators Short a Syntax Form <a href="#incident-87" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS and Crystal DBA each disclosed a read-only bypass in a Postgres MCP server on September 4, 2026. AWS bulletin 2026-101 covers CVE-2026-85787 in <code>awslabs.postgres-mcp-server</code> before 1.1.7, which AWS describes as an incomplete list of disallowed inputs in the SQL validation component, letting injected SQL escape the read-only restriction and modify the database. Ashwak N reported it. The same day VulnCheck published CVE-2026-85620 against Crystal DBA&#39;s Postgres MCP Pro through 0.3.0, credited to George Chen: <code>safe_sql.py</code> applies its function-name validation to ordinary function calls but not to RangeFunction nodes, so putting the call in the FROM clause reaches <code>pg_read_file</code> and reads arbitrary files off the host. VulnCheck scores it 9.2 and NVD 8.6; neither scores the AWS issue above 6.5.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/zero-days-without.png" alt="sign reading zero days without a sql allowlist meeting a syntax form it had not met" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>sign reading zero days without a sql allowlist meeting a syntax form it had not met</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Both products sell the same promise, which is that an agent can be handed a database connection safely because a validator sits in front of it. Both validators were written as a list of things to reject, and SQL is a language with more syntax than anyone finishes enumerating. The FROM clause is not an exotic corner of it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A caller who is only supposed to be able to SELECT writes to the database on one server, and reads arbitrary files off the database host on the other.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-101-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-101: Issue with awslabs postgres-mcp-server (CVE-2026-85787)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/postgres-mcp-pro-0.3.0-restricted-mode-bypass-via-from-clause-function" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Postgres MCP Pro 0.3.0 restricted-mode bypass via FROM-clause function (CVE-2026-85620)</a><a href="https://github.com/crystaldba/postgres-mcp/issues/178" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">postgres-mcp issue #178</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-88" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="sandbox-escape filesystem command-injection authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-shell Patches Its Fifth and Sixth Secure-Mode Bypass, Then Deletes the Feature <a href="#incident-88" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">sonirico published two more mcp-shell advisories on September 4, 2026, neither with a CVE. GHSA-xvpv-9qcq-636h, reported by orca0xeaa5a, is a denied-flag policy that compares flags as exact strings while git accepts any unambiguous abbreviation, so <code>git blame --cont=&lt;path&gt;</code> gets through the check that <code>--contents</code> would not and reads files outside the working directory. GHSA-gg85-6grh-63fp, reported by CyberKareem, is the wrapper problem: <code>isInterpreterExecutable</code> knew about bash, python and ruby, and did not know about <code>env</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>nice</code>, <code>nohup</code> or <code>xargs</code>, so an operator who allowlisted any of those reasonable-looking utilities got arbitrary execution back. The second advisory calls itself a surviving member of the bypass class already addressed in GHSA-3x77-wg38-92r3 (CVE-2026-55581, the default <code>/bin/bash</code> entry), GHSA-74hp-mggr-hv58 (CVE-2026-55582, the git shell alias) and GHSA-qp2m-x9hp-cj5p, which has no CVE and covers <code>find -fls</code> writing files. An earlier one, GHSA-f5pj-2738-996m (CVE-2026-55580), had already covered secure mode being off entirely in the bare-binary deploy path. Those three CVEs published June 14, ten weeks before NVD ingested them. The 1.0.0 fix stops patching the list: secure mode no longer exposes <code>shell_exec</code> at all, and the server builds the arguments itself behind typed MCP tools.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/futurama-fry.jpg" alt="fry squinting at `--cont`, which git is delighted to read as `--contents`" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>fry squinting at `--cont`, which git is delighted to read as `--contents`</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Six advisories against one secure-mode policy, and the sixth was found by asking what else on the allowlist can start another program. The fix that finally worked was removing the tool that takes a command string, which is the conclusion every shell allowlist reaches eventually and usually later than this.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Before 1.0.0, an MCP client talking to a secure-mode mcp-shell reads arbitrary files and executes arbitrary binaries through whichever allowlisted program the current bypass runs through.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-xvpv-9qcq-636h" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xvpv-9qcq-636h: mcp-shell git denied-option bypass via abbreviated flags</a><a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-gg85-6grh-63fp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-gg85-6grh-63fp: mcp-shell secure-mode allowlist bypass via command-wrapper executables</a><a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-qp2m-x9hp-cj5p" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-qp2m-x9hp-cj5p: find -fls bypasses the find escape-hatch policy</a><a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-f5pj-2738-996m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-f5pj-2738-996m: security disabled by default in bare-binary deploy path (CVE-2026-55580)</a><a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-3x77-wg38-92r3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-3x77-wg38-92r3: secure mode allowlist bypass via default /bin/bash executable (CVE-2026-55581)</a><a href="https://github.com/sonirico/mcp-shell/security/advisories/GHSA-74hp-mggr-hv58" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-74hp-mggr-hv58: secure mode allowlist bypass via git shell alias (CVE-2026-55582)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-89" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata data-leak authentication"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-85666</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> OGX Takes a URL From the MCP Tool Definition and Fetches It Without Asking Where It Goes <a href="#incident-89" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-85666 on September 4, 2026, credited to George Chen, against OGX 1.3.1 and earlier. OGX is the project formerly known as Llama Stack. Its OpenAI-compatible <code>POST /v1/responses</code> endpoint accepts MCP tool definitions that carry a <code>server_url</code>, along with headers and authorization values, and fetches that URL server-side with no destination check. The codebase already has <code>validate_url_not_private()</code> and applies it to other URL inputs; <code>server_url</code> is the one it was never wired to. The endpoint needs no authentication in the default configuration. VulnCheck scores it 8.7, NVD 7.5.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/squidward-window.jpg" alt="squidward at the blinds watching `validate_url_not_private` miss `server_url`" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>squidward at the blinds watching `validate_url_not_private` miss `server_url`</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The guard was written, tested, and pointed at the other URL fields. <code>server_url</code> arrives inside an MCP tool definition rather than as a plain request parameter, which is apparently enough distance for it to read as somebody else&#39;s input.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller makes an OGX server request any internal address, including `http://169.254.169.254/`, and has it forward attacker-supplied headers and authorization tokens to whatever answers.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/ogx-1.3.1-server-side-request-forgery-via-mcp-tool-server-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: OGX 1.3.1 server-side request forgery via MCP tool server_url (CVE-2026-85666)</a><a href="https://github.com/ogx-ai/ogx/issues/6287" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ogx issue #6287</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-101" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-63657, CVE-2026-63658</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCP Kotlin SDK Patches Two Transports That Buffer Until the Heap Is Gone <a href="#incident-101" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The MCP Kotlin SDK published two advisories on September 3, 2026, both fixed in 0.14.0. CVE-2026-63658 (GHSA-f47c-p7wj-q7gx, CVSS 7.5) is a <code>maxRequestBodySize</code> bypass in the Streamable HTTP server transport. The pre-read guard compares against <code>Content-Length</code>, which a <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code> request does not send and an HTTP/2 request may omit, so the absent header defaults to zero, the request passes, and the allocation in <code>receiveText()</code> exhausts the heap before the post-read length check runs. The legacy SSE transport enforced no size limit at all. CVE-2026-63657 (GHSA-4x39-xh35-w96w, CVSS 6.5) runs the other direction: <code>handleInlineSse</code> in the Streamable HTTP client appends every <code>data:</code> line to a <code>StringBuilder</code> that only flushes on a blank line, so a malicious server can stream <code>data:</code> lines indefinitely and exhaust the client&#39;s memory. hewei-gikaku and nirzaitsme reported them and devcrocod wrote both fixes.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Filed Medium because an availability-only bug hands an attacker a crash and nothing else to do with it, which overrules the 7.5 on the server-side one; the 6.5 was already Medium on the numbers. The client-side one is the more interesting half. The SDK capped its stdio read buffer at 16 MiB back in June after CVE-2026-53937, and the HTTP client shipped beside it went on appending without a limit.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One unauthenticated POST with chunked encoding crashes the JVM hosting an MCP server. A hostile server crashes any client that connects to it. Neither bug yields code execution or data.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/kotlin-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-f47c-p7wj-q7gx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-f47c-p7wj-q7gx: unauthenticated remote denial of service in MCP Kotlin SDK Streamable HTTP transport (CVE-2026-63658)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/kotlin-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-4x39-xh35-w96w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-4x39-xh35-w96w: unbounded memory growth in StreamableHttpClientTransport parsing inline SSE (CVE-2026-63657)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/kotlin-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-74gp-qhv5-v493" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-74gp-qhv5-v493: unbounded line buffer in the stdio transports (CVE-2026-53937)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-100" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication exploited-in-the-wild authorization credential-theft rce advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-59822</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> CISA Puts LiteLLM&#39;s MCP Endpoint in the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog <a href="#incident-100" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CISA added CVE-2026-59822 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on September 2, 2026, with a federal remediation deadline of September 16. The catalog names the surface itself: &quot;BerriAI LiteLLM contains an improper authentication vulnerability in the MCP Streamable HTTP endpoint that could allow an unauthenticated attacker to establish an authenticated MCP session using an arbitrary Bearer token.&quot; The flaw was reported by yaaras and disclosed in GHSA-7488-6r32-c95q on June 30, 2026: LiteLLM&#39;s OAuth2 passthrough fallback replaced a failed key validation with an empty <code>UserAPIKeyAuth()</code> object instead of rejecting the request, and it was fixed in 1.84.0. Wiz published 90 days of AI-infrastructure honeypot observations on August 27, 2026 recording exploitation of it, noting that any Bearer token, &quot;even just a single character, e.g., x&quot;, granted full MCP access. The same post describes attackers exploiting CVE-2026-42271, arbitrary command execution through LiteLLM&#39;s MCP server preview endpoints, by submitting fake MCP configurations whose embedded Python fetched a cryptominer while returning a valid MCP handshake so the connection test looked successful. That CVE entered the catalog on June 8, 2026.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/always-has-been.png" alt="one astronaut drawing on the other above earth: any bearer token works? always has" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>one astronaut drawing on the other above earth: any bearer token works? always has</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A one-character bearer token was enough. The check ran, returned an object, and the object was empty, which is what a fallback path looks like when it is written before anyone decides what failure means. Two LiteLLM MCP endpoints are now in the federal catalog, three months apart.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Attackers reaching an unpatched LiteLLM proxy list and invoke every configured MCP tool and the services behind them with no valid credential. Wiz recorded post-exploitation tuned to AI infrastructure, including reading the LiteLLM master key out of process memory because it is never written to disk.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog?field_cve=CVE-2026-59822" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog: CVE-2026-59822, BerriAI LiteLLM improper authentication</a><a href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/security/advisories/GHSA-7488-6r32-c95q" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-7488-6r32-c95q: LiteLLM MCP Streamable HTTP endpoint authentication bypass (CVE-2026-59822)</a><a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/ai-infrastructure-honeypot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wiz: what 90 days of AI infrastructure honeypots caught</a><a href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/security/advisories/GHSA-v4p8-mg3p-g94g" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-v4p8-mg3p-g94g: LiteLLM MCP server preview endpoints spawn arbitrary commands (CVE-2026-42271)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-85" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf credential-theft privilege-escalation cross-tenant advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-18486, CVE-2026-18489, CVE-2026-18905, CVE-2026-77822</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM ContextForge Ships Four MCP Gateway Bulletins in One Day, Two of Them Outbound <a href="#incident-85" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published four security bulletins for ContextForge on September 2, 2026. Two are egress failures. CVE-2026-18905 validates a REST tool&#39;s target URL for SSRF once, at registration time, then reuses the stored URL on every later invocation with no re-validation and no DNS pinning, so a low-privilege user re-points the hostname after registration. CVE-2026-77822 is the same idea at a different door: the A2A invocation endpoint <code>POST /a2a/{agent_name}/invoke</code> is the one outbound path in the gateway that does not pin the resolved IP at connection time, and it returns the full response body to the caller. CVE-2026-18486 is not outbound at all. A developer-privileged user supplies a jq program in a tool&#39;s <code>jsonpath_filter</code> field, jq evaluates <code>$ENV</code> inside the server process, and the environment hands back signing keys and database credentials good enough to forge an administrator token. The fourth, CVE-2026-18489, is in the separate Translate utility, which maps request headers into subprocess environment variables and shares one stdio subprocess across clients; a restart limiter lets a second client run under the first client&#39;s credentials. IBM rates the jq flaw 8.8 and the tool-invocation SSRF 7.7; NVD puts the other two at 8.2 and 7.4.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/hide-the-pain-harold.jpg" alt="every egress path pins the dns record. every path but one" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>every egress path pins the dns record. every path but one</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The A2A bulletin is the interesting one, because IBM says out loud that every other outbound path pins DNS and this one does not. That is not a missing control. That is a control that exists, works, and was applied everywhere except the endpoint added last.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated low-privilege ContextForge user reaches internal network targets through two separate SSRF paths, and a developer-privileged one reads the gateway&#39;s environment variables and mints admin tokens from the signing key it finds there.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286052" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: ContextForge MCP Gateway credential disclosure and privilege escalation via jq filter execution (CVE-2026-18486)</a><a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286056" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: ContextForge Translate cross-client credential context confusion (CVE-2026-18489)</a><a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286053" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: ContextForge MCP Gateway SSRF via DNS TOCTOU at tool invocation (CVE-2026-18905)</a><a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7286055" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: ContextForge MCP Gateway SSRF (DNS rebinding) via the A2A agent invocation endpoint (CVE-2026-77822)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-86" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="authorization data-leak ssrf cross-tenant credential-theft"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCPHub Adds Three More Advisories, and the Audit Log Was Readable by Everyone It Audited <a href="#incident-86" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">MCPHub published three advisories on September 2, 2026, all fixed in 1.0.34, none with a CVE. GHSA-94jf-cmwm-q3p5 is the loudest: in database mode the <code>/api/activities</code> endpoints build their filter entirely from user query parameters, with no owner predicate in <code>buildWhereClause</code> and no admin gate, so any authenticated non-admin reads every user&#39;s tool-call history. MCPHub stores tool inputs and outputs verbatim, secrets and API responses included. The same endpoints accept <code>DELETE /api/activities/cleanup?daysOld=1</code>. GHSA-cvw6-m995-5vvv is the same shape on the system-log routes. <code>GET /api/logs</code>, <code>DELETE /api/logs</code> and <code>GET /api/logs/stream</code> sit on an authenticated router with what the advisory calls zero authz references, so any non-admin reads the global log buffer, streams it live over SSE, or wipes it outright. GHSA-xvpg-v6pr-xc32 is an SSRF: a non-admin creating an <code>sse</code> or <code>streamable-http</code> server supplies OAuth discovery, dynamic-registration and protected-resource-metadata URLs that get dialed server-side, because an earlier fix routed transport URLs through <code>assertSafeUrl</code> and never extended it to the OAuth ones. Reported by manus-use and zx (Jace).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/megamind-peeking.png" alt="megamind peering closely: no admin check on the audit log?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>megamind peering closely: no admin check on the audit log?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Storing tool-call inputs and outputs verbatim is a defensible logging choice. Serving that store to every authenticated user, and letting them delete it afterwards, turns the audit trail into the richest single endpoint on the box.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any authenticated non-admin reads other users&#39; tool calls including the secrets inside them, streams and wipes the system logs that would show it happening, and makes the hub issue blind requests to internal addresses.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-94jf-cmwm-q3p5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-94jf-cmwm-q3p5: Activity-log IDOR in MCPHub</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-cvw6-m995-5vvv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-cvw6-m995-5vvv: Missing authorization on MCPHub log routes</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-xvpg-v6pr-xc32" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xvpg-v6pr-xc32: SSRF via unvalidated OAuth discovery URLs in MCPHub server config</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · september · 01</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-91" data-severity="low" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization cross-tenant data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar low"> <span class="l">[low]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-81846</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> runZero Finds Its Own MCP Service Handing Findings Across Organizations <a href="#incident-91" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">runZero disclosed CVE-2026-81846 on September 1, 2026, in platform versions before 5.1.260826.0. The MCP service accepted an organization UUID from the caller and returned that organization&#39;s Findings summaries without checking whether the caller belonged to it. The company says it found the flaw in an internal code security review on August 27 and shipped the fix before publishing. CVSS 3.5, and the access is read-only.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Worth logging less for the severity than for the shape of the disclosure: a vendor found a cross-tenant authorization bug in its own MCP endpoint during a routine review, fixed it, and wrote it up with the discovery date attached. That is a rarer artifact in this ecosystem than the bug is.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated runZero user who knows or guesses another organization&#39;s UUID reads that organization&#39;s Findings summaries. Nothing can be modified.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.runzero.com/advisories/runzero-mcp-findings-summaries-data-leak-cve-2026-81846/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">runZero advisory: MCP Findings summaries data leak (CVE-2026-81846)</a><a href="https://help.runzero.com/docs/release-notes/#512608260" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">runZero release notes 5.1.260826.0</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 31</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-159" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-8355</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM ContextForge Registers an OAuth Gateway and POSTs Wherever token_url Points <a href="#incident-159" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published a bulletin for CVE-2026-8355 against IBM MCP Context Forge at 1.0.5 and earlier carrying an initial publish date of August 31, 2026. Registering an OAuth-enabled gateway does not validate the <code>oauth_config.token_url</code> parameter, so the gateway issues arbitrary HTTP POST requests to internal network resources and cloud metadata endpoints on behalf of any authenticated user holding gateway creation privileges. NVD published the record on October 20, 50 days later. IBM scores it 6.4 under CVSS 3.1 on the bulletin itself; the CVE record carries no NVD score of its own, and the 5.4 on it is CISA-ADP&#39;s.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yall-got-any-more.jpg" alt="tyrone biggums asking: y'all got any more of them contextforge bulletins" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>tyrone biggums asking: y&#39;all got any more of them contextforge bulletins</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The fifth ContextForge entry here, counting default credentials across eight releases, a <code>str.startswith()</code> path confinement, a <code>getattr</code> left sitting in <code>safe_builtins</code> and a four-bulletin day in September. Three of those are dated later than this one, which is what a fifty-day wait for a CVE record does to a timeline. Gateway creation is an ordinary delegated privilege in this product, which is what moves this from an administrator&#39;s own footgun to something a tenant can reach.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any user who can create a gateway reaches internal services and the cloud metadata endpoint from the gateway&#39;s network position, with POST rather than GET.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7285723" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: SSRF in IBM MCP Context Forge (CVE-2026-8355)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-92" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization local-network authentication protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-81315</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ash_ai Asks the Browser Whether the Request Was HTTPS, and the Browser Says Yes <a href="#incident-92" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">zachdaniel published GHSA-c92r-f3rr-q49h on August 30, 2026, tracked as CVE-2026-81315 by the Erlang Ecosystem Foundation. <code>AshAi.Mcp.Server</code> guards against DNS rebinding in <code>origin_allowed?/3</code>, which accepts a request when the host matches and the forwarded scheme is <code>https</code>. It reads <code>x-forwarded-proto</code> straight off the request with no trusted-proxy check, and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> is not on the browser&#39;s forbidden header list, so script on any page can set it. Affects ash_ai from 0.8.0 up to 1.0.0 in the default configuration. Reported by PJUllrich and coordinated by maennchen. CVSS 7.4 under CVSS 4.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/evil-kermit.jpg" alt="hooded kermit: add one header and the guard will take your word for it" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>hooded kermit: add one header and the guard will take your word for it</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The rebinding guard was correct about needing to know the scheme. It just asked the one participant in the exchange with a motive to lie. Header-derived trust needs a proxy you control in front of it, and a local MCP server is the deployment least likely to have one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A page the developer visits issues cross-site requests to their local ash_ai MCP server and gets them accepted, with whatever tools that server exposes.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ash-project/ash_ai/security/advisories/GHSA-c92r-f3rr-q49h" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c92r-f3rr-q49h: AshAi.Mcp.Server origin validation bypass (CVE-2026-81315)</a><a href="https://cna.erlef.org/cves/CVE-2026-81315.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Erlang Ecosystem Foundation CNA record for CVE-2026-81315</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 28</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-81" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="supply-chain ai-supply-chain rce advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-82021</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Nous Research&#39;s Hermes Agent Tracked Its MCP Catalog by Branch Name <a href="#incident-81" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-82021 on August 28, 2026, crediting Zubair Ashraf of Helmet Security. Hermes Agent&#39;s bundled MCP catalog referenced a third-party upstream repository through a mutable branch rather than a pinned commit, so whoever controlled that branch controlled what every installing host fetched and ran. The fix commit rewrites the n8n catalog manifest from <code>ref: main</code> to <code>ref: 7a9ae00795593aa1fdb4e61ecd640e8bfd0c3841</code> and states the reasoning outright: branches and tags can be moved by the upstream owner, SHAs cannot. Affected in 0.18.2 up to 0.19.0, and in the 2026.7.7.2 line up to 2026.7.20. CVSS 8.3 at NVD and 9.0 at VulnCheck, CWE-494.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The catalog is the list of things the agent is permitted to launch, which makes it the trust boundary rather than a convenience. Pinning a <code>pyproject</code> dependency and pinning the MCP server that dependency starts are the same problem, and only one of them had a written policy.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who takes over the referenced upstream repository executes code on every host that installs that catalog entry.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/hermes-agent-mcp-catalog-supply-chain-rce-via-mutable-branch-reference" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Hermes Agent MCP Catalog Supply Chain RCE via Mutable Branch Reference (CVE-2026-82021)</a><a href="https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/commit/9df5f879b4a5925c0f8f947e7e16ed8e845932c3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hermes-agent commit 9df5f87: pin MCP catalog entries to commit SHAs</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 27</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-77" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication rce command-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-81096, CVE-2026-81098</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ToolUniverse and Telnyx Serve MCP on Every Interface and Ask for No Credential <a href="#incident-77" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published two MCP advisories on August 27, 2026, both crediting Avishai Gonen of Pluto Security, and both are the same bug wearing a different logo. ToolUniverse, the agentic tool platform out of Harvard&#39;s MIMS lab, ran caller-supplied Python behind a denylist of attribute names and calls, on a server that asked for no credential at all (CVE-2026-81096). The Telnyx MCP server served MCP at the root path from a listener bound to every interface, and parsed the caller&#39;s authentication headers in a mode that did not fail when they were absent (CVE-2026-81098). VulnCheck scores both 9.3. NVD scores them 10.0 and 9.1.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/this-is-fine.jpg" alt="two mcp servers on every interface, auth middleware optional" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>two mcp servers on every interface, auth middleware optional</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Neither project switched a protection off. Binding to loopback and requiring a credential were both parameters, and a parameter&#39;s default is what actually ships. ToolUniverse went to the trouble of denylisting attribute names inside the sandbox while leaving the front door unlocked, which is a lot of care spent one layer too deep.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any client that can reach the port executes Python on the ToolUniverse host or drives the Telnyx tool surface. Neither requires a credential.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/tooluniverse-through-1.2.6-unauthenticated-remote-code-execution-via-python-code-executor-sandbox-escape" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: ToolUniverse through 1.2.6 Unauthenticated RCE via python_code_executor Sandbox Escape (CVE-2026-81096)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/telnyx-mcp-server-through-6.83.0-missing-authentication-on-streamable-http-transport" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Telnyx MCP Server through 6.83.0 Missing Authentication on Streamable HTTP Transport (CVE-2026-81098)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 27</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-78" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="local-network authentication protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-81092, CVE-2026-81095, CVE-2026-81099, CVE-2026-81100, CVE-2026-81102</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Five DNS Rebinding Findings in One Day, Four of Them an SDK Option Nobody Set <a href="#incident-78" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The same August 27 batch carries five host-header findings credited to Avishai Gonen of Pluto Security. <code>mcp-go</code> served any request arriving over a loopback connection regardless of the host it named, in both <code>StreamableHTTPServer.ServeHTTP</code> and <code>SSEServer.ServeHTTP</code>, and its SSE transport&#39;s cross-origin handling had the same gap (CVE-2026-81092). Validation landed in v0.56.0, tagged July 8, 2026. The other four are servers that called their SDK&#39;s HTTP factory and never set the DNS-rebinding-protection option it offers: Timescale&#39;s <code>pg-aiguide</code> (CVE-2026-81095) and <code>tiger-slack</code> (CVE-2026-81099), <code>tiger-gh-mcp-server</code> (CVE-2026-81100), and Dropbox&#39;s Dash MCP server (CVE-2026-81102), which restricted its listener to loopback and then accepted whatever host a request named.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/hide-the-pain-harold.jpg" alt="the sdk offers dns-rebinding protection and four callers left it unset" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the sdk offers dns-rebinding protection and four callers left it unset</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Filed Medium against VulnCheck&#39;s 7.6, because the attacker needs the victim to load a page first. The more interesting number is the spread: VulnCheck scored Dropbox&#39;s omission 2.3 and Timescale&#39;s 7.6, and it is the same omission. The score is grading the tool surface behind the door, not the door, and the door is identical in all four.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A page in the victim&#39;s browser resolves its own hostname to 127.0.0.1 and talks to the locally bound MCP server through them, reaching a tool surface that treated the loopback bind as the access control.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-go-before-0.56.0-missing-host-header-validation-enables-dns-rebinding" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-go before 0.56.0 Missing Host Header Validation Enables DNS Rebinding (CVE-2026-81092)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/timescale-pg-aiguide-through-0.5.0-dns-rebinding-via-disabled-host-header-allow-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Timescale pg-aiguide through 0.5.0 DNS Rebinding via Disabled Host Header Allow-List (CVE-2026-81095)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/timescale-tiger-slack-dns-rebinding-via-disabled-host-header-allow-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Timescale tiger-slack DNS Rebinding via Disabled Host Header Allow-List (CVE-2026-81099)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/timescale-tiger-gh-mcp-server-dns-rebinding-via-disabled-host-header-allow-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Timescale tiger-gh-mcp-server DNS Rebinding via Disabled Host Header Allow-List (CVE-2026-81100)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/dropbox-dash-mcp-server-dns-rebinding-via-missing-host-header-validation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Dropbox Dash MCP Server DNS Rebinding via Missing Host Header Validation (CVE-2026-81102)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 27</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-79" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-81091, CVE-2026-81093</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Apify&#39;s SSRF Fix Shipped in March and the CVE Turned Up in August <a href="#incident-79" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two SSRF advisories from the August 27 batch credit Yotam Perkal of Pluto Security. The <code>get-html-skeleton</code> tool in Apify&#39;s Actors MCP server validated its <code>url</code> argument with <code>isValidHttpUrl</code>, which confirmed the string began with an http or https scheme and parsed as a URL, then fetched it without inspecting the hostname or the address it resolved to (CVE-2026-81093, CVSS 8.7). Private ranges and cloud metadata endpoints were in reach. The fix is in <code>@apify/actors-mcp-server</code> 0.9.12, published to npm on March 20, 2026, five months before the advisory named it. The mcp-use inspector&#39;s proxy middleware read its destination from an <code>X-Target-URL</code> header or an <code>__mcp_target</code> parameter and proxied there without inspecting the host (CVE-2026-81091, CVSS 8.7); that one was fixed in 2.3.3, published one day before the advisory.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/waiting-skeleton.jpg" alt="skeleton on a bench: the apify ssrf fix, waiting since march for its cve" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>skeleton on a bench: the apify ssrf fix, waiting since march for its cve</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">One fix shipped five months before its CVE and the other one day before. Same batch, same researcher, same 8.7. The gap says nothing about the bugs and everything about when somebody got around to writing them down.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A tool argument or a proxy header reaches loopback, private ranges, and cloud metadata endpoints, and the response comes back into the model&#39;s context.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/apify-actors-mcp-server-before-0.9.12-server-side-request-forgery-via-get-html-skeleton" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Apify Actors MCP Server before 0.9.12 SSRF via get-html-skeleton (CVE-2026-81093)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-use-inspector-proxy-server-side-request-forgery-via-caller-supplied-target-url" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-use Inspector Proxy SSRF via Caller-Supplied Target URL (CVE-2026-81091)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-83" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf local-network data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-80347</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-fetch Asks Node Whether `[::1]` Is an IP Address and Believes the Answer <a href="#incident-83" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-80347 on August 26, 2026, crediting George Chen, against <code>mcp-fetch</code> through 1.6.3. <code>isSafeUrl</code> reads <code>hostname</code> off the parsed URL, which for a literal such as <code>http://[::1]/</code> hands back the string with its brackets still attached, and passes that to <code>net.isIP</code>. <code>net.isIP</code> returns 0 for a bracketed literal, so the guard concludes the target is not an IP address at all and skips the private-range checks it exists to run. The HTTP client then strips the brackets and connects to loopback. CVSS 8.7 at VulnCheck, 7.5 at NVD.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/futurama-fry.jpg" alt="fry squinting: not sure if ip address or just a string with brackets" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>fry squinting: not sure if ip address or just a string with brackets</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two parsers, one string, two answers about what it is. <code>net.isIP</code> is doing exactly what it documents. The guard asked it a question about a URL hostname and read the reply as a question about an address.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A fetch target supplied through a tool argument reaches loopback and private addresses, and the response is returned into the model&#39;s context.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/mcp-fetch-through-1.6.3-server-side-request-forgery-via-unstripped-ipv6-literal-brackets" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: mcp-fetch through 1.6.3 SSRF via Unstripped IPv6 Literal Brackets (CVE-2026-80347)</a><a href="https://github.com/kazuph/mcp-fetch/issues/16" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">kazuph/mcp-fetch issue #16</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 25</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-84" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization credential-theft advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-79786</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Coroot&#39;s MCP OAuth Endpoint Registers Any Redirect URI a Stranger Sends It <a href="#incident-84" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">VulnCheck published CVE-2026-79786 on August 25, 2026, crediting George Chen, against Coroot 1.20.2 through 1.24.5. The MCP OAuth dynamic client registration endpoint is unauthenticated and accepts any syntactically valid redirect URI without validating where it points, so an attacker registers a client aimed at a host they control. Send the resulting authorization URL to a signed-in user, and their authorization code lands on the attacker&#39;s host. CWE-601, CVSS 7.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Dynamic client registration exists so an MCP client nobody pre-registered can still connect, which means the endpoint is designed to be reachable by strangers. Coroot implemented that part faithfully. Deciding which redirect targets a stranger may name is the half that has to survive contact with one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who gets a signed-in Coroot user to open one link captures their authorization code and takes over the session.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/coroot-1.20.2-through-1.24.5-unvalidated-redirect-uri-in-mcp-oauth-client-registration" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Coroot 1.20.2 through 1.24.5 Unvalidated Redirect URI in MCP OAuth Client Registration (CVE-2026-79786)</a><a href="https://github.com/coroot/coroot/issues/929" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">coroot/coroot issue #929</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 24</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-112" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce sandbox-escape authentication command-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-53710</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ContextForge&#39;s Python Sandbox Server Left getattr Sitting in safe_builtins <a href="#incident-112" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">brian-hussey published GHSA-xm98-3vcf-fph7 on August 24, 2026 for CVE-2026-53710, affecting <code>mcp-contextforge-gateway</code> 1.0.1 and earlier. The <code>python_sandbox_server</code> exposes an <code>execute_code</code> MCP tool over HTTP and SSE with no authentication, and three weaknesses compound into remote code execution. <code>getattr</code> sits in RestrictedPython&#39;s <code>safe_builtins</code> without the <code>_getattr_</code> mediation layer that is supposed to police attribute access. <code>validate_code</code> rejects dunder names written as literals and has no opinion about the same names assembled at runtime. The proof of concept walks the class hierarchy with <code>getattr</code>, finds <code>subprocess.Popen</code>, and runs OS commands as the server process. The advisory scores it 9.0 over HTTP and 8.8 over stdio, and the fix is in 1.0.2.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/megamind-peeking.png" alt="megamind peering at safe_builtins: no _getattr_ on this one?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>megamind peering at safe_builtins: no _getattr_ on this one?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A sandbox shipping <code>getattr</code> unmediated is a sandbox the way a fence with a gate in it is a fence. The blocklist is the detail worth keeping: it refused the literal string <code>__class__</code> and was untroubled by the same name built one character at a time.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated caller who can reach the sandbox server executes arbitrary commands with the privileges of the process that was supposed to be containing them.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/IBM/mcp-context-forge/security/advisories/GHSA-xm98-3vcf-fph7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xm98-3vcf-fph7: RestrictedPython sandbox bypass in mcp-context-forge (CVE-2026-53710)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-53710" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-53710</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 24</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-137" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication advisory local-network denial-of-service"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-107824, CVE-2026-107820</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> x64dbg&#39;s MCP Plugin Serves the Whole Debugger on 0.0.0.0 With No Authentication <a href="#incident-137" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">x64dbg-MCP Server is a native MCP plugin that exposes the debugger&#39;s functionality over HTTP and SSE. duty1g published GHSA-4478-h5jv-647m on August 24, 2026: in versions before 1.0 every MCP debugger tool was served without authentication while the plugin listened on 0.0.0.0 by default, so any unauthenticated network client that could reach port 9094 for x64 or 9095 for x32 could execute arbitrary x64dbg commands and attach to processes. The advisory records it fixed in 1.0, and NVD carries CVSS 9.3 for CVE-2026-107824, attributed to GitHub as the assigning CNA. A second advisory, GHSA-jgj3-97w2-9v9r, published August 27 as CVE-2026-107820, covers <code>parseContentLength()</code> in <code>src/core/mcp_server.zig</code> parsing an unbounded Content-Length and using it in unchecked <code>usize</code> addition in <code>wsRecv()</code>, which runs before token authentication. That one affects versions up to and including 1.1 and is fixed in 1.2, and the advisory is explicit that the overflowed value is only ever a comparison operand, so the impact is denial of service rather than memory corruption. NVD ingested both on October 9, six and seven weeks after the advisories, and its text for the first one reads &quot;prior to 1.1&quot; where the advisory itself says 1.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/two-guys-on-a-bus.jpg" alt="two guys on a bus: the same plugin bound to 127.0.0.1 and bound to 0.0.0.0" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>two guys on a bus: the same plugin bound to 127.0.0.1 and bound to 0.0.0.0</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A debugger is a tool for doing anything to any process on the machine. Wrapping one in MCP and binding it to every interface is less a missing auth check than a remote administration service nobody asked for. The overflow is the smaller finding by a wide margin, availability only by the maintainer&#39;s own analysis, and it still fires before the token check, which by that point is decorative.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can reach the port drives a debugger that is already attached to processes on the host, which is arbitrary code execution with a user interface.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/duty1g/x64dbg-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-4478-h5jv-647m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">x64dbg-MCP Server GHSA-4478-h5jv-647m: unauthenticated MCP transport gives any network client remote control of x64dbg (CVE-2026-107824)</a><a href="https://github.com/duty1g/x64dbg-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-jgj3-97w2-9v9r" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">x64dbg-MCP Server GHSA-jgj3-97w2-9v9r: unauthenticated pre-auth integer-overflow denial of service via Content-Length (CVE-2026-107820)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-94" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem data-leak authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-85580</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> SiYuan&#39;s Fifth MCP Advisory Is a Path Guard That Matches Filenames Case-Sensitively <a href="#incident-94" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">SiYuan published GHSA-mmgw-3mx9-cfwp on August 21, 2026, later assigned CVE-2026-85580. The advisory&#39;s summary states that on Linux the MCP file-access path guard compares a case-variant path against the protected <code>publishAccess.json</code> filename using case-sensitive matching, and its details add that a path such as <code>data/.siyuan/PublishAccess.json</code> bypasses a case-sensitive basename or suffix comparison &quot;even though the underlying file is the protected metadata file&quot;. It does not say how the variant reaches that file. SiYuan shipped a regression test named <code>TestSecurityReproMCPReadsPublishAccessMetadataOnLinux</code> and fixed it in v3.8.2 by canonicalising the path and matching with platform-appropriate case semantics. CVSS 6.5. The protected file is the same one CVE-2026-60083 added to the blocklist three weeks earlier.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The August 8 advisory established that this file needed protecting and put it on a list. This one is about the comparison behind the list, and a blocklist entry is only ever as good as the match behind it. Worth noting that the advisory asserts the bypass and ships a Linux regression test for it without spelling out how a case variant lands on a case-sensitive filesystem; take the fix, not the mechanism, until someone explains that part.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An MCP caller with read access discloses SiYuan&#39;s publish-access configuration and metadata, which the advisory says should be treated as sensitive.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-mmgw-3mx9-cfwp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-mmgw-3mx9-cfwp: SiYuan MCP path guard bypass via case mismatch (CVE-2026-85580)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/siyuan-before-3.8.2-path-guard-bypass-via-case-mismatch" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: SiYuan before 3.8.2 path guard bypass via case mismatch</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-69" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection rce sandbox-escape filesystem"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-18482</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Neo.mjs Checks That the Path Is Inside the Project, Then Hands the Whole String to a Shell <a href="#incident-69" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">novice-22 disclosed CVE-2026-18482 on August 20, 2026, a command injection in the file-system MCP server that ships inside Neo.mjs at <code>ai/mcp/server/file-system</code>. The <code>checkSyntax()</code> and <code>runPlaywrightTest()</code> tools build command strings by interpolating a caller-supplied <code>absolutePath</code> into <code>node --check ...</code> and <code>npx playwright test ...</code>, then run them through <code>child_process.exec()</code>, which spawns a shell. The sandbox check, <code>ensureSandboxed()</code>, resolves the path and confirms it sits under the project root. That answers where the path points and says nothing about what follows it, so <code>/home/user/neo/README.md; touch /tmp/MARKER</code> passes validation and the shell runs both halves. Versions through 13.1.0 are affected. The fix swaps <code>exec()</code> for <code>execFile()</code>, passing arguments as an array so there is no shell to parse them.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/roll-safe.jpg" alt="the sandbox check passed, so the rest of the string must be fine too" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the sandbox check passed, so the rest of the string must be fine too</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The validator was asked whether the path is inside the project. It said yes, correctly, about the part of the string before the semicolon. <code>execFile</code> was available the whole time and makes the bug unrepresentable rather than filtered.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An agent steered into calling check_syntax or run_playwright_test on an attacker-chosen path executes arbitrary OS commands with the developer’s privileges.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://novice-22.com/posts/neo-mjs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">novice-22: Command Injection in Neo.mjs MCP File-System Server (CVE-2026-18482)</a><a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-cmg9-62q9-xmww" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Advisory Database: GHSA-cmg9-62q9-xmww</a><a href="https://github.com/neomjs/neo/commit/5acc564ea1b278bca5fab1f8f397a6ba9b849d75" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">neomjs/neo fix commit 5acc564e</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-70" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service authentication protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-59279</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Spring AI’s MCP Streamable HTTP Transport Keeps Every Session It Has Ever Handed Out <a href="#incident-70" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Spring published CVE-2026-59279 on August 20, 2026 against Spring AI 2.0.0. The MCP Streamable HTTP server transport, in both the WebFlux and WebMvc variants, places no limit on the number of sessions it retains, and by default does not require clients to authenticate. A remote attacker sends initialization requests in a loop, the server keeps every session it creates, memory climbs, and the process eventually stops serving anyone. CVSS 7.5. Fixed in 2.0.1 for open source and 2.0.0.1 for enterprise support.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Filed Medium against a CVSS of 7.5, because availability is the entire prize and a crash buys the attacker nothing else. The detail worth pausing on is that &quot;does not require clients to be authenticated&quot; appears in the advisory as a contributing condition rather than as its own finding.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated remote attacker exhausts the memory of a Spring AI MCP server and takes it down for every legitimate client.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://spring.io/security/cve-2026-59279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spring Security Advisory: CVE-2026-59279</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 19</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-149" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-108395</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The MCP Java SDK Patches the Same Unbounded Read on Both Ends of the Wire <a href="#incident-149" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Kehrlann published two advisories against the official Java SDK on August 19, 2026, both fixed in 0.18.4, 1.1.4 and 2.0.1. CVE-2026-108395 (GHSA-4x8c-5vv7-973f, CVSS 7.5) is the server side: the Streamable HTTP, Stateless Streamable HTTP and SSE transports in <code>mcp-core</code> all read a whole request body into memory before parsing, none of them checks <code>Content-Length</code>, and chunked transfer encoding walks past any declared-length check anyway. Credits go to razashariff, tonghuaroot, hewei-gikaku, mohammad228, Gal3m and shmulc8. GHSA-5q7x-232h-f834 (CVSS 6.5, no CVE) is the client side, and it is the same shape pointed the other way: <code>ResponseSubscribers.SseLineSubscriber</code>, shared by both HTTP client transports, appends every <code>data:</code> line to a buffer and dispatches only when a blank line arrives, so a hostile server sends <code>data:</code> lines and never the blank one.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The 7.5 is overruled to Medium on availability, and the 6.5 is already Medium, so nothing was argued down. The interesting half is the one with no CVE: the client transport trusts a server to eventually send a blank line, which is the same trust the server advisory says not to extend to clients. One parser, one afternoon, both directions.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated client kills a Java MCP server with one chunked body. A hostile MCP server kills its clients by never finishing an event, at almost no cost to itself.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/java-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-4x8c-5vv7-973f" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Java SDK GHSA-4x8c-5vv7-973f: Unbounded request-body reads in mcp-core HTTP server transports allow remote denial of service (CVE-2026-108395)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/java-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5q7x-232h-f834" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP Java SDK GHSA-5q7x-232h-f834: Unbounded reads when parsing Server-Sent Events in HTTP client transports</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 19</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-71" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-76404</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Splunk’s MCP Server App Deserializes Whatever Is Sitting in the Credential Store <a href="#incident-71" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Splunk published SVD-2026-0808 on August 19, 2026, covering CVE-2026-76404 in the Splunk MCP Server app below 1.2.1. The app’s credential management component deserializes stored data without checking whether the content is of the expected type, which Splunk classifies as CWE-502. A user holding the <code>admin</code> Splunk role can place a crafted object in that store and obtain arbitrary command execution on the underlying operating system. CVSS 9.1. Fixed in 1.2.1. The same bulletin carries a second deserialization bug, CVE-2026-76395 in the Splunk AI Toolkit, where a model codec unpickles sparse matrix data.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Splunk graded this 9.1 even though it needs the admin role, and that is the right call. A Splunk role is an application role. It was never supposed to be a shell account, and the credential store was never supposed to be an executable format.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An admin-role Splunk user escapes the application entirely and runs commands on the host operating system.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://advisory.splunk.com/advisories/SVD-2026-0808" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Splunk Security Advisory SVD-2026-0808</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-93" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authorization ssrf cross-tenant privilege-escalation"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-79744, CVE-2026-79745, CVE-2026-79746, CVE-2026-79747, CVE-2026-79748, CVE-2026-79749, CVE-2026-79750</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCPHub Publishes Seven Advisories in a Week, Including a Non-Admin Route to /bin/sh <a href="#incident-93" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">MCPHub, which exists to centrally manage other people&#39;s MCP servers, published seven advisories between August 17 and August 23, 2026. CVE-2026-79748 is the one that matters: <code>POST /api/servers</code> and <code>PUT /api/servers/:name</code> have no admin gate, no command allowlist and no path validation, so any authenticated non-admin submits a server config with <code>command: &quot;/bin/sh&quot;</code> and arbitrary args, and MCPHub spawns it. CVSS 9.9, reported by @offset, and the advisory notes the Docker, npx and systemd deployments all run as root. CVE-2026-79750, from @BerSecHub, is the cross-tenant version: list views and config edits enforce ownership and the tool-execution API does not, so a non-admin calls tools on somebody else&#39;s connected server. The rest are the same missing check in other places: global security config rewritable by non-admins (CVE-2026-79744), a server-scoped bearer key that matches a whole group on any overlap (CVE-2026-79746), prompt and resource CRUD with no authorization (CVE-2026-79745), an open SSRF (CVE-2026-79747), and an SSRF guard that IPv6 transition addresses walk through (CVE-2026-79749). NVD ingested all seven on August 31.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A hub is a concentration of other systems&#39; credentials by design, so the interesting number here is not 9.9, it is seven. The pattern across all of them is an ownership check that exists on the read paths and not the write ones, which is what happens when multi-tenancy is added to a single-tenant tool one endpoint at a time.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated non-admin gets remote code execution as the MCPHub process owner, which in the documented deployments is root, and short of that reads and executes across every other tenant&#39;s connected servers.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-4gc8-885f-qj36" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-4gc8-885f-qj36: missing authorization on PUT /api/system-config (CVE-2026-79744)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-6cvf-cfch-4g7m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-6cvf-cfch-4g7m: missing authorization on built-in prompt and resource CRUD (CVE-2026-79745)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-454m-4vm6-842f" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-454m-4vm6-842f: server-scoped bearer key gains access to an entire group (CVE-2026-79746)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-9wx9-prgc-gmjr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9wx9-prgc-gmjr: non-admin SSRF via mcphub (CVE-2026-79747)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-mx89-jjx9-gjr8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-mx89-jjx9-gjr8: missing authorization on server config endpoints leads to RCE (CVE-2026-79748)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-pr4x-3pc7-2fhw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-pr4x-3pc7-2fhw: SSRF guard bypass via IPv6 transition addresses (CVE-2026-79749)</a><a href="https://github.com/samanhappy/mcphub/security/advisories/GHSA-wfqf-hqwq-8wvf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-wfqf-hqwq-8wvf: cross-user tool execution via unowned servers (CVE-2026-79750)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-72" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-75060</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> PyCharm Shipped Unauthenticated Jupyter MCP Code Execution, and the Public Description Is One Sentence <a href="#incident-72" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-75060 was published on August 17, 2026 against JetBrains PyCharm before 2026.2.1. The entire public description reads: &quot;In JetBrains PyCharm before 2026.2.1 code execution was possible via unauthenticated Jupyter MCP tools.&quot; NVD scores it 8.4. JetBrains lists the issue on its shared fixed-issues page rather than in a dedicated advisory, so there is no write-up of the attack path, no statement of what &quot;unauthenticated&quot; reaches, and no proof of concept. The Jupyter MCP tools ship with the IDE, which puts the exposed surface on developer workstations.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/best-i-can-do.jpg" alt="jetbrains, on documenting an 8.4 in its own ide" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>jetbrains, on documenting an 8.4 in its own ide</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">An 8.4 in a widely deployed IDE gets one line on a page that lists everything JetBrains fixed. Nobody downstream can tell whether &quot;unauthenticated&quot; means a local process, another host on the network, or a web page you happened to open, and those are three very different Tuesdays.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Code execution on a machine running an affected PyCharm, reached through the bundled Jupyter MCP tools without authenticating to them.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.jetbrains.com/privacy-security/issues-fixed/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JetBrains: Fixed security issues</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-75060" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-75060</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 14</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-73" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="denial-of-service protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-53965</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Official MCP PHP SDK Lets a Hostile Server Eat the Client’s Memory <a href="#incident-73" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">tonghuaroot reported CVE-2026-53965 in <code>modelcontextprotocol/php-sdk</code>, the official PHP SDK, published as GHSA-7m52-jw36-44r3 on August 14, 2026. The HTTP client transport reads Server-Sent Events incrementally and appends every chunk to <code>$this-&gt;sseBuffer</code>, which is only drained when the <code>&quot;\n\n&quot;</code> event delimiter arrives. A server that never sends the delimiter grows that buffer without bound. The advisory ships a proof of concept: 1,000 well-formed events hold the client at 2.0 MB, while 400 MB of delimiter-free data kills a client running the default 256 MB <code>memory_limit</code>. Affects 0.5.0 through 0.7.0, fixed in 0.7.1.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Almost every MCP bug on this site runs client to server. This one runs backwards, and there is no approval dialog for &quot;the server you already trusted is now sending you 400 MB of nothing.&quot; Medium against a CVSS of 8.7, because a dead PHP process is the whole payout.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any MCP server a PHP client connects to can crash that client on demand, with no authentication and no earlier signal the client could act on.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/php-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-7m52-jw36-44r3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">modelcontextprotocol/php-sdk advisory GHSA-7m52-jw36-44r3 (CVE-2026-53965)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 13</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-82" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem credential-theft data-exfiltration advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-82233</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> SiYuan&#39;s Fourth MCP Advisory in a Month Is Another Tool That Skips the Workspace Check <a href="#incident-82" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">SiYuan published GHSA-p23f-cm6q-2qp8 on August 13, 2026, crediting joysinleung, against SiYuan before v3.8.1. The <code>asset.upload</code> MCP tool takes a comma-separated list of absolute file paths and normalises them without ever checking that they land inside the workspace. An agent talked into calling it copies files from outside the workspace into the notebook&#39;s asset store, and the approval dialog the user sees does not show the source path. VulnCheck assigned CVE-2026-82233 two weeks later, on August 28, at CVSS 6.9. This is the fourth SiYuan MCP advisory in under two weeks, after the three from August 3 and August 8.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Four advisories, four tools, one boundary that keeps not being checked. SiYuan&#39;s MCP layer is discovering the workspace root one tool at a time, and <code>asset.upload</code> is a tool whose entire purpose is writing files into that root, so it is the one place the check was least optional.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any file the SiYuan kernel process can read, including SSH keys and credential files, gets copied into the workspace and served as a notebook asset.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-p23f-cm6q-2qp8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-p23f-cm6q-2qp8: SiYuan asset.upload path traversal</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/siyuan-before-3.8.1-path-traversal-via-asset-upload" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: SiYuan before 3.8.1 Path Traversal via asset.upload (CVE-2026-82233)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-102" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain ai-supply-chain prompt-injection credential-theft data-exfiltration llm-manipulation github"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Deadbugz Pushes a Malicious MCP Server Through 23 Pull Requests, Then Waits Three Tool Calls <a href="#incident-102" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Pillar Security published the Deadbugz campaign on August 12, 2026 and reported it as still active. A single GitHub account, <code>zellkernel</code>, filed 23 pull requests against unrelated AI, MCP and developer-tool repositories inside a 74-minute window on August 10, adding MCP server configurations: 17 remote, four local-script and two directory listings. The server they point at offers text formatting and summarisation, and keeps a per-client in-memory counter of <code>tools/call</code> requests. Once that counter reaches three, the server&#39;s <code>tools/list</code> and <code>prompts/get</code> responses change, and the returned tool metadata becomes instructions to collect SSH keys, AWS credentials, shell history and Kubernetes config while concealing the activity from the user. Pillar published the indicators, including the <code>productivity-suite-mcp.onrender.com/mcp</code> endpoint and the <code>deadbug-mcp.py</code> local artifact. No CVE was assigned.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/scooby-doo-mask-reveal.jpg" alt="fred pulling the sheet off the ghost: the productivity server, on its fourth tool call" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>fred pulling the sheet off the ghost: the productivity server, on its fourth tool call</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Tool definitions are fetched at runtime from the thing they describe. Reading them once tells you what the server was willing to say while being read. Three calls is a cheap counter, and it is enough to make a one-time approval mean nothing.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An operator who approved the server after reading its tool definitions gets a different tool set on the fourth call, aimed at whatever credentials the agent&#39;s host can reach.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.pillar.security/blog/deadbugz-currently-active-mcp-supply-chain-campaign" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pillar Security: Deadbugz, a currently active MCP supply-chain campaign</a><a href="https://adversa.ai/blog/top-mcp-security-resources-september-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adversa AI: top MCP security resources, September 2026</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 11</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-80" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication rce privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-82456</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> argocd-mcp Lends the Operator&#39;s Argo CD Token to Whoever Connects First <a href="#incident-80" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">leoluz published GHSA-rp45-5x3v-48mr on August 11, 2026, crediting shmulc8, against argocd-mcp 0.8.0 and earlier. Three gaps compose. The HTTP transport starts with <code>app.listen(port)</code>, so it binds every interface. It accepts MCP sessions without any caller credential whenever <code>ARGOCD_API_TOKEN</code> is configured, because the token is for talking outbound to Argo CD and nothing checks inbound. And it never applies the MCP SDK&#39;s Host validation. CVSS 10.0, fixed in 0.9.0. NVD ingested it as CVE-2026-82456 eighteen days later.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/oprah-you-get-a.jpg" alt="oprah gesturing: the operator's argo cd token, for every caller who connects" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>oprah gesturing: the operator&#39;s argo cd token, for every caller who connects</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The token is there so the server can reach Argo CD. Nothing was there to establish who was reaching the server. Argo CD&#39;s entire job is turning a manifest reference into running workloads, so an unauthenticated caller holding that token isn&#39;t reading a cluster. They&#39;re deploying to one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can reach the port drives the full Argo CD tool surface with the operator&#39;s stored token: create applications, point them at attacker-controlled manifests, and sync them into the destination Kubernetes cluster.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/argoproj-labs/mcp-for-argocd/security/advisories/GHSA-rp45-5x3v-48mr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-rp45-5x3v-48mr: argocd-mcp unauthenticated HTTP transport (argoproj-labs/mcp-for-argocd)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/argocd-mcp-0.8.0-authentication-bypass-via-unauthenticated-http" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: argocd-mcp 0.8.0 Authentication Bypass via Unauthenticated HTTP (CVE-2026-82456)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 11</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-58" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-19516</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Grafana&#39;s MCP Server Lets the Caller Choose the Destination, Which Is What the Last Fix Was For <a href="#incident-58" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Grafana published CVE-2026-19516 on August 11, 2026. The <code>grafana_api_request</code> tool in <code>mcp-grafana</code> lets the caller choose the HTTP method, path, and body. A caller-supplied <code>X-Grafana-URL</code> request header then controls the destination, which is not restricted to the configured Grafana instance. Requests can be aimed at internal, loopback, and link-local services, including metadata endpoints, and the responses come back to the caller. CVSS 9.1. This is an incomplete fix for CVE-2026-15583, which stopped the tool from sending Grafana tokens to arbitrary hosts but left the destination itself unconstrained. Fixed in 1.1.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/zero-days-without.png" alt="an ssrf fix that constrained the token and not the destination" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>an ssrf fix that constrained the token and not the destination</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The first fix stopped the credential from travelling. It did not stop the request. <code>X-Grafana-URL</code> is still, by name and by function, the field where the caller says where to go.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated MCP caller reaches anything the Grafana host can reach and reads the response, with full control of method, path, and body.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://grafana.com/security/security-advisories/cve-2026-19516" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grafana Security Advisory: CVE-2026-19516, SSRF in mcp-grafana</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 11</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-59" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="data-leak advisory web-surface"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-73037</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Next AI Draw.io Interpolates Its mcp Query Parameter Into Both a Script Block and the HTML <a href="#incident-59" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Hồ Việt Khánh reported CVE-2026-73037 on August 11, 2026 against <code>@next-ai-drawio/mcp-server</code> 0.2.1 and earlier, bundled in <code>next-ai-draw-io</code> 0.2.1 through 0.4.16. The MCP HTTP server takes the <code>mcp</code> query parameter and interpolates it without escaping into two contexts: a JavaScript string literal and the surrounding HTML. A payload of <code>&quot;;alert(1);//</code> breaks out of the script context, and an <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=...&gt;</code> renders straight into the page. Both execute in the localhost origin. CVSS 6.1. The report was still unpatched when it was filed.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Reflected XSS reads as a 2010 finding until you notice where it lands. The localhost origin is where the agent&#39;s tooling keeps its session.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A crafted link opened in the victim&#39;s browser runs JavaScript on the localhost origin that serves the MCP endpoint, reaching diagram sessions and API data held there.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/DayuanJiang/next-ai-draw-io/issues/917" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">next-ai-draw-io issue #917: reflected XSS in the mcp query parameter</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/next-ai-draw-io-reflected-xss-via-unsanitized-mcp-query-parameter" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Next AI Draw.io reflected XSS via unsanitized mcp query parameter</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-56" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization data-leak local-network"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-73296</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Microsoft&#39;s UFO Agent Framework Ships Two Unauthenticated MCP Servers That Drive an Android Phone <a href="#incident-56" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-24fq-m9rr-g3mm, published August 10, 2026, covers <code>create_mobile_data_collection_server</code> and <code>create_mobile_action_server</code> in <code>ufo/client/mcp/http_servers/mobile_mcp_server.py</code>. Both expose Streamable HTTP MCP services, on TCP 8020 and 8021, with no authentication. Port 8020 serves <code>capture_screenshot</code>, <code>get_ui_tree</code>, and <code>get_device_info</code>. Port 8021 serves <code>tap</code>, <code>swipe</code>, <code>type_text</code>, <code>launch_app</code>, <code>press_key</code>, and <code>click_control</code>. When UFO is configured for remote deployment and binds <code>0.0.0.0</code>, any client that can reach the ports initializes an MCP session and calls the tools without presenting an API key. The tools reach the connected Android device through ADB subprocesses. CVSS 9.4, affecting v3.0.7 and earlier, fixed in v3.0.8.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/always-has-been.png" alt="wait, both mcp ports are unauthenticated? always have been" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>wait, both mcp ports are unauthenticated? always have been</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The framework&#39;s threat model stops at the model. Two HTTP servers underneath it hand out screenshots and touch events to whoever asks, and that path never involves a prompt at all.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can reach the ports reads the phone&#39;s screen and UI tree, then taps, types, and launches apps on it. No credentials, no user approval, and no cooperation from the model.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/UFO/security/advisories/GHSA-24fq-m9rr-g3mm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-24fq-m9rr-g3mm: microsoft/UFO mobile MCP servers exposed without authentication</a><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/UFO/releases/tag/v3.0.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">microsoft/UFO release v3.0.8</a><a href="https://github.com/microsoft/UFO/commit/e562d10060b077dedae93e0fd58c1ee379558962" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fix commit e562d10</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-57" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection llm-manipulation data-exfiltration credential-theft protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> GhostSplice Splits One Exfiltration Request Across Three MCP Channels and Compliance Doubles <a href="#incident-57" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">ASSET Research Group published GhostSplice on August 10, 2026. Researchers Murali Ediga and Sudipta Chattopadhyay call the technique cross-channel trust fragmentation. A malicious MCP server splits a credential-theft request into fragments that each read as routine and delivers them through channels the agent already trusts. The tool description advertises an <code>integrity_checker</code> with bland field names. A first tool result returns an ordinary project file scan. A second result supplies the mapping, telling the model which files fill which fields for a server-side hash check. No single channel carries the whole request. Across eleven API-tested models, splitting a request into two halves raised average compliance from 42 percent to 82 percent, and GPT-4o, Gemini, and Llama went from refusing to 100 percent. VS Code adds a third channel through server-initiated sampling. The tests ran in isolated projects seeded with fake credentials, and the group says CVE identifiers will follow coordinated disclosure.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/three-headed-dragon.jpg" alt="tool description, tool result, and sampling, none of them the bad one" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>tool description, tool result, and sampling, none of them the bad one</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The refusal training works on the whole request. It was never shown the whole request. Filling in a form is the one task no model has been trained to decline.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>SSH keys, environment variables, proprietary source, and customer data leave through a tool call the model reads as a form. Results depend on the client: GPT-5.4 runs the attack 90 percent of the time under Cursor and 0 percent behind Claude Code.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://asset-group.github.io/disclosures/ghostsplice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASSET Research Group: The AI refused to steal the secrets. So we handed it a form.</a><a href="https://github.com/asset-group/ghostsplice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">asset-group/ghostsplice: PoC for the cross-channel trust fragmentation attack</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/08/malicious-mcp-servers-can-split.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Malicious MCP Servers Can Split Instructions to Make AI Coding Agents Exfiltrate Secrets</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 06</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-55" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="command-injection ssrf filesystem authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-19039, CVE-2026-19040, CVE-2026-19041, CVE-2026-19244, CVE-2026-19263, CVE-2026-19268, CVE-2026-19270, CVE-2026-19279, CVE-2026-19329, CVE-2026-19332, CVE-2026-19337, CVE-2026-19338</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> VulDB Files Twelve MCP Server CVEs in Four Days and Most Maintainers Never Answered <a href="#incident-55" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Between August 6 and August 9, 2026, VulDB published twelve CVEs against small MCP servers, nearly all of them single-maintainer GitHub projects. The shape repeats: a tool parameter reaches a shell. <code>codex_mcp</code> passes the <code>model</code> argument (CVE-2026-19329), MCP4EDA passes <code>design_name</code> and <code>vcd_file</code> (CVE-2026-19332), <code>mcp-pdf-vision</code> passes <code>pdfPath</code> and <code>sessionId</code> (CVE-2026-19279), MCPGateway passes a <code>since</code> date (CVE-2026-19268), and <code>mcp-bridge-api</code> takes <code>command</code> and <code>args</code> from its servers endpoint (CVE-2026-19263). Path traversal covers <code>mcp-ui-probe</code> (CVE-2026-19270) and MCPyATS (CVE-2026-19338). SSRF covers <code>mcp-google-search</code> (CVE-2026-19337) and MissionSquad&#39;s <code>mcp-api</code> (CVE-2026-19040), which also shipped a command injection in its npm package installer (CVE-2026-19041). HKUDS <code>nanobot</code> registered MCP resource and prompt wrappers outside the declared <code>enabledTools</code> scope (CVE-2026-19244). The <code>ssh-mcp-server</code> entry (CVE-2026-19039) is disputed by its maintainer, whose stated threat model is that the server is a local trusted tool for running SSH commands, so callers already hold execution.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/monkey-puppet.jpg" alt="twelve mcp maintainers opening the issues tab this week" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>twelve mcp maintainers opening the issues tab this week</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The <code>ssh-mcp-server</code> maintainer asks the question the rest of the batch avoids. If a tool exists to run commands over SSH, the line between command injection and the product is not obvious. Twelve filings in four days suggests nobody has agreed where that line sits.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Each finding is small on its own. The batch is a census of the long tail: several projects never responded to the report, several ship rolling releases with no version anyone can pin, and some fixes exist only as unversioned commits.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19039" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19039: ssh-mcp-server command injection (disputed by maintainer)</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19040" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19040: MissionSquad mcp-api SSRF</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19041" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19041: MissionSquad mcp-api command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19244" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19244: HKUDS nanobot enabledTools scope bypass</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19263" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19263: mcp-bridge-api command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19268" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19268: MCPGateway command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19270" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19270: mcp-ui-probe path traversal</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19279" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19279: mcp-pdf-vision command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19329" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19329: codex_mcp command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19332" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19332: MCP4EDA command injection</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19337" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19337: mcp-google-search SSRF</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19338" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19338: MCPyATS path traversal</a><a href="https://github.com/KinoThe-Kafkaesque/ssh-mcp-server/issues/3#issuecomment-4319612623" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ssh-mcp-server issue #3: maintainer response on the intended threat model</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-54" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="aws credential-theft prompt-injection authorization data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-18655, CVE-2026-18954</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Labs Patches Two MCP Servers in Three Days, One Sending Broker Credentials Wherever the Model Points <a href="#incident-54" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two AWS security bulletins in the same week. Bulletin 2026-070, published August 3, 2026, covers CVE-2026-18655 in <code>awslabs.amazon-mq-mcp-server</code> before 2.0.24: the RabbitMQ broker connection tools do not restrict which endpoint they will connect to, so a broker hostname introduced into the MCP client context by prompt injection redirects Amazon MQ broker credentials or OAuth access tokens to a host the attacker controls. CVSS 6.5. Bulletin 2026-076, published August 5, covers CVE-2026-18954 in the DocumentDB MCP Server before 1.0.12: the write-capable aggregation pipeline stages <code>$out</code> and <code>$merge</code> slip past the read-only mode enforcement, so an authenticated MCP client writes to a database the server was configured to treat as read-only. CVSS 5.5.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">AWS&#39;s interim advice for the first one is to turn off auto-approve on the RabbitMQ initialization tools, which concedes that the destination hostname was the model&#39;s to pick. The second is a reminder that read-only enforced in the server is a preference, and read-only enforced by database credentials is a control.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Broker credentials and OAuth tokens go to whatever hostname reaches the model&#39;s context. DocumentDB deployments running in read-only mode accept writes through the aggregation pipeline.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-070-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-070: Amazon MQ MCP Server (CVE-2026-18655)</a><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-076-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-076: DocumentDB MCP Server (CVE-2026-18954)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-74" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem data-leak credential-theft authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-74798, CVE-2026-59809, CVE-2026-60083</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> SiYuan Ships Three MCP Advisories in Six Days, Each One a Caller the Previous Fix Skipped <a href="#incident-74" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">SiYuan published GHSA-43jx-gxq4-jpjc on August 3, 2026 and two more on August 8. The first, CVE-2026-74798, is a path traversal in the <code>database_clean</code> MCP tool: <code>RemoveUnusedAttributeView()</code> checks only that <code>id</code> is non-empty before joining it into a filesystem path, so an authenticated MCP client can copy any file the kernel can read into the history directory and delete the original. An earlier fix, GHSA-7hm9-v7vf-7g4w, had hardened the HTTP API caller of that same function and left the MCP one alone. The August 8 pair repeats the shape. CVE-2026-59809 resolves <code>{{secrets.*}}</code> placeholders inside the destination URL of the <code>http_request</code> tool, so a crafted URL mails stored secrets to any public host, and GET requests skip the confirmation prompt. CVE-2026-60083 is an MCP file tool blocklist covering one of the four paths the HTTP file API blocks, which leaves <code>data/.siyuan/publishAccess.json</code> readable. Fixed in v3.7.4 and v3.8.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/surprised-pikachu.jpg" alt="the http api was hardened. the mcp tool calls the same function" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the http api was hardened. the mcp tool calls the same function</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three advisories, three tools, and in each case the HTTP surface already had the guard. Bolting an MCP tool layer onto an existing app builds a second front door, and the lock keeps getting fitted to the first one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated SiYuan MCP client reads and deletes arbitrary files the kernel process can reach, exfiltrates stored API secrets through unprompted GET requests, and reads publish-mode passwords in cleartext.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-43jx-gxq4-jpjc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SiYuan advisory GHSA-43jx-gxq4-jpjc (CVE-2026-74798)</a><a href="https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-853m-gvvm-6rvx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SiYuan advisory GHSA-853m-gvvm-6rvx (CVE-2026-59809)</a><a href="https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-c8r8-95hg-mp34" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SiYuan advisory GHSA-c8r8-95hg-mp34 (CVE-2026-60083)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · august · 01</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-47" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization privilege-escalation advisory web-surface"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-15988</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AI Engine for WordPress Hands Out Admin Accounts to Anyone Who Gets an Admin to Click a Link <a href="#incident-47" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The AI Engine plugin bundles a chatbot, an AI framework, and an MCP server for WordPress, and every version through 3.6.5 is vulnerable. The <code>reauth_for_authorize</code> function in the MCP OAuth path is missing nonce validation, which makes it cross-site request forgery. An unauthenticated attacker who gets a logged-in administrator to click a link can create a brand new administrator account with credentials the attacker chose. WordPress&#39;s <code>?_method=POST</code> override turns that top-level GET navigation into an authenticated POST against the REST users endpoint, so the attacker needs no account of their own. CVSS 8.8.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The missing nonce is in the MCP OAuth code specifically. Bolt an agent protocol onto a WordPress plugin and the plugin&#39;s auth mistakes graduate into agent-protocol mistakes.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One click from a logged-in admin creates a second admin under attacker control. That is full site takeover from a link, with no prior access.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-15988" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-15988 (AI Engine for WordPress, CSRF to administrator creation)</a><a href="https://plugins.trac.wordpress.org/browser/ai-engine/tags/3.6.2/labs/mcp-oauth.php#L120" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WordPress Plugin Trac: ai-engine labs/mcp-oauth.php</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 31</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-103" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design authentication ssrf prompt-injection"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Scan of 640 Internet-Facing MCP Servers Finds 91.8% Without OAuth <a href="#incident-103" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Nicolás Padilla posted &quot;Exposed by Design: A Dynamic Security Assessment of Internet-Facing MCP Servers at Scale&quot; to arXiv on July 31, 2026. Passive discovery across eleven sources (crt.sh, HuggingFace, GitHub, npm, Smithery, PyPI, Censys, FOFA, Shodan, glama.ai and pulsemcp.com) put over 21,000 server instances within reach. Four measurement runs across July 2026 confirmed 640 production servers and dynamically audited 414 of them with Corvus, a framework of 34 test modules covering ten MCP-specific vulnerability classes. The audit produced 68 reportable vulnerabilities including SQL injection, SSRF against cloud metadata services, prompt template injection and path traversal via cursor manipulation. 91.8% of audited servers had no OAuth authentication and 687 tool instances exposed shell execution with no access control. 41.6% of confirmed servers disappeared within three days between consecutive runs. Corvus was released as open source.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The 41.6% churn figure is the one to sit with. A population where two in five servers vanish inside three days is not being decommissioned carefully, it is being spun up, pointed at something, and forgotten. The 91.8% without OAuth is what that looks like when nobody comes back.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Not a single vulnerability but a population measurement. OAuth is absent from 91.8% of the servers audited, and 687 tool instances expose shell execution with no access control at all, across a set of servers that turns over every few days.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00150" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2608.00150, Exposed by Design: A Dynamic Security Assessment of Internet-Facing MCP Servers at Scale</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 31</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-53" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication filesystem privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-17623, CVE-2026-17626, CVE-2026-9077, CVE-2026-8446, CVE-2026-7646</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM&#39;s Second Langflow Bulletin in Three Weeks Carries Five More MCP Findings <a href="#incident-53" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published bulletin 7282147 on July 31, 2026, seventeen days after the one covering the <code>SHELLOPTS</code> blocklist gap. Seven CVEs, five of them in MCP handling, all fixed in Langflow OSS 1.11.0. CVE-2026-17623 (CVSS 8.8) is improper validation of the <code>command</code> field in MCP server configurations, giving a remote authenticated attacker arbitrary command execution. CVE-2026-17626 (8.8) is incomplete filtering of Docker volume-mount and device-mapping arguments for Docker-based MCP servers, exposing host files for read or modification. CVE-2026-9077 (8.5) lets a remote authenticated attacker bypass localhost-only restrictions and write arbitrary MCP server configurations into IDE config files on the host. CVE-2026-8446 (7.5) is an authentication bypass in the MCP composer endpoint, which ships enabled by default. CVE-2026-7646 (6.5) is a URL-encoded path traversal in <code>resources/read</code> that returns the JWT signing secret, the SQLite database, other users&#39; uploads, and process environment variables.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/zero-days-without.png" alt="a langflow bulletin with remote code execution in an mcp path" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>a langflow bulletin with remote code execution in an mcp path</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Five of the seven findings in this bulletin sit in MCP paths, and the previous bulletin was MCP too. The protocol surface is where this codebase keeps failing, which is what tends to happen when it arrives after the architecture.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Command execution and host file access for any authenticated user, plus an auth bypass on an endpoint that is on by default. The IDE config write is the one that travels: a Langflow host ends up registering attacker-chosen MCP servers in a developer&#39;s editor.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7282147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin 7282147: Langflow OSS (seven CVEs, five in MCP paths)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-17623" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-17623</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-17626" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-17626</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-9077" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-9077</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-8446" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-8446</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-7646" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-7646</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 31</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-44" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization ssrf denial-of-service data-leak"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-14537, CVE-2026-14538, CVE-2026-14539, CVE-2026-14540, CVE-2026-14541</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Google&#39;s mcp-toolbox Patches Five, Including an OAuth Path That Accepts Any Google Token <a href="#incident-44" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Google shipped fixes for five mcp-toolbox CVEs on July 31, 2026. CVE-2026-14541 is the loudest: a Google <code>authService</code> initialized with <code>mcpEnabled: true</code> but no explicit audience or clientId skips audience validation for opaque tokens entirely, so the toolbox accepts any valid Google OAuth access token, including ones minted for unrelated applications. CVE-2026-14537 lets an unauthenticated caller invoke tools protected by <code>scopeRequired</code> through legacy HTTP endpoints when <code>--enable-api</code> is on. CVE-2026-14540 is SSRF: the generic HTTP client is built with no <code>CheckRedirect</code> policy and no target IP validation, so a crafted path parameter walks it into internal endpoints. CVE-2026-14538 has <code>bigquery-execute-sql</code> trusting the BigQuery dry-run API to enforce <code>allowedDatasets</code>, then failing open when that API returns an empty array. CVE-2026-14539 rounds it off with <code>io.ReadAll</code> on the <code>/mcp</code> handler and no size cap.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two of the five fail open. The dataset check trusts an empty array, and the audience check skips itself when nobody configured an audience. A missing config value should narrow what you accept, not widen it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Unauthenticated tool invocation, cross-application token reuse, SSRF into internal networks, dataset allowlists that quietly stop applying, and a single oversized request that ends the process.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox/pull/3450" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">googleapis/mcp-toolbox PR #3450: Google OAuth audience validation (CVE-2026-14541)</a><a href="https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox/pull/3435" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">googleapis/mcp-toolbox PR #3435: scopeRequired bypass on legacy HTTP endpoints (CVE-2026-14537)</a><a href="https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox/pull/3448" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">googleapis/mcp-toolbox PR #3448: HTTP client redirect policy (CVE-2026-14540)</a><a href="https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox/pull/3452" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">googleapis/mcp-toolbox PR #3452: bigquery-execute-sql allowedDatasets bypass (CVE-2026-14538)</a><a href="https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox/pull/3216" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">googleapis/mcp-toolbox PR #3216: unbounded /mcp request body (CVE-2026-14539)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-52" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce ssrf cloud-metadata supply-chain command-injection"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-69257, CVE-2026-69263</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Flowise Blocked npx --yes, So the Custom MCP Node Reads npm_config_yes Instead <a href="#incident-52" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two Flowise advisories published on July 29, 2026, both fixed in 3.1.3. CVE-2026-69263 is a bypass of the CVE-2025-8943 patch. That patch blocked the <code>-y</code> and <code>--yes</code> flags on <code>npx</code>, and <code>packages/components/nodes/tools/MCP/core.ts</code> denied environment variables by exact name: <code>PATH</code>, <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code>, <code>DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH</code>, and <code>NODE_OPTIONS</code>. npm also reads its own configuration from <code>npm_config_*</code> variables, so setting <code>npm_config_yes=true</code> reproduces <code>--yes</code> without touching a blocked flag, and launching a Custom MCP server then auto-installs and executes the named package. CVSS 8.7. CVE-2026-69257 landed the same day: <code>httpSecurity.ts</code> did not normalize IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses before testing them, so <code>::ffff:169.254.169.254</code> was classified as IPv6 and skipped every IPv4 CIDR rule in <code>isDeniedIP()</code>, the check used for MCP server URL validation. CVSS 7.6.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/surprised-pikachu.jpg" alt="the denylist, learning that npm also reads npm_config_yes" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the denylist, learning that npm also reads npm_config_yes</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A denylist of four variable names is a bet that npm has exactly one way to say yes. It has two, and the second one is documented.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>On a default unauthenticated Flowise deployment, a Custom MCP server definition installs and runs an attacker&#39;s npm package. Separately, the SSRF guard covering MCP server URLs can be pointed at cloud metadata and internal services.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise/security/advisories/GHSA-xc48-889x-5qmw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-xc48-889x-5qmw: Flowise npm_config_yes bypass in Custom MCP node (CVE-2026-69263)</a><a href="https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise/security/advisories/GHSA-c6xh-wv4j-ppv5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c6xh-wv4j-ppv5: Flowise IPv4-mapped IPv6 deny-list bypass (CVE-2026-69257)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-68" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection supply-chain authorization"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-73601</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Flowise&#39;s Custom MCP Node Takes a Second RCE in the Same Release, This Time Through PYTHONWARNINGS <a href="#incident-68" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-g98q-rm45-q9h8, reported by igor-magun-wd and published July 29, 2026, is the third Flowise advisory fixed in 3.1.3 and the second in the Custom MCP node. With <code>CUSTOM_MCP_PROTOCOL</code> set to <code>stdio</code>, which is the default, the node&#39;s environment-variable denylist still leaves an authenticated user two routes to code execution. Against a <code>python3</code> server, <code>PYTHONWARNINGS</code> set to <code>module::antigravity.</code> alongside a chosen <code>BROWSER</code> value runs a command during interpreter startup. Against <code>node</code>, the spawned process has <code>/</code> as its working directory, which defeats the absolute-path validation, and a <code>HOME</code> carrying JavaScript is picked up through <code>require()</code>. Refreshing the available actions on the node triggers either one. CVSS 9.0, affecting <code>flowise</code> and <code>flowise-components</code> 3.1.2 and earlier.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/three-headed-dragon.jpg" alt="PYTHONWARNINGS, BROWSER, and HOME, none of them on the denylist" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>PYTHONWARNINGS, BROWSER, and HOME, none of them on the denylist</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three advisories, one release, and two of them in the same node. The earlier fixes blocked flag names and variable names. This one arrives through the interpreter&#39;s own startup hooks, which are documented, numerous, and not on anybody&#39;s list.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated Flowise user runs arbitrary commands on the host and takes over the application.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise/security/advisories/GHSA-g98q-rm45-q9h8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-g98q-rm45-q9h8: Flowise Custom MCP node RCE via environment variables (CVE-2026-73601)</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/flowise-before-remote-code-execution-via-custom-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck: Flowise remote code execution via Custom MCP</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-33" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication credential-theft command-injection exploited-in-the-wild"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-59726</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Ruflo MCP Bridge Binds 233 Unauthenticated Tools to 0.0.0.0 by Default (RufRoot, CVSS 10.0) <a href="#incident-33" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Noma Labs publicly disclosed CVE-2026-59726 on July 29, 2026, codenamed RufRoot, against Ruflo, an open-source AI-agent platform with 67,000+ GitHub stars that sits at #2 on MCPMarket. Ruflo&#39;s MCP Bridge is an Express.js server that exposes 233 internal tools over MCP: shell command execution, database operations, agent management, and memory storage. The shipped <code>docker-compose.yml</code> binds port 3001 to <code>0.0.0.0</code>, and the bridge&#39;s tool-invocation endpoints require no authentication. A single unauthenticated HTTP POST from anywhere on the network reaches <code>terminal_execute</code> and any of the other 232 tools. Noma reported the finding to maintainer Reuven Cohen on June 30, 2026 with a working proof-of-concept against a default deployment; a fix shipped in Ruflo 3.16.3 within 24 hours, binding the bridge to loopback, gating <code>terminal_execute</code> behind server-side executeTool controls, and turning on MongoDB authentication. CVSS 10.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imtryingtounderstand.webp" alt="trying to understand why terminal_execute shipped without auth" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>trying to understand why terminal_execute shipped without auth</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Two hundred and thirty-three tools. One of them is <em><code>terminal_execute</code></em>. Zero authentication. The docker-compose file that binds <code>0.0.0.0</code> and the tool registry that includes shell exec are in the same repo, shipped by the same maintainer, and neither one is <em>hidden</em>. It took Noma an afternoon to write the PoC and the maintainer 24 hours to fix it, which is the whole timeline you need to know.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who can reach port 3001 on a default Ruflo install executes arbitrary shell commands, reads and rewrites the platform&#39;s persistent AI memory, exfiltrates the LLM API keys the platform brokers, and takes over every agent it manages. The docker-compose default meant that reach was `0.0.0.0`, not `127.0.0.1`.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://noma.security/blog/rufroot-the-mcp-bridge-vulnerability-that-turns-agents-into-rogue-admins-cve-2026-59726/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Noma Security: RufRoot, The MCP Bridge Vulnerability That Turns Agents Into Rogue Admins (CVE-2026-59726)</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/ruflo-mcp-flaw-lets-unauthenticated.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Ruflo MCP Flaw Lets Unauthenticated Attackers Run Commands and Poison AI Memory</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-34" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization ssrf credential-theft cross-tenant"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-14869, CVE-2026-16326, CVE-2026-16496</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> HashiCorp Ships Terraform and Consul MCP Server Advisories on Consecutive Days, Both Session-State in Stateless Mode <a href="#incident-34" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">HashiCorp posted HCSEC-2026-23 on July 28, 2026 for <code>terraform-mcp-server</code> and HCSEC-2026-24 on July 29, 2026 for <code>consul-mcp-server</code>. Both bulletins document session-state failures in each server&#39;s streamable-HTTP stateless transport. In Terraform MCP Server, CVE-2026-14869 (CVSS 8.6, High) lets an unauthenticated client override the outbound Terraform API destination so the server sends its own authorization bearer token to an attacker-controlled endpoint, and CVE-2026-16496 lets a caller who obtains another user&#39;s MCP session ID run tool calls under that user&#39;s Terraform credentials. In Consul MCP Server, CVE-2026-16326 (CVSS 10.0, Critical) lets one client reuse another client&#39;s Consul authentication token across requests, and a companion issue lets clients override the Consul backend address to redirect API traffic to an attacker-controlled endpoint. Fix versions are <code>terraform-mcp-server</code> 1.1.0 and <code>consul-mcp-server</code> 0.1.4. Both defective ranges are 0.2.1 through 1.0.0 for Terraform and 0.1.0 through 0.1.3 for Consul.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/theyre-the-same-picture.png" alt="hcsec-2026-23 · hcsec-2026-24, on consecutive-day disclosure" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>hcsec-2026-23 · hcsec-2026-24, on consecutive-day disclosure</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">HashiCorp put out two MCP server advisories in two calendar days and both were <code>stateless mode is stateful, actually</code> in a different key. The 2026-07-28 MCP spec ships the same week and its headline feature is <em>stateless request/response</em>. Every SDK vendor is going to spend the next six months learning what web-framework authors learned when PHP sessions were new.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Two of HashiCorp&#39;s flagship MCP servers, in the same 24-hour window, hand their configured backend addresses or their session-bound credentials to whichever HTTP request asks. The Consul session-mixup is the same defect people spent 2003 fixing in web frameworks; the CVSS is 10.0 because the mixed-up thing is a Consul ACL token in an enterprise deployment.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://discuss.hashicorp.com/t/hcsec-2026-23-multiple-vulnerabilities-impacting-hashicorp-terraform-mcp-server/77606" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HashiCorp Discuss: HCSEC-2026-23, Multiple vulnerabilities impacting HashiCorp Terraform MCP Server</a><a href="https://discuss.hashicorp.com/t/hcsec-2026-24-multiple-vulnerabilities-impacting-hashicorp-consul-mcp-server/77612" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HashiCorp Discuss: HCSEC-2026-24, Multiple vulnerabilities impacting HashiCorp Consul MCP Server</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-16326" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-16326 (Consul MCP session state isolation)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 28</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-35" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Model Context Protocol 2026-07-28 Spec Rips Out the Stateful Handshake, Deprecates Sampling and Roots and Legacy HTTP+SSE <a href="#incident-35" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The MCP maintainers published the 2026-07-28 specification on July 28, 2026, the largest revision since launch. The bidirectional stateful protocol becomes a stateless request/response protocol. The <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> header is gone; every request carries its own protocol version, client identity, and capabilities. Server-initiated sampling and elicitation streams are replaced by Multi Round-Trip Requests (<code>resultType: input_required</code>) so the client re-issues the call with the required inputs. New <code>Mcp-Method</code> and <code>Mcp-Name</code> HTTP headers let gateways route without parsing JSON bodies. Authorization moves to RFC 9207 issuer validation, credentials are bound to the issuing server, and Dynamic Client Registration is being deprecated in favor of Client ID Metadata Documents. Roots, Sampling, Logging, and the legacy HTTP+SSE transport are all on a 12-month sunset. Tier 1 SDKs (TypeScript, Python, Go, C#) ship day-one support; Rust ships in beta.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The spec that got this site started is being deprecated. The maintainers heard the disclosures, agreed with a surprising number of them, and shipped the <em>rewrite</em>. It is genuinely the correct move. It is also going to generate a new run of CVEs, because <code>Mcp-Method</code> and <code>Mcp-Name</code> are HTTP headers, and HTTP headers have a track record.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>The specific attack surfaces that produced Asana&#39;s cross-tenant leak, the mcp-remote command injection, and the string of session-hijack advisories catalogued elsewhere on this site are, per the release notes, no longer part of the spec. What arrives in their place is a stateless HTTP protocol with two custom routing headers, which is a category of design that has produced request-smuggling and header-desync bugs in every previous incarnation.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model Context Protocol Blog: The 2026-07-28 Specification</a><a href="https://www.securityweek.com/new-enterprise-ready-mcp-specification-brings-new-security-challenges/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SecurityWeek: New Enterprise-Ready MCP Specification Brings New Security Challenges</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 28</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-42" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication denial-of-service github advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-47427</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> GitHub&#39;s Own MCP Server Panics on a Completion Request With No Ref, Before It Checks Your Token <a href="#incident-42" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-47427 covers GitHub&#39;s official MCP Server prior to 1.1.0. The <code>CompletionsHandler</code> function in <code>pkg/github/server.go</code> reads <code>params.Ref</code> without first checking whether it is nil, so a <code>completion/complete</code> request with a missing or empty <code>ref</code> field dereferences nil and takes the Go runtime down with a panic. The crash happens before any authentication or token validation runs, so any unauthenticated client that can send JSON-RPC messages can do it. Fixed in 1.1.0.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">CVSS put this at 7.5. It is availability only, so it sits at Medium here. The part worth keeping is the ordering: the panic beats the token check, which is what makes <em>unauthenticated</em> load-bearing in that sentence.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One malformed JSON-RPC message from an unauthenticated client crashes GitHub&#39;s official MCP server. Every agent connected to it loses its tooling until the process comes back.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-w4q6-qw23-4rg7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-w4q6-qw23-4rg7: GitHub MCP Server nil pointer dereference in CompletionsHandler</a><a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/pull/2502" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github/github-mcp-server PR #2502</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-51" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce sandbox-escape authentication"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-67531</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> FrontMCP&#39;s Sandbox Returns the Raw Host Object Because a Proxy Is Not Allowed to Lie <a href="#incident-51" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">FrontMCP is a TypeScript framework for MCP. Its <code>codecall:execute</code> tool runs caller-supplied scripts behind a Proxy-based security membrane, and <code>getTool()</code> exposes live Zod schema instances to those scripts. Zod v4 defines <code>_zod</code> as a non-configurable, non-writable own property, and ECMAScript requires a Proxy to report the true value for exactly that kind of property. The membrane is therefore obliged to hand back the unwrapped host object. From there, <code>_zod.constr.constructor</code> is the host <code>Function</code> constructor. Assembling those property names at runtime also slips past the lexical denylist, which inspects the AST. Affects <code>@frontmcp/plugin-codecall</code> through 1.5.6, fixed in 1.5.7. CVSS 9.3, and the framework default of <code>auth: { mode: &#39;public&#39; }</code> means no credentials are needed.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Nothing here is a coding mistake. The membrane followed the Proxy invariants exactly, and following them is what surrendered the <code>Function</code> constructor. A sandbox built on Proxies inherits every guarantee the language makes, including the guarantee that it cannot misreport a frozen property.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Arbitrary code execution in the MCP server process, reachable over the network without authentication on a default configuration.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/agentfront/frontmcp/security/advisories/GHSA-mp29-fxh8-92px" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-mp29-fxh8-92px: FrontMCP codecall sandbox escape via Zod schema membrane leak</a><a href="https://github.com/agentfront/frontmcp/commit/209cddd19a8d4db0777f725b527818da7df6f67f" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">agentfront/frontmcp: fix commit 209cddd</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-41" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="aws authorization prompt-injection privilege-escalation advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-16584</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS API MCP Server Logs a Warning When Its Policy Engine Fails to Load, Then Serves Every Request Unchecked <a href="#incident-41" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-16584, published July 23, 2026, covers <code>awslabs.aws-api-mcp-server</code> 0.2.13 through 1.3.46. The server loads a read-only operations index at startup and uses it to apply the operator&#39;s deny and gate rules to AWS CLI commands. If that load throws, whether from a transient network failure, a file permission problem, or anything else, the server writes a warning to the log and keeps running. The per-request policy check is then skipped for the entire lifetime of the process, and so are the consent prompts. Classified CWE-455, non-exit on failed initialization. Version 1.3.47 refuses to start when the index will not initialize and blocks command execution if it goes missing at runtime. CVSS 7.3.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/this-is-fine.jpg" alt="the operations index failed to load. the server logged it and kept serving" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the operations index failed to load. the server logged it and kept serving</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The degraded state and the healthy state look identical from outside the process. One <code>WARNING</code> line in a log is the entire difference between a configured policy engine and no policy engine at all.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Every configured deny and gate rule stops applying, silently, until someone restarts the process. Indirect prompt injection can then drive the mutating AWS CLI commands the operator explicitly blocked, with IAM on the credentials as the only control left standing.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-29w2-fq35-v728" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-29w2-fq35-v728: AWS API MCP Server security policy bypass via startup initialization failure</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-67" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf local-network authorization"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-72768</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> n8n&#39;s MCP Client Node Sends Its Requests Around the Platform&#39;s SSRF Protection <a href="#incident-67" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-vhf8-cg2h-cg3p, published July 22, 2026, covers two n8n maintenance lines: the 2.31 line below 2.31.5, and the 2.32 line below 2.32.1. The MCP Client node sends requests to user-supplied endpoints without routing them through the configured SSRF protections, and it does not pin resolved addresses, so a hostname that resolves acceptably during a check can resolve elsewhere on the request itself. CVSS 6.4, and NVD later assigned CVE-2026-72768. Fixed in 2.31.5 and 2.32.1. For operators who cannot upgrade, n8n suggests restricting access to trusted users, disabling the node through <code>NODES_EXCLUDE</code>, or applying network-level egress rules.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The SSRF protection is configured once, at the platform level, and one node declines to use it. A platform-wide guarantee lasts exactly until a single node opts out of it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A user who can create or edit a workflow points the server at internal or blocked hosts and reads the responses back out through the workflow.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/security/advisories/GHSA-vhf8-cg2h-cg3p" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-vhf8-cg2h-cg3p: n8n MCP Client node bypasses SSRF protections</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-72768" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-72768</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-32" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection llm-manipulation data-exfiltration privilege-escalation authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Microsoft Azure DevOps MCP Server Returns Invisible PR Comments Verbatim, Prompt Injection Turns Reviewer Agent Into a Cross-Project Exfiltrator <a href="#incident-32" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Manifold Security published <code>When Your AI Reviewer Works for the Attacker</code> on July 22, 2026, describing a confused-deputy flaw in Microsoft&#39;s official <code>azure-devops-mcp</code> server. Azure DevOps PR descriptions accept Markdown, and the PR-description tool the server exposes to the agent returns the raw body from the API without stripping HTML comments. An attacker with contributor access to any project the victim&#39;s Azure DevOps account can see opens a PR whose description is an ordinary human sentence in the rendered UI and a paragraph of instructions inside an <code>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</code> block underneath. When the victim tells their agent to review the PR, the agent ingests the invisible block as tool output and follows the instructions, which typically direct it to read work-item contents or files from a private project the attacker has no direct access to and stitch them into the review comment the attacker will get to see. Microsoft ships a prompt-injection guardrail called <code>spotlighting</code> on some tools in the same server, but not the pull-request-description tool the bug lives on. MSRC has acknowledged the report; no CVE and no fix as of publication.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yousureaboutthat.png" alt="the review-agent trust model when the pull-request-body tool returns raw markdown" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the review-agent trust model when the pull-request-body tool returns raw markdown</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Microsoft already knows this attack. The company shipped <code>spotlighting</code> as an antidote to indirect prompt injection and applied it to some of the tools this server exposes. The PR-description tool, the one whose whole job is to hand attacker-controlled Markdown to a language model, is not one of them.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A contributor to any single project in an Azure DevOps organization writes hidden text into a PR description and steers a victim&#39;s AI reviewer into reading and returning data from projects the contributor was never authorized to see. The exfil channel is whatever comment or artifact the agent posts back on the poisoned PR.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.manifold.security/blog/azure-devops-mcp-server-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Manifold Security: When Your AI Reviewer Works for the Attacker, A Confused-Deputy Bug in Microsoft&#39;s Azure DevOps MCP Server</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/microsoft-azure-devops-mcp-flaw-lets.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Microsoft Azure DevOps MCP Flaw Lets Hidden PR Comments Hijack AI Review Agents</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 22</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-31" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="prompt-injection rce file-overwrite aws llm-manipulation"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-10591</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS Kiro Fetches a Poisoned Web Page, Follows the Hidden Instructions to Rewrite Its Own mcp.json, and Auto-Loads the Attacker&#39;s Server <a href="#incident-31" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Intezer Research, with Kodem Security, published <code>When the AI Edits Its Own Trust Boundary</code> on July 21, 2026 against AWS Kiro, Amazon&#39;s agentic IDE. The agent has unattended write access to <code>~/.kiro/settings/mcp.json</code> and Kiro auto-reloads that file the instant it changes. A page hosted anywhere on the public web can carry an invisible block of prompt-injection payload; a developer asking Kiro to summarize the URL sends the whole page through the model, and the payload instructs the agent to add an attacker-controlled MCP server entry to the settings file. The reload fires, the attacker&#39;s server process starts under the developer&#39;s account, and the tool description it advertises to the agent becomes the next prompt-injection surface. Amazon assigned CVE-2026-10591 on July 22, 2026 and shipped the fix in Kiro 0.11.130. Kiro now marks <code>mcp.json</code>, <code>.vscode/tasks.json</code>, <code>.git</code>, and other paths as protected so the agent has to obtain explicit developer approval before writing them.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/isthatthejoke.jpg" alt="letting the model write `mcp.json` was the entire security boundary" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>letting the model write `mcp.json` was the entire security boundary</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The IDE&#39;s answer to the question <code>who is allowed to configure this IDE&#39;s tools</code> was <code>whoever the agent decides to trust</code>, and the agent&#39;s answer to <code>who is allowed to write my prompt</code> was <code>whoever wrote the last web page you asked me to read</code>. Kiro&#39;s fix is a hardcoded list of files the model is not allowed to edit without a click, which is the correct fix and also a small monument to the design that got here.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A URL a developer thought they were asking their IDE to summarize gets code execution on the developer&#39;s machine with the developer&#39;s own privileges. Anything reachable from that shell is reachable to the attacker, including cloud credentials, source trees, and the local git and npm identities the developer signs commits and releases with.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://research.intezer.com/blog/2026/07/remote-code-execution-kiro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intezer Research: When the AI Edits Its Own Trust Boundary, Remote Code Execution Vulnerability in AWS&#39;s Agentic IDE</a><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-019" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin AWS-2025-019: Amazon Q Developer and Kiro, Prompt Injection Issues in Kiro and Q IDE plugins</a><a href="https://www.kodemsecurity.com/resources/aws-kiro-agentic-ide-rce-prompt-injection-mcp-config-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kodem Security: AWS Kiro Agentic IDE, Prompt Injection to Code Execution via MCP Config Vulnerability</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="file-overwrite" aria-pressed="false">file-overwrite</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-160" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication credential-theft local-network advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-114090</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> trigger dev --mcp Binds Every Interface and Authenticates Nobody <a href="#incident-160" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">carderne published GHSA-pqmg-rq46-vm4m on July 21, 2026 against <code>@trigger.dev/cli-v3</code> from 3.0.0, fixed in 4.5.4. Running <code>trigger dev --mcp</code> starts an MCP server whose listen call omits the hostname argument, so it binds the default network interface rather than loopback, and its <code>/sse</code> and <code>/messages</code> endpoints carry no auth middleware. The developer&#39;s Trigger.dev access token is bound to the server at startup, so whoever reaches the port spends it: trigger a task run, cancel a run, list runs, read run state and read run logs. The reporter names the three root causes as the missing hostname argument, the absent auth middleware and the token bound at startup. VulnCheck assigned CVE-2026-114090 on October 20, scoring it 7.6 under CVSS 4.0 and 7.1 under 3.1, and NVD published it on October 21, 92 days after the advisory.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/sad-pablo-escobar.jpg" alt="pablo waiting alone on a swing: july advisory, october cve" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>pablo waiting alone on a swing: july advisory, october cve</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Three separate root causes in one flag, and the token is the worst of them: binding it at startup means the server does not need to be tricked into anything, it just needs to be reachable. The CVE landed in October for an advisory published in July, which is the ordinary shape now. The repository said it first and the catalog arrived a quarter later.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone on the developer&#39;s network, any other process on that machine, and any web page the developer happens to have open can drive their Trigger.dev account using the CLI&#39;s own stored token.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/triggerdotdev/trigger.dev/security/advisories/GHSA-pqmg-rq46-vm4m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Trigger.dev GHSA-pqmg-rq46-vm4m: missing authentication in the trigger dev --mcp server</a><a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/trigger-dev-cli-3.0.0-before-4.5.4-missing-authentication-in-mcp-server" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulnCheck advisory for CVE-2026-114090: Trigger.dev cli-v3 missing authentication in MCP server</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-114090" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-114090 (VulnCheck scores, CVSS 4.0 and 3.1)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-30" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain ai-supply-chain github exploited-in-the-wild credential-theft llm-manipulation"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Island Security Publishes AgentBaiting: 800+ Fake MCP Servers Inside a 7,600-Repo FakeGit Campaign Deliver SmartLoader When AI Agents Recommend Them <a href="#incident-30" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Island Security published <code>AgentBaiting: How Fake AI Skills Deliver Malware at Scale</code> on July 21, 2026, and Bleeping Computer and Help Net Security wrote it up the same week. The wider FakeGit operation, ~7,600 GitHub repositories across ~6,600 accounts, seeds convincing lookalikes of common developer tooling; roughly 800 of those repositories pose as MCP servers or <code>awesome-ai-skills</code>-style catalogs. The AI-skill wave built through March and peaked in April 2026 and the repositories have appeared more than 600 times in public AI registries and catalogs. Island&#39;s testing found Claude Code, Google Gemini, and ChatGPT surfacing the fake repositories unprompted when asked for an MCP server for a given task and then handing the README&#39;s install instructions to the developer. The install ZIP drops SmartLoader, which pulls in the StealC infostealer. About 200 of the FakeGit repositories account for the 14 million measured downloads across GitHub Release assets to date.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The MCP registry story for the last year has been <code>the agent will find you the right tool.</code> The AgentBaiting result is that the agent does, in fact, find a tool; the tool is a payload; and the developer&#39;s role in the exchange is to say yes.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An AI coding agent asked to find an MCP server or Skill returns an attacker&#39;s repository as a legitimate option and walks the developer through installing it. On install, SmartLoader lands and pulls StealC, which harvests credentials, tokens, cookies, and session material from browsers and developer tooling. The exposure scales with the agent, not the developer, because the agent is the discovery layer.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.island.io/blog/agentbaiting-how-800-fake-ai-skills-and-mcp-servers-delivered-malware" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Island Security: AgentBaiting, How 800 Fake AI Skills and MCP Servers Delivered Malware</a><a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/fakegit-campaign-uses-7-600-github-repos-to-push-smartloader-malware/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bleeping Computer: FakeGit campaign uses 7,600 GitHub repos to push SmartLoader malware</a><a href="https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/07/21/github-repos-malware-campaign-fakegit-ai-agents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Help Net Security: AI agents tricked into recommending malicious GitHub repositories</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-75" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="credential-theft cross-tenant data-leak authorization"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-71424</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Onyx Copies Every User’s MCP OAuth Token Into One Shared Admin Row <a href="#incident-75" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Onyx published GHSA-q62f-rv3h-f822 on July 20, 2026. In <code>backend/onyx/server/features/mcp/api.py</code>, <code>OnyxTokenStorage.set_tokens</code> and <code>set_client_info</code> wrote per-user OAuth tokens into a shared admin <code>MCPConnectionConfig</code> row, and <code>_db_mcp_server_to_api_mcp_server</code> returned that row through <code>auth_template.headers</code>. <code>GET /api/mcp/servers</code> and <code>GET /api/mcp/servers/persona/{persona_id}</code> therefore returned another user’s <code>Authorization</code> header to any BASIC_ACCESS user. CVSS 9.6 with scope changed. The code fix landed in PR #11238 on May 20, the advisory followed two months later, and NVD assigned CVE-2026-71424 on August 17. Fixed in 3.1.10, 3.2.14, and 4.0.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/oprah-you-get-a.jpg" alt="GET /api/mcp/servers, distributing everyone else’s oauth header" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>GET /api/mcp/servers, distributing everyone else’s oauth header</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Per-user tokens, stored in a row called admin. The endpoint that hands them out is the one that lists your MCP servers, so it is the endpoint everybody calls. The credentials are for third-party services that will never learn any of this happened.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any authenticated Onyx user with basic access reads other users’ OAuth Authorization headers for their connected MCP servers and impersonates them against those upstream services.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/onyx-dot-app/onyx/security/advisories/GHSA-q62f-rv3h-f822" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Onyx advisory GHSA-q62f-rv3h-f822 (CVE-2026-71424)</a><a href="https://github.com/onyx-dot-app/onyx/pull/11238" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">onyx-dot-app/onyx PR #11238: fix mcp oauth token handling</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-146" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authorization authentication advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-84317, CVE-2026-84318, CVE-2026-84319, CVE-2026-84360</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> nginx-ui Validates Nginx Directives on Three of Its Four Config Write Paths <a href="#incident-146" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Four advisories landed against nginx-ui on July 17, 2026, all fixed in 2.4.1 and scored 8.1 to 8.8 at NVD. <code>internal/config/validation.go</code>&#39;s <code>ValidateConfigFile</code> is the control that blocks <code>js_<em></code>, <code>perl_</em></code>, <code>load_module</code> of the njs and perl modules, and <code>user root</code>. CVE-2026-84317 (GHSA-76pm-mq2q-9gcr) is the MCP tool <code>nginx_config_add</code> being the one write path it is not wired to. CVE-2026-84318 (GHSA-9jww-cxg5-rj9m) is that same tool writing config to disk and triggering a reload without calling it, where the identical REST flow at <code>POST /api/configs</code> does. CVE-2026-84319 (GHSA-5r62-gj48-897x) is the MCP configuration interface accepting restricted directives that the regular HTTP API rejects, <code>load_module</code>, <code>js_import</code> and <code>js_content</code> among them. CVE-2026-84360 (GHSA-vc32-4gwf-77xp) is <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/mcp_message</code> requiring only ordinary authentication where the HTTP mutation routes require <code>RequireSecureSession</code>, so an OTP-enabled user skips the step-up by going through MCP.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/squidward-window.jpg" alt="squidward at the blinds watching ValidateConfigFile cover three of the four writers" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>squidward at the blinds watching ValidateConfigFile cover three of the four writers</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">This site has carried nginx-ui since April 2026, when <code>/mcp</code> had the auth middleware and <code>/mcp_message</code>, the one that writes config, did not. Three months later the validator and the step-up are both wired to the HTTP routes and both miss the MCP ones. The MCP surface keeps getting added beside the security controls instead of behind them.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated user loads an njs module or sets `user root` through the MCP tools, which is code execution as root on the official image, and does it without the OTP step-up the web interface demands for the same change.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/0xJacky/nginx-ui/security/advisories/GHSA-76pm-mq2q-9gcr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-76pm-mq2q-9gcr: MCP nginx_config_add skips config directive validation (CVE-2026-84317)</a><a href="https://github.com/0xJacky/nginx-ui/security/advisories/GHSA-9jww-cxg5-rj9m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9jww-cxg5-rj9m: MCP nginx_config_add bypasses directive validation, authenticated RCE (CVE-2026-84318)</a><a href="https://github.com/0xJacky/nginx-ui/security/advisories/GHSA-5r62-gj48-897x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-5r62-gj48-897x: MCP configuration tools bypass restricted directive validation and allow NJS execution (CVE-2026-84319)</a><a href="https://github.com/0xJacky/nginx-ui/security/advisories/GHSA-vc32-4gwf-77xp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-vc32-4gwf-77xp: OTP secure-session step-up can be bypassed via MCP configuration tools (CVE-2026-84360)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-48" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization privilege-escalation credential-theft rce"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-67357, CVE-2026-68578</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ArcadeDB&#39;s MCP Transport Never Binds the Authenticated User, So Every Permission Check Passes <a href="#incident-48" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two advisories landed against ArcadeDB before 26.7.3 on July 17, 2026. CVE-2026-68578 is the structural one: the MCP HTTP transport fails to bind the authenticated principal, so every engine permission check silently passes as a no-op. Any non-root user allowed to reach MCP can perform arbitrary database writes, DDL, and schema mutations, and can execute arbitrary JavaScript through the <code>query</code> tool. CVE-2026-67357 sits next to it: the <code>get_server_settings</code> MCP tool returns <code>arcadedb.ha.clusterToken</code> in cleartext, and that token combined with the <code>X-ArcadeDB-Cluster-Token</code> and <code>X-ArcadeDB-Forwarded-User</code> headers is enough to impersonate root. Both rated CVSS 7.5.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/werealltryingtofindtheguywhodidthis.jpg" alt="the engine permission checks, silently returning true since the mcp transport shipped" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the engine permission checks, silently returning true since the mcp transport shipped</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The permission checks were running the entire time. They just had nobody to check them against, and passing was the default. Chain the token leak on top and a low-privilege user owns the server.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Read the cluster token out of one tool call and come back as root. Skip that entirely and an ordinary MCP-allowed user still has arbitrary JavaScript execution, because the permission checks are decorative.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ArcadeData/arcadedb/security/advisories/GHSA-6x73-v3rc-f57c" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-6x73-v3rc-f57c: ArcadeDB authentication bypass via MCP transport</a><a href="https://github.com/ArcadeData/arcadedb/security/advisories/GHSA-p9wc-4fhr-78wm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-p9wc-4fhr-78wm: ArcadeDB information disclosure via get_server_settings</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-37" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="rce command-injection credential-theft authorization cursor"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Adversa AI&#39;s DeepJack Turns Cursor&#39;s cursor:// MCP Install Handler Into One-Click RCE <a href="#incident-37" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Adversa AI published DeepJack on July 15, 2026, an attack class against the <code>cursor://</code> protocol handler that Cursor registers with the operating system at install time. A deeplink pointing at Cursor&#39;s <code>mcp/install</code> endpoint pops the MCP server install dialog, and that dialog is the only consent step between a clicked link and an attacker-controlled command running with the developer&#39;s privileges. The researchers pad the command with whitespace so trailing arguments sit off the right edge of the dialog&#39;s single-line box, and they nest a double-URL-encoded <code>mcp/install</code> URI inside a <code>pr-review</code> parameter that Cursor never recursively decodes, so a link that presents as a pull-request review carries an install instruction. Cursor&#39;s triage closed both reports as duplicates of an issue filed internally on April 27, 2026. Build 3.9.8, shipped after the disclosure, still reproduces it. No CVE was assigned.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/spiderman point.png" alt="deeplink handler, install dialog, url decoder: each assuming another checked" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>deeplink handler, install dialog, url decoder: each assuming another checked</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Cursor knew about the dialog on April 27 and shipped <code>3.9.8</code> almost three months later with the primitive intact. The install dialog is the entire security model for <code>cursor://</code>, and it renders the command in a single-line box that scrolls. <em>Whitespace</em> is the exploit.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One click and one Approve on a link that looks like a code review installs the attacker&#39;s MCP server and runs its command unsandboxed. That reaches SSH keys, cloud credentials, live session tokens, browser cookies, source code, and anything else the developer&#39;s account can touch, plus a path straight into CI.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://adversa.ai/blog/cursor-security-deepjack-deeplink-vulnerability-mcp-rce/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adversa AI: DeepJack Cursor deeplink vulnerability, 1-click MCP server RCE</a><a href="https://www.darkreading.com/application-security/2-click-cursor-exploit-dev-environment-takeover" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dark Reading: 2-Click Cursor Exploit Enables Dev Environment Takeover</a><a href="https://www.securityweek.com/unpatched-cursor-vulnerability-exposes-users-to-code-execution/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SecurityWeek: Unpatched Cursor Vulnerability Exposes Users to Code Execution</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cursor" aria-pressed="false">cursor</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 14</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-43" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-12940</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> IBM Langflow&#39;s MCP stdio Launcher Blocklist Forgot SHELLOPTS, BASHOPTS, and PS4 <a href="#incident-43" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">IBM published CVE-2026-12940 on July 14, 2026 against Langflow OSS 1.0.0 through 1.10.1: unauthenticated remote code execution through environment variable injection in the MCP stdio launcher. <code>src/lfx/src/lfx/base/mcp/util.py</code> keeps a <code>DANGEROUS_ENV_VARS</code> blocklist of variables it refuses to pass into a spawned MCP server process. Three names were missing from it, and they happen to be the three that let you talk a shell into running a command at startup: <code>SHELLOPTS</code>, <code>BASHOPTS</code>, and <code>PS4</code>. Turning on shell tracing and pointing the trace prompt at a command substitution has been a documented bash execution trick for years. CVSS 9.8.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/roll-safe.jpg" alt="can't have env var injection if you remember every dangerous variable" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>can&#39;t have env var injection if you remember every dangerous variable</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A blocklist is a bet that you already thought of everything. This one covered the obvious names and missed the trio that every shell-injection cheatsheet lists.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated attacker reaches code execution on the Langflow host through the MCP stdio launcher. Every 1.x release up to 1.10.1 is affected.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/node/7279995" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IBM Security Bulletin: Langflow OSS unauthenticated RCE via MCP stdio launcher (CVE-2026-12940)</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-12940" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-12940</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 14</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-36" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf aws credential-theft authentication"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-15643</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS HealthLake MCP Server Doesn&#39;t Validate Its Own Pagination URLs, Ships Temp Credentials to Whoever Sends a next_token <a href="#incident-36" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-054-AWS landed on July 14, 2026 for CVE-2026-15643, a server-side request forgery in <code>awslabs.healthlake-mcp-server</code>, the AWS-published MCP server that fronts AWS HealthLake FHIR datastores. The pagination handler accepts a caller-supplied <code>next_token</code> value and dereferences it without checking that the URL points back at HealthLake. A crafted <code>next_token</code> sends the server&#39;s outbound request, together with the temporary AWS credentials the MCP server uses to call HealthLake, to any endpoint the attacker names. CVSS 3.1 is 7.3, High. Authenticated remote attacker, no user interaction. The fix ships as version 0.0.14.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The pagination token is a URL the server produced last request and is now being asked to dereference. Zero code in the tool needed to accept a caller-supplied URL. It did anyway. HealthLake is the compliance-branded HIPAA offering, which is the funny part.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A remote authenticated caller who reaches the HealthLake MCP tool gets the server to POST its own temporary AWS credentials to an attacker-controlled URL. Those credentials scope to whatever role the MCP server assumed when it started, which on a HealthLake deployment reaches every FHIR record the role can see.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-054-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-054-AWS: CVE-2026-15643, SSRF in AWS HealthLake MCP Server via Unvalidated Pagination URL</a><a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp/tree/main/src/healthlake-mcp-server" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub: awslabs/healthlake-mcp-server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-66" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem credential-theft prompt-injection authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-73498</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-atlassian Has a Path Validator, and confluence_upload_attachment Never Calls It <a href="#incident-66" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-g5r6-gv6m-f5jv, published July 10, 2026, covers <code>mcp-atlassian</code> before 0.22.0. <code>confluence_upload_attachment</code> passes its client-supplied <code>file_path</code> straight to <code>open(file_path, &quot;rb&quot;)</code> in <code>src/mcp_atlassian/confluence/attachments.py</code>, by way of <code>_upload_attachment_direct()</code>. The codebase already ships <code>validate_safe_path</code> and applies it elsewhere; this call site does not. CVSS 7.7. The fix is one validation call added ahead of the open, released in 0.22.0.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/monkey-puppet.jpg" alt="validate_safe_path, watching the upload tool call open() directly" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>validate_safe_path, watching the upload tool call open() directly</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The validator was written, shipped, and used. The one path it never reached is the one whose job is to open a file the caller names.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated MCP client, or an agent acting on injected instructions, reads any file the server process can read and uploads it to Confluence. On Linux that includes `/proc/self/environ`, which carries the server&#39;s own credentials and API tokens.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/sooperset/mcp-atlassian/security/advisories/GHSA-g5r6-gv6m-f5jv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-g5r6-gv6m-f5jv: mcp-atlassian arbitrary file read via confluence_upload_attachment</a><a href="https://github.com/sooperset/mcp-atlassian/releases/tag/v0.22.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-atlassian PR #1448 and release v0.22.0</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-65" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf data-leak authorization"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-73845, CVE-2026-73846, CVE-2026-73844</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> CKAN MCP Server Patches Three: A Prefix-Only Regex, a Cache Key That Collides, and Errors That Repeat Everything <a href="#incident-65" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Three advisories for <code>@aborruso/ckan-mcp-server</code>, all published July 9, 2026. <code>isValidMqaServer</code> validates the <code>server_url</code> parameter against <code>/^https?:\/\/(www\.)?dati\.gov\.it/i</code>, which has no end anchor and no host boundary, so <code>https://dati.gov.it.attacker.com/x</code> and <code>https://dati.gov.it@attacker.com/x</code> both pass and the MQA quality tools return an attacker&#39;s response (CVE-2026-73845, CVSS 5.3, fixed in 0.4.111). <code>canonicalizeParams</code> in <code>src/utils/cache.ts</code> joins sorted key-value pairs with unescaped <code>&amp;</code>, <code>=</code>, and <code>|</code> delimiters, so <code>{ q: &quot;budget&quot;, rows: 10 }</code> and <code>{ q: &quot;budget&amp;rows=10&quot; }</code> build the same key and share one entry in a cache that is enabled by default (CVE-2026-73846, CVSS 6.5, fixed in 0.4.111). Error paths return raw upstream response bodies and internal exception messages verbatim rather than a generic message (CVE-2026-73844, CVSS 3.7, fixed in 0.4.112).</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The error-disclosure bug scores 3.7 alone and is the least interesting of the three by itself. Sitting behind the host-validation bug, it is the channel that hands back whatever that one fetched.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker primes the shared cache so another user&#39;s query returns a response prepared for a different one, points the quality tools at a lookalike host, and reads upstream content, internal hostnames, and database errors out of the error text.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ondata/ckan-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-83x6-42hr-jc76" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-83x6-42hr-jc76: ckan-mcp-server prefix-only host validation in isValidMqaServer (CVE-2026-73845)</a><a href="https://github.com/ondata/ckan-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-78x9-fhhx-v2g6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-78x9-fhhx-v2g6: ckan-mcp-server cache key collision in canonicalizeParams (CVE-2026-73846)</a><a href="https://github.com/ondata/ckan-mcp-server/security/advisories/GHSA-6f9w-9hf2-5rg3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-6f9w-9hf2-5rg3: ckan-mcp-server reflects raw upstream bodies and exception messages (CVE-2026-73844)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-29" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem authorization data-leak"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-15138</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> tumf mcp-text-editor Path Traversal in _validate_file_path, Maintainer Closes the Report <a href="#incident-29" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-15138 landed on July 9, 2026 against tumf&#39;s <code>mcp-text-editor</code>, an MCP server that gives an AI assistant read and write access to files on the developer&#39;s machine. The <code>_validate_file_path</code> function in <code>mcp_text_editor/text_editor.py</code> accepts a caller-supplied <code>file_path</code> argument and does not normalize it before deciding whether the target sits inside the allowed directory, so a <code>../</code>-laden path walks past the intended sandbox and back to any file the server process can reach. Versions 1.0.0, 1.0.1, and 1.0.2 are affected. The disclosure is a 5.3 Medium under CVSS 4.0 and requires that the operator invoke the vulnerable tool with attacker-influenced input, which for an MCP server is what the whole product does. The reporter filed an issue on the project&#39;s GitHub before publication; the maintainer closed it without a comment, a patch, or a release.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The function is literally named <code>_validate_file_path</code>. It is the one place in the codebase whose entire purpose is to answer the question the CVE is about. The maintainer&#39;s response to the report was to close the issue, which is a defensible position for a hobby project and an alarming one for something the MCP registry lists as an available tool.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any prompt or tool response that reaches the agent and coaxes it to open `../../etc/passwd`, `~/.ssh/id_ed25519`, or any other file readable by the server process gets that file&#39;s contents, past whatever directory scoping the operator thought they had configured. The write path has the same missing check, so a poisoned prompt can also overwrite files outside the intended workspace.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-15138" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-15138, Path Traversal in tumf mcp-text-editor</a><a href="https://github.com/tumf/mcp-text-editor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub: tumf/mcp-text-editor</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-28" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design advisory ai-supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> SPELLSMITH Study Puts Numbers on Taint-Style Bugs Being the Median MCP Server Vulnerability <a href="#incident-28" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">A team from Tongji University posted <code>Mitigating Taint-Style Vulnerabilities in MCP Servers via Security-Aware Tool Descriptions</code> on arXiv on July 8, 2026 (2607.07461). The paper catalogs the vulnerability landscape of published MCP servers, finds that taint-style flaws (SSRF, path traversal, SQL injection, command injection, and their cousins) account for a substantial fraction of reported issues, and reports that these bugs require significant code changes to remediate and are met with slow or absent community responses. The authors propose SPELLSMITH, a defense that packages security guidance for the model into the tool description itself so the agent is nudged away from feeding tainted input into the tool in the first place. The paper is one of a small cluster of academic papers this quarter that back the informal observation that MCP servers keep shipping the same bug classes into the CVE database.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Every week the CVE database picks up another MCP server with an unnormalized path in its file API or an unvalidated URL in its HTTP fetcher, and every week the SPELLSMITH numbers get more defensible. Naming a mitigation <code>SPELLSMITH</code> and shipping it as <em>a prompt for the agent</em> is a very 2026 answer to the question of who is responsible for input sanitization in a protocol whose entire pitch was that the LLM does the plumbing.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>The paper does not disclose a new vulnerability. It puts numbers on a claim that has been anecdotal for the last six months: the median public MCP server ships with at least one taint-style bug, and the median maintainer has not shipped a fix.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07461" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv 2607.07461: Mitigating Taint-Style Vulnerabilities in MCP Servers via Security-Aware Tool Descriptions</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-40" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization denial-of-service protocol-design local-network"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-63118, CVE-2026-63119, CVE-2026-67430, CVE-2026-67431, CVE-2026-67432</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCP Ruby SDK Ships Five Advisories in One Day, Including Session Poisoning the Spec Warned About <a href="#incident-40" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The <code>modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk</code> repo published five advisories against the <code>mcp</code> gem on July 8, 2026, one day after 0.23.0 shipped the fixes. Every one of them affects 0.22.0 and earlier. CVE-2026-67431 is session poisoning: the Streamable and SSE HTTP transports never verify session ownership, so anyone holding a stolen session ID can POST tool calls to <code>/messages/{session-id}</code>, and the server executes them and streams the results back down the victim&#39;s SSE connection where they read as the victim&#39;s own. CVE-2026-67432 reads the full HTTP body into memory with no upper bound, and does it before session validation; the reporter&#39;s 512 MB request took RSS from 44 MB to 1.66 GB. CVE-2026-63118 skips <code>Host</code> and <code>Origin</code> validation, so any page a developer visits can reach a loopback MCP server by DNS rebinding and call its tools. CVE-2026-63119 and CVE-2026-67430 finish the set with unbounded <code>IO#gets</code> on the stdio transports and sessions that never expire, 50,000 of which fit in 27 seconds.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/idontwanttobearound.png" alt="publishing advisory four of five against your own sdk in a single afternoon" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>publishing advisory four of five against your own sdk in a single afternoon</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The session poisoning advisory says the quiet part itself: the MCP spec recommends binding session IDs to user identity, and the C# and Go SDKs already do it. Ruby shipped 22 minor versions without it, then fixed five things in one afternoon.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker with a session ID runs tools as the victim, and the victim reads the output as legitimate. Separately, one unauthenticated POST takes down a Ruby MCP server, and any web page can drive a developer&#39;s localhost server through their browser.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5p9g-j988-pcwv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-5p9g-j988-pcwv: Ruby SSE Session Poisoning</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-h669-8m4g-r2hc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-h669-8m4g-r2hc: Unbounded JSON-RPC request body causes uncontrolled memory allocation</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rjr6-rcgv-9m7m" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-rjr6-rcgv-9m7m: Streamable HTTP transport lacks DNS-rebinding protection</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-7683-3w9x-ch42" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-7683-3w9x-ch42: Unbounded line buffer in stdio transports leads to memory exhaustion</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ruby-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-52jp-gj8w-j6xh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-52jp-gj8w-j6xh: Unbounded session retention allows memory exhaustion via initialize flood</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 06</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-27" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization data-leak aws"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-14471</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AWS mcp-gateway-registry Metrics Service Interpolates table_name Straight Into SQL <a href="#incident-27" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-052-AWS landed on July 6, 2026 for CVE-2026-14471, an authenticated SQL injection in <code>mcp-gateway-registry</code>, the open-source gateway and registry that agentic-community publishes for centralizing MCP servers behind OAuth. The metrics-service retention policy management component takes a caller-supplied <code>table_name</code> value and interpolates it into SQL statements in identifier position with no neutralization. An authenticated remote user submits a crafted <code>table_name</code> and executes arbitrary SQL against the metrics database, which the bulletin notes stores API key material alongside the metrics themselves. CVSS 3.1 comes in at 8.1 and CVSS 4.0 at 8.6, both High. The fix ships as version 1.0.13; there is no workaround.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/clippy.PNG" alt="it looks like you're trying to interpolate `table_name` into a SQL identifier. want me to skip the escaping?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>it looks like you&#39;re trying to interpolate `table_name` into a SQL identifier. want me to skip the escaping?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The registry is the piece of the stack the vendor asked customers to trust so they would not have to trust the individual MCP servers behind it. The metrics service inside that registry took a <code>table_name</code> string from a request and concatenated it into a SQL identifier. <code>psycopg2.sql.Identifier</code> has existed since 2017. So has the practice of not doing this.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An authenticated caller with reach to the metrics-service endpoint reads every row the metrics database holds, including the stored API keys the registry uses to broker calls to downstream MCP servers, then deletes or alters records at will. The registry&#39;s whole reason to exist is being the trusted broker in front of a fleet of MCP servers; the SQL injection turns that broker into a credential dump.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-052-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-052: CVE-2026-14471, Authenticated SQL Injection in mcp-gateway-registry Metrics Service</a><a href="https://github.com/agentic-community/mcp-gateway-registry" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub: agentic-community/mcp-gateway-registry</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 05</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-26" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata authorization"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-14748</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> AIAnytime Awesome-MCP-Server wiki-summary Hands the URL Argument to requests, SSRF to Anywhere <a href="#incident-26" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-14748 published on July 5, 2026 against <code>AIAnytime/Awesome-MCP-Server</code>, whose <code>mcp-wiki</code> subproject ships an MCP tool that summarizes wiki pages for the connected agent. The <code>wiki-summary</code> handler in <code>mcp-wiki/src/mcp_wiki/server.py</code> takes a caller-supplied <code>url</code> argument and passes it into the outbound HTTP client with no scheme allow-list, no host allow-list, and no block for link-local ranges. A prompt-injected wiki summary request to <code>http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/</code> returns the instance profile credentials for whichever cloud account the agent happens to be running in. The disclosure is Medium at CVSS 6.3 and the exploit has been published. The reporter filed the issue with the project ahead of publication and, per the CVE record, the project has not responded.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">This is the sixth or seventh time in 2026 that an MCP tool with <code>url</code> in its argument list has turned out to have neither scheme nor host filtering. Every wiki-summary MCP server on the planet reads the same tutorial before it ships, and the tutorial does not have the <code>169.254.169.254</code> line in it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An MCP server whose only intended reach was the public Wikipedia HTTP endpoint becomes an unauthenticated outbound fetcher for any URL an attacker can convince the agent to pass in. On a cloud host that is enough to reach the instance metadata service, cluster DNS, and internal admin endpoints the agent&#39;s network position happens to include.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-14748" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-14748, SSRF in AIAnytime Awesome-MCP-Server mcp-wiki/wiki-summary</a><a href="https://github.com/AIAnytime/Awesome-MCP-Server" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub: AIAnytime/Awesome-MCP-Server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 03</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-147" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authorization cross-tenant data-leak filesystem advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-77366, CVE-2026-77363, CVE-2026-77364, CVE-2026-77361, CVE-2026-77362</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> obsidian-tc Publishes Five Advisories and the First One Is Any Token, Any Vault <a href="#incident-147" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The-40-Thieves published five advisories on July 3, 2026 against obsidian-tc, an access-controlled MCP server for Obsidian vaults, all fixed in 1.3.1. CVE-2026-77366 (GHSA-84c9-qqpq-2pq5, 9.9) is the structural one: tool dispatch never compares the caller-supplied <code>input.vault</code> against the vault bound to the caller, the JSON Web Token carries no vault claim, and the folder ACL is shared across vaults, so any token reaches any configured vault. CVE-2026-77363 (GHSA-pqh8-4pj7-32xx, 8.8) is a Streamable HTTP transport that validates neither Host nor Origin and enables no DNS-rebinding protection, which the project&#39;s own documented loopback setup pairs with <code>auth.mode</code> set to none. CVE-2026-77364 (GHSA-cvp8-vvgf-69q4, 6.5) is undefined <code>readPaths</code> and <code>writePaths</code> allowing every path, <code>strictReadDefault</code> not covering direct requests, and <code>.obsidian</code>, <code>.git</code> and <code>.trash</code> being excluded only during traversal. CVE-2026-77361 (GHSA-c5xx-h28h-f682, 4.7) checks the read ACL on a lexical path and containment on the resolved one, so an in-vault symlink under an allowed glob lands in a denied folder. CVE-2026-77362 (GHSA-43r2-c7g8-prc8, 4.3) returns denied-note data through fields nobody filtered: group counts in <code>tasks_filter</code>, unfiltered queries in <code>makemd_query</code>.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/trophy-case.jpg" alt="empty display case: where the vault claim goes in the token" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>empty display case: where the vault claim goes in the token</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The project calls itself governed and agent-ready, and the governance layer is the part that broke in all five: an ACL shared across the tenants it exists to separate, a default that allows everything, and two path checks that disagree about which path they are checking.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One token reads and writes every vault the server is configured with. A page the user visits reaches the loopback transport. The default ACL allows every path, including the `.obsidian` directory where plugin secrets are stored.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-84c9-qqpq-2pq5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-84c9-qqpq-2pq5: Cross-vault authorization bypass via input.vault lets any token access any configured vault (CVE-2026-77366)</a><a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-pqh8-4pj7-32xx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-pqh8-4pj7-32xx: HTTP transport lacks Origin/DNS-rebinding protection, enabling drive-by vault access on loopback (CVE-2026-77363)</a><a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-cvp8-vvgf-69q4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-cvp8-vvgf-69q4: Default folder ACL is fail-open; .obsidian plugin secrets are readable by default (CVE-2026-77364)</a><a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-c5xx-h28h-f682" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c5xx-h28h-f682: Symlink read-ACL scope bypass via lexical path checks (CVE-2026-77361)</a><a href="https://github.com/The-40-Thieves/obsidian-tc/security/advisories/GHSA-43r2-c7g8-prc8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-43r2-c7g8-prc8: Bridge tools leak denied-note data via unfiltered sibling fields (CVE-2026-77362)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · july · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-25" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization data-leak"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-52830</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> fast-mcp-telegram Uses Bearer Tokens as Session-File Paths; Path Traversal Grabs the Default Account <a href="#incident-25" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CVE-2026-52830 (CVSS 9.4) landed in the NVD on July 2, 2026 against <code>fast-mcp-telegram</code>, an MCP server that bridges HTTP requests to Telegram&#39;s MTProto API and supports multi-user Bearer-token authentication. The server authenticates incoming HTTP clients by joining the raw Bearer-token string into a session-file path and checking whether that file exists on disk. The verifier explicitly rejects the reserved literal <code>telegram</code> so HTTP callers cannot select the stdio/legacy default session, but it does not reject <code>..</code> and does not normalize the path before the existence check. A remote HTTP client that sends <code>Authorization: Bearer ../fast-mcp-telegram/telegram</code> walks the traversal back to the documented default session file at <code>~/.config/fast-mcp-telegram/telegram.session</code>, and the server hands the caller that session. With account-prefixed MCP tools enabled, the prefix middleware still exposes tools for the default account, so the intended isolation between the HTTP transport and the legacy default account collapses. Every version prior to 0.19.1 is vulnerable.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/werealltryingtofindtheguywhodidthis.jpg" alt="the reviewer who approved `session_path = join(dir, bearer_token)` as the entire auth layer" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the reviewer who approved `session_path = join(dir, bearer_token)` as the entire auth layer</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body"><code>os.path.join(sessions_dir, bearer_token)</code> is written down somewhere in this repository as an authentication decision. The reserved-name check knew <code>telegram</code> was the string to worry about. The path traversal reached the string <code>telegram</code> anyway, because the string was also a filename. The bug is not the missing normalization; the bug is that the token was a path.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated remote HTTP client with network reach to a `fast-mcp-telegram` HTTP endpoint impersonates the default Telegram account any time that account&#39;s session file lives at the documented default path. From there the caller drives every account-prefixed MCP tool as the default user, which for a Telegram bridge covers reading and sending messages, listing chats, and shipping attachments.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-52830" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-52830, fast-mcp-telegram Bearer Token Path Traversal</a><a href="https://advisories.gitlab.com/pypi/fast-mcp-telegram/CVE-2026-52830/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitLab Advisory Database: CVE-2026-52830, fast-mcp-telegram Bearer Token Path Traversal Bypasses Reserved Telegram Session Protection</a><a href="https://github.com/leshchenko1979/fast-mcp-telegram" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub: leshchenko1979/fast-mcp-telegram</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 30</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-24" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection advisory protocol-design ai-supply-chain llm-manipulation"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Microsoft Incident Response: Enterprise Copilot Agents Trust Poisoned MCP Tool Descriptions <a href="#incident-24" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Microsoft&#39;s Detection and Response Team, the incident-response arm inside Microsoft Security, published guidance on June 30, 2026 flagging tool-description poisoning as an active attack path against enterprise agents built on Microsoft 365 Copilot, Copilot Studio, and Azure AI Foundry. Every MCP tool ships a plain-text description that tells the agent what the tool does and when to invoke it, and the guidance notes that the description lives in the agent&#39;s working memory next to its real orders. Third-party MCP servers can change that description at runtime and, in default enterprise configurations, the poisoned version becomes active without a new consent prompt. Microsoft&#39;s worked scenario has a finance team wiring a vendor-enrichment MCP server into a Copilot Studio invoice agent. The tool&#39;s visible name and summary stay unchanged, while the description grows a formatting-note-shaped instruction to attach the last thirty unpaid invoices to the next call. The next routine supplier lookup ships those invoices to whatever endpoint the tool&#39;s HTTP client points at. Microsoft&#39;s mitigation stack is a tenant-level MCP publisher allowlist (disabling <code>Allow all</code>), Prompt Shields inspection of tool metadata and responses, Purview DLP on tool parameters, human approval on high-impact actions, Entra Agent ID plus Conditional Access on agent identities, and Sentinel correlation between agent behavior and MCP telemetry.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Invariant Labs coined <em>tool poisoning</em> in April 2025. Fifteen months later Microsoft Incident Response is walking Copilot Studio customers through a worked example of it, with a mitigation checklist that reads <code>assume MCP publishers are hostile</code>. The protocol still ships the same trust boundary the coining paper described, which is to say it does not really ship one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An approved third-party MCP tool can be turned into an exfiltration channel by rewriting its description, and every enterprise Copilot tenant that leaves the default `Allow all` MCP configuration on picks up the poisoned version without any re-approval. The advisory applies to Microsoft 365 Copilot, Copilot Studio, and Azure AI Foundry agents that can send email, create files, change calendars, query business systems, or run multi-step workflows.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/06/30/securing-ai-agents-ai-tools-move-from-reading-acting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Security Blog: Securing AI Agents, When AI Tools Move From Reading to Acting</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/microsoft-warns-poisoned-mcp-tool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Microsoft Warns Poisoned MCP Tool Descriptions Can Make AI Agents Leak Data</a><a href="https://www.techrepublic.com/article/news-microsoft-mcp-tool-risk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TechRepublic: Microsoft Flags MCP Tool Descriptions as Hidden AI Agent Attack Path</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-111" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication credential-theft denial-of-service ssrf advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-63127, CVE-2026-63128</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> The Official MCP Rust SDK Published Three Advisories in One Day, and NVD Caught Up in September <a href="#incident-111" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">On June 29, 2026 the <code>modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk</code> maintainers published three advisories against the <code>rmcp</code> crate. CVE-2026-63127 (GHSA-33f5-2c5q-wgwj, 8.2) is the OAuth one: <code>ResourceServerMetadata</code> carries no <code>resource</code> field at all, so <code>discover_oauth_server_via_resource_metadata</code> never runs the RFC 9728 check that the resource identifier matches what was requested. A server at <code>fake-mcp.com/mcp</code> can name a real server&#39;s authorization server as its own and collect tokens minted for the real one. Jian Cui, Minsun Shim, Zhou Li and Xiaojing Liao of UIUC and UC Irvine reported it. CVE-2026-63128 (GHSA-9pj6-vhgr-3mwh, 7.5), from hewei-gikaku, has the Streamable HTTP server create a session entry before validating the request body and never release it when validation fails, which the advisory measures at roughly 2,000 malformed requests a second and about 75 GB of resident memory a day. GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9, which has no CVE, has the OAuth client fetch a server-supplied <code>resource_metadata</code> URL out of <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> with no same-origin or private-network check. All three are fixed in 2.0.0 and a fourth advisory followed on July 2. NVD published the two CVEs on September 16, seventy-nine days after the repository did.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/waiting-skeleton.jpg" alt="skeleton on the bench: published June 29, indexed by NVD September 16" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>skeleton on the bench: published June 29, indexed by NVD September 16</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Audience validation is the part of OAuth that stops one server spending another server&#39;s tokens, and the struct that was supposed to carry the audience did not have the field. The September timestamps are the other half of the story: three months of anyone watching CVE feeds for SDK bugs seeing nothing at all.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A malicious MCP server collects access tokens issued for a different server and impersonates the user against it, or drives the client into fetching loopback and RFC 1918 addresses from inside its network. Separately, an unauthenticated caller exhausts a Streamable HTTP server&#39;s memory.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-33f5-2c5q-wgwj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-33f5-2c5q-wgwj: missing resource field validation in OAuth protected resource metadata discovery (CVE-2026-63127)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-9pj6-vhgr-3mwh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9pj6-vhgr-3mwh: unauthenticated permanent session-table leak in the rmcp Streamable HTTP server transport (CVE-2026-63128)</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c9xm-49cp-xcr9: rmcp OAuth client fetches server-controlled resource_metadata URLs</a><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk/security/advisories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">modelcontextprotocol/rust-sdk security advisories, with repository publication dates</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="denial-of-service" aria-pressed="false">denial-of-service</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 29</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-23" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="exploited-in-the-wild credential-theft ai-supply-chain data-exfiltration authentication"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-48558</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Djinn Stealer Adds ~/.claude/mcp.json to Its Loot List After SimpleHelp Auth Bypass <a href="#incident-23" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Blackpoint Cyber&#39;s Adversary Pursuit Group published an intrusion investigation on June 29, 2026 that started with CVE-2026-48558, a critical (CVSS 10.0) authentication bypass in SimpleHelp RMM&#39;s OpenID Connect flow originally disclosed by Horizon3.ai on June 12. The server accepted OIDC identity tokens without verifying their cryptographic signature, so an unauthenticated attacker submitted a forged token with arbitrary claims and got a fully authenticated technician session on an internet-facing SimpleHelp install. From that foothold the operator dropped TaskWeaver, a heavily obfuscated Node.js loader that runs as <code>jquery.js</code> under <code>node.exe</code>, then used TaskWeaver&#39;s encrypted channel to deliver a second previously undocumented family, Djinn Stealer. Djinn ships collection rules for Windows, macOS, and Linux and, alongside the standard AWS/Azure/GCP/Oracle/Okta/Cloudflare/Vault/Terraform/browser/crypto-wallet sweep, has a dedicated section for AI-assisted development tools. It reads configuration, session, and auth material from Anthropic Claude, Google Gemini, and OpenAI Codex, plus open-source coding agents Cline, OpenCode, and Kilo, and it explicitly walks paths like <code>~/.claude/mcp.json</code> where MCP server URLs and tokens live. CISA added CVE-2026-48558 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog the same day Blackpoint published, with a BOD 26-04 remediation deadline of July 7 for federal civilian agencies.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/theyre-the-same-picture.png" alt="commodity infostealer collection rules, 2024 | commodity infostealer collection rules, 2026 (now with ~/.claude/mcp.json)" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>commodity infostealer collection rules, 2024 | commodity infostealer collection rules, 2026 (now with ~/.claude/mcp.json)</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The moment a commodity infostealer&#39;s collection ruleset lists <code>~/.claude/mcp.json</code> next to <code>.env</code> files and browser cookies is the moment MCP configuration is officially loot. Djinn also enumerates Cline, OpenCode, Kilo, Gemini, and Codex, so the operators did the market research. The <code>.claude</code> directory is now a threat model of its own.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any organization exposing an unpatched SimpleHelp RMM to the internet could have its technician session hijacked with a forged OIDC token, then have a developer machine&#39;s MCP configuration harvested along with the usual cloud, source-control, and wallet material. Because MCP configs carry the tokens the developer&#39;s own agent uses, the stolen material grants the attacker the same downstream reach the agent already had into repos, databases, cloud accounts, and internal APIs.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://blackpointcyber.com/blog/a-djinn-in-the-machine-taskweavers-node-js-intrusion-chain/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Blackpoint Cyber APG: A Djinn in the Machine, TaskWeaver&#39;s Node.js Intrusion Chain</a><a href="https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/06/30/simplehelp-vulnerability-exploited-cve-2026-48558/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Help Net Security: SimpleHelp vulnerability exploited to deliver mighty Djinn Stealer (CVE-2026-48558)</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/attackers-exploit-simplehelp-cve-2026.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Attackers Exploit SimpleHelp CVE-2026-48558 to Deploy TaskWeaver and Djinn Stealer</a><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2026/06/29/cisa-adds-one-known-exploited-vulnerability-catalog" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CISA: Adds One Known Exploited Vulnerability to Catalog</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-22" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="supply-chain credential-theft aws ai-supply-chain authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-12957, CVE-2026-12958</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Amazon Q for VS Code Auto-Loads .amazonq/mcp.json From Any Cloned Repo <a href="#incident-22" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Wiz Research&#39;s Maor Dokhanian disclosed that the Amazon Q Developer extension for Visual Studio Code reads <code>.amazonq/mcp.json</code> from any opened workspace and spawns the MCP servers it defines without checking workspace trust, surfacing a prompt, or even logging the action. The spawned processes inherit the developer&#39;s full environment, so anything the shell carries, including AWS access keys, cloud CLI tokens, SSH agent sockets, and API secrets, is handed directly to whatever command the config pointed at. Wiz reported the issue to AWS on April 20, 2026; AWS deployed an initial fix on May 12 and publicly disclosed on June 26 under Security Bulletin 2026-047-AWS as CVE-2026-12957 (CVSS 8.5). The same bulletin tracks a second flaw, CVE-2026-12958, a missing symlink check in Language Servers for AWS that lets a maliciously crafted symlink inside an opened workspace point at a target outside the workspace trust boundary, enabling arbitrary file writes. Both are remediated in Language Servers for AWS 1.65.0; AWS asks customers to upgrade to 1.69.0 for the broader rollup.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imtryingtounderstand.webp" alt="git clone, open in VS Code, lose AWS creds. apparently that's the design" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>git clone, open in VS Code, lose AWS creds. apparently that&#39;s the design</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Workspace trust exists in VS Code specifically so that <code>git clone &amp;&amp; code .</code> is not also <code>chmod +x ./*.sh &amp;&amp; ./run-all</code>. Amazon Q&#39;s MCP loader read past that signal because the config file lived under <code>.amazonq/</code> and was therefore, by assumption, friendly. The same assumption produced the symlink bug. The fix list is the assumption list.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone who clones a repository, opens it in VS Code with Amazon Q installed, and is connected to their cloud session executes whatever the repo&#39;s `.amazonq/mcp.json` told MCP to run, attached to their live AWS credentials, with no further interaction. The chained symlink bug bumps the same workspace-open into arbitrary file writes outside the trust boundary.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/amazon-q-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wiz Research: Amazon Q Vulnerability, Compromise via MCP Auto-Execution</a><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/2026-047-aws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS Security Bulletin 2026-047: Issues in Language Servers for AWS and Amazon Q Developer Plugins</a><a href="https://www.theregister.com/cyber-crime/2026/06/26/amazon-q-flaw-let-booby-trapped-git-repos-execute-code-swipe-cloud-creds/5263202" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Register: Amazon Q flaw let booby-trapped Git repos execute code, swipe cloud creds</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/amazon-q-developer-flaw-could-let.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Amazon Q Developer Flaw Could Let Malicious Repos Run Code via MCP Configs</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 25</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-64" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata advisory"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-19751, CVE-2026-19752, CVE-2026-19753</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Three MCP Servers Fetch Whatever URL You Name, Reported in June and Given CVEs in August <a href="#incident-64" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">TianYu-0829 opened public GitHub issues on June 25, 2026 against three MCP servers, all the same shape. The <code>parse-csv</code> tool in <code>mcp-dominican-layer</code> validates <code>csvUrl</code> with <code>z.string().url()</code> and passes it to <code>axios.get()</code> (CVE-2026-19751). Its <code>parse-pdf</code> tool does the same with <code>pdfUrl</code> (CVE-2026-19752). The <code>explore_url</code> tool in <code>mcp-rdf-explorer</code> hands <code>url</code> to <code>requests.get()</code> in <code>server.py</code> (CVE-2026-19753). None of the three restrict scheme, hostname, resolved IP range, port, redirects, or timeouts. Each proof of concept drives the server at a local HTTP listener through the MCP Inspector. VulDB filed the CVEs on August 13, seven weeks after the issues went public.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Zod confirmed the string was a URL. That is the entire check, and it is exactly the check that a URL-fetching SSRF is built to pass.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A tool argument reaches an outbound HTTP client with no destination policy, so the server fetches internal services and cloud metadata endpoints on the caller&#39;s behalf and returns what it gets.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/EnzoVezzaro/mcp-dominican-layer/issues/2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-dominican-layer issue #2: SSRF in the parse-csv tool (CVE-2026-19751)</a><a href="https://github.com/EnzoVezzaro/mcp-dominican-layer/issues/3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-dominican-layer issue #3: SSRF in the parse-pdf tool (CVE-2026-19752)</a><a href="https://github.com/emekaokoye/mcp-rdf-explorer/issues/3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mcp-rdf-explorer issue #3: SSRF in the explore_url tool (CVE-2026-19753)</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19751" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19751</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19752" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19752</a><a href="https://vuldb.com/cve/CVE-2026-19753" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VulDB CVE-2026-19753</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 23</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-21" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization data-leak privilege-escalation"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-12112, CVE-2026-9073</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Red Hat Satellite foreman-mcp-server Treats Session IDs as Auth, Logs Them <a href="#incident-21" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Red Hat published advisories for two issues in foreman-mcp-server, the Technology Preview MCP server bundled with Satellite 6.18. CVE-2026-12112 (CVSS 7.8, Important) is the session-management bug. The server caches authenticated client connections and trusts the session ID on subsequent requests without re-validating the underlying authentication tokens, so anyone holding a session ID inherits the active administrative session. CVE-2026-9073 (Moderate) is how that session ID gets obtained. The server writes every newly created session ID to standard logs at the informational level, and when debug logging is enabled, also persists HTTP authorization headers in cleartext. Both advisories landed on June 23, 2026, without a fixed-version pointer at publication. Red Hat&#39;s interim mitigation is to restrict access to foreman-mcp-server, scrub log forwarding, and watch for suspicious session activity.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A session ID treated as a bearer token is a design choice. A session ID treated as a bearer token <em>and</em> logged at INFO is the kind of thing that fails its first compliance audit. Both behaviors live in the same component, shipped under the Technology Preview label, which is the industry-standard phrasing for <code>please discover the bugs for us</code>.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone with read access to Satellite&#39;s container logs, or to any sink those logs were forwarded to, harvests session IDs that are themselves the bearer credential. Replaying them produces hijacked administrative sessions on a Satellite instance and, through it, infrastructure-wide code execution against the hosts it manages.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/cve-2026-12112" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Red Hat Security: CVE-2026-12112, Improper Authentication in foreman-mcp-server</a><a href="https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/cve-2026-9073" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Red Hat Security: CVE-2026-9073, Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File in foreman-mcp-server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 18</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-50" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce supply-chain command-injection"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-66065</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Ouroboros Rebuilds Its .env Denylist After the First One Left the MCP Config Root Reachable <a href="#incident-50" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Ouroboros is a local-first runtime for AI coding agents that records their actions and applies user-defined policies. CVE-2026-47211 was closed by adding <code>_UNTRUSTED_ENV_DENYLIST</code>, which stops a <code>.env</code> in an untrusted project directory from redirecting execution. That list did not cover every key. Through 0.42.0, <code>OUROBOROS_MCP_CONFIG</code> still pointed the runtime at a YAML file whose server <code>command</code> and <code>args</code> are executed through the stdio client, while <code>OUROBOROS_PLUGIN_LOCKFILE</code> and <code>OUROBOROS_PLUGIN_TRUST_ROOT</code> redirected the installed-plugin roster and its trust root. A <code>.env</code> is auto-loaded at import with no review step, so cloning a hostile repo is the entire delivery mechanism. Version 0.42.1 denylisted those keys and separately stopped auto-loading <code>./.ouroboros/mcp_servers.yaml</code> from the working directory, a path that reached the same place with no <code>.env</code> involved at all. CVSS 8.4.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/roll-safe.jpg" alt="can't be redirected by a hostile .env if you denylist most of the variables" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>can&#39;t be redirected by a hostile .env if you denylist most of the variables</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The first fix enumerated dangerous variables and missed the one named after the MCP config file. Underneath it sat a working-directory auto-load that made the <code>.env</code> optional anyway.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Clone a malicious repository and the agent runtime launches the attacker&#39;s MCP servers running the attacker&#39;s commands. The approval gate does not intervene, because the config that defines the gate is the thing being replaced.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/Q00/ouroboros/security/advisories/GHSA-jv2h-4p9v-wf5w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-jv2h-4p9v-wf5w: Ouroboros incomplete untrusted-.env denylist (CVE-2026-66065)</a><a href="https://github.com/Q00/ouroboros/releases/tag/v0.42.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ouroboros v0.42.1 release notes</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-20" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain credential-theft ai-supply-chain exploited-in-the-wild data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Mastra AI npm Scope Hijacked by Sapphire Sleet, 142 MCP Framework Packages Backdoored <a href="#incident-20" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Between 01:12 and 02:39 UTC on June 17, 2026, a single compromised npm account named <code>ehindero</code> republished 142 packages across the <code>@mastra</code> scope, the TypeScript AI agent framework whose <code>@mastra/core</code>, <code>@mastra/mcp</code>, and <code>@mastra/mcp-docs-server</code> packages clear roughly a million weekly downloads between them. The compromised versions were byte-for-byte identical to the legitimate builds; the only change in each manifest was a single injected dependency, <code>easy-day-js</code>, a typosquat of <code>dayjs</code> published one hour earlier under the alias <code>sergey2016</code>. The dependency&#39;s <code>postinstall</code> hook disabled TLS certificate verification, fetched a second-stage payload from attacker infrastructure, executed it as a detached background process, and deleted itself to limit forensic traces. The cross-platform infostealer harvested browser data from Chrome, Edge, and Brave, extracted credentials from 166 cryptocurrency wallet extensions, and swept GitHub tokens, npm tokens, SSH keys, and <code>.env</code> files before exfiltrating to attacker C2. Microsoft Threat Intelligence attributed the activity to Sapphire Sleet (BlueNoroff), a North Korean state actor that has been running fake-recruiter LinkedIn campaigns against open-source maintainers; Mastra confirmed the compromised maintainer was a current employee whose machine was taken over after exactly that kind of contact. Socket flagged the malicious wave within six minutes of publication and Mastra force-published clean releases across all 142 packages.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/idontwanttobearound.png" alt="every AI dev who installed @mastra/core this morning, reading the IOCs over coffee" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>every AI dev who installed @mastra/core this morning, reading the IOCs over coffee</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">One LinkedIn DM took over the entire <code>@mastra</code> npm scope. Time-to-publish-malware was eighty-eight minutes, faster than most incident-response oncall rotations resolve a page. The remediation checklist starts at &quot;rotate every credential a developer machine has ever held&quot; and the list gets longer from there.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone running `npm install` against an `@mastra/*` version in the 88-minute window received a credential harvester running inside their developer or CI/CD environment. The blast radius covered every consumer of the framework&#39;s MCP client and server packages; Mastra advised treating any affected install as fully compromised and rotating every credential the host had touched, including cryptocurrency wallet seeds.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/06/17/postinstall-payload-inside-mastra-npm-supply-chain-compromise/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Security Blog: Preinstall to Postinstall Payload, Inside the Mastra npm Supply Chain Compromise by Sapphire Sleet</a><a href="https://socket.dev/blog/mastra-npm-packages-compromised" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Socket: 140+ Mastra npm Packages Compromised in Coordinated Supply Chain Attack</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/144-mastra-npm-packages-compromised-via.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: 145 Mastra npm Packages Compromised via Hijacked Contributor Account</a><a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/microsoft-links-mastra-ai-supply-chain-attack-to-north-korean-hackers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BleepingComputer: Microsoft Links Mastra AI Supply Chain Attack to North Korean Hackers</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-169" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication local-network web-surface advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-61542, CVE-2026-61546, CVE-2026-62863</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Three Desktop Apps Ship MCP Servers That Trust Whoever Reaches Them <a href="#incident-169" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Three unrelated desktop applications published advisories in June 2026 for the same shape of bug, and all three surfaced in NVD only in late October. Gearboy, a Game Boy emulator project, published GHSA-fmvx-487g-mrww for Gearsystem on June 12, credited to avishaigonen-pluto: its optional <code>--mcp-http</code> mode binds <code>INADDR_ANY</code> on port 7777 and accepts POSTs to <code>/mcp</code> with no <code>Authorization</code>, <code>Host</code> or <code>Origin</code> check, despite documentation and logging that say localhost (CVE-2026-61542). AnkiMCP published GHSA-j9xx-59ph-wmr6 on June 18, from the same reporter with yotampe-pluto: it sets <code>enable_dns_rebinding_protection=False</code> globally, including for the default <code>127.0.0.1:3141</code> listener, so a malicious web page rebinds to loopback and reaches <code>initialize</code>, <code>tools/list</code> and <code>tools/call</code> (CVE-2026-61546). MoLing published GHSA-qpgh-hp5g-4vwp on June 27, credited to Songwu, Zeyu Luo, Dr. CAO Yinfeng and Kevin: in SSE mode its <code>/sse</code> and <code>/message</code> endpoints do not authenticate and the <code>mcp-go</code> dependency sets <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>, so a cross-origin <code>EventSource</code> reads the <code>sessionId</code> out of the <code>endpoint</code> event and uses it to send tool calls to <code>/message</code> (CVE-2026-62863).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imagination-spongebob.jpg" alt="spongebob making a rainbow: localhost, as a security boundary, with a browser on the same machine" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>spongebob making a rainbow: localhost, as a security boundary, with a browser on the same machine</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Binding to loopback stops being a boundary the moment the attacker&#39;s code runs in a browser on that same loopback, which is what undoes AnkiMCP. MoLing never asked who was calling at all. Gearsystem skipped the question, binding every interface while its documentation said localhost. One reporter found two of the three, four months before NVD noticed any of them.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>A peer on the same network calls Gearsystem&#39;s emulator and debugger tools. A web page the user happens to visit does the same to AnkiMCP&#39;s collection tools and to whatever tools MoLing has been configured with.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/drhelius/Gearboy/security/advisories/GHSA-fmvx-487g-mrww" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-fmvx-487g-mrww: Gearsystem MCP HTTP transport binds all interfaces without authentication (CVE-2026-61542)</a><a href="https://github.com/ankimcp/anki-mcp-server-addon/security/advisories/GHSA-j9xx-59ph-wmr6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-j9xx-59ph-wmr6: DNS rebinding reaches unauthenticated local AnkiMCP HTTP tools (CVE-2026-61546)</a><a href="https://github.com/gojue/moling/security/advisories/GHSA-qpgh-hp5g-4vwp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-qpgh-hp5g-4vwp: SSE mode exposes unauthenticated endpoint with CORS wildcard, leaking sessionId (CVE-2026-62863)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="web-surface" aria-pressed="false">web-surface</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-18" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection llm-manipulation ai-supply-chain credential-theft data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Agentjacking Turns Fake Sentry Errors Into AI Coding Agent RCE via MCP <a href="#incident-18" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Tenet Security disclosed <code>agentjacking</code>, an attack class that uses Sentry&#39;s open event-ingestion architecture to plant prompt-injection payloads inside fake bug reports, then waits for an AI coding agent connected to the Sentry MCP server to read them. A Sentry DSN is a write-only credential that every frontend ships in its JavaScript, so finding one is a GitHub search. The injected event&#39;s <code>message</code> field and context keys carry markdown that renders identically to Sentry&#39;s own system template: headings, code blocks, tables. When Claude Code, Cursor, or Codex retrieves the event over MCP as a triage prompt, the agent treats the embedded instructions as legitimate diagnostic steps and executes attacker-controlled commands with the developer&#39;s own privileges. Tenet reported an 85% success rate against the three agents across more than 100 organizations in controlled tests and identified at least 2,388 organizations with injectable DSNs in production. The Cloud Security Alliance AI Safety Initiative published the research as a CSA Research Note on June 12, 2026, with parallel coverage at The Hacker News the same day.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/isthatthejoke.jpg" alt="sentry on the root cause: &#34;technically not defensible&#34;" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>sentry on the root cause: &quot;technically not defensible&quot;</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Sentry&#39;s response was that the issue is <code>technically not defensible</code> at the platform level, so they shipped a content filter for the exact payload string Tenet sent them. The architectural pathway, where any DSN can plant agent-executable commands inside a customer&#39;s triage workflow, is untouched. The acknowledgement and the non-fix arrived the same day.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Anyone with a public Sentry DSN can plant a single error event that exfiltrates AWS keys, GitHub tokens, Sentry auth tokens, git credentials, and private repo URLs from the developer&#39;s machine through the Sentry MCP triage path. Every step in the chain is authorized, so EDR, WAF, IAM, VPN, and Cloudflare see nothing to block.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://tenetsecurity.ai/blog/agentjacking-coding-agents-with-fake-sentry-errors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tenet Security: A Fake Bug Report Hijacks Your AI Coding Agent, and Nothing Catches It</a><a href="https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-agentjacking-mcp-sentry-injection-20260612/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloud Security Alliance: Agentjacking, MCP Injection Hijacks AI Coding Agents</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/agentjacking-attack-tricks-ai-coding.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Agentjacking Attack Tricks AI Coding Agents Into Running Malicious Code</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-45" data-severity="medium" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="filesystem data-exfiltration data-leak"> <div class="sev-bar medium"> <span class="l">[medium]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-54785</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> gemini-bridge Reads Any File You Name, Then Ships It to Google <a href="#incident-45" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">gemini-bridge is a small MCP server that hands AI agents a path to Google&#39;s Gemini through the official CLI. From 1.0.0 until 1.3.1, <code>consult_gemini_with_files</code> in inline mode read any path supplied in the <code>files</code> argument without confining it to the working directory, then forwarded the contents to the Gemini CLI. Because the caller also controls <code>query</code>, the contents come straight back through the Gemini round trip. Fixed in 1.3.1. CVSS 6.2.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The exfiltration channel is the product. A path traversal that ends in a file read is ordinary. This one reads the file, uploads it, and hands you the transcript.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Arbitrary local file read for anything the server process can open, with the contents returned to the caller and a copy sent to Google along the way.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/eLyiN/gemini-bridge/security/advisories/GHSA-c5px-58j2-7fqp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c5px-58j2-7fqp: gemini-bridge arbitrary local file read via consult_gemini_with_files</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-19" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="supply-chain credential-theft ai-supply-chain exploited-in-the-wild data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Shai-Hulud &#39;Hades&#39; Wave Drops Split-Loader Malware on MCP-Themed PyPI Packages <a href="#incident-19" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Socket&#39;s Threat Research team disclosed on June 9, 2026 that the active Mini Shai-Hulud / Miasma / Hades supply-chain campaign had added 23 fresh malicious PyPI artifacts the day prior, five of them aimed directly at developers building MCP integrations: <code>langchain-core-mcp</code>, <code>openai-mcp</code>, <code>instructor-mcp</code>, <code>tiktoken-mcp</code>, and <code>ray-mcp-server</code>. The wheels follow the Hades pattern earlier waves established on npm: a <code>.pth</code> startup hook fires during Python&#39;s site initialization, downloads the Bun JavaScript runtime as a living-off-the-land binary, then runs an obfuscated stealer staged through Bun with a fake prompt-injection header at the top of the payload. The <code>langchain-core-mcp</code> wheel ships only the <code>.pth</code> loader and no bundled <code>_index.js</code>, instead walking every entry in <code>sys.path</code> for the payload at runtime. The split-staging architecture decouples loader from payload so static scanners that audit the wheel they were handed see nothing executable. The June 8 PyPI wave brought the campaign&#39;s cross-ecosystem total to 471 artifacts spanning 411 npm packages and 60 PyPI wheels since June 1, with Socket tracking it pivoting delivery mechanisms every 48 to 72 hours.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yousureaboutthat.png" alt="every MCP integration tutorial that opened with pip install something-mcp" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>every MCP integration tutorial that opened with pip install something-mcp</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The package names are unsubtle on purpose. Anyone typing <code>pip install langchain-core-mcp</code> in the dark is the demographic. The split-staging <code>.pth</code> trick is also on purpose: most scanners look inside the wheel they&#39;re handed, not the rest of <code>sys.path</code>. <em>Defense in depth</em>, attacker-side.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Developers searching PyPI for an MCP integration could land on a wheel that dropped a credential harvester at install time. Targeted material includes GitHub tokens, npm and PyPI publish keys, AWS, GCP, Azure, Kubernetes service-account material, SSH keys, Docker config, shell history, `.env` files, and AI developer tool configuration including `~/.claude.json`. PyPI removed the artifacts after Socket reported them, but any install that ran during the window should be treated as compromised.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://socket.dev/blog/mini-shai-hulud-miasma-and-hades-worms-target-bioinformatics-and-mcp-developers-via-malicious" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Socket: Mini Shai-Hulud, Miasma, and Hades Worms Target Bioinformatics and MCP Developers via Malicious PyPI Packages</a><a href="https://www.darkreading.com/application-security/hades-campaign-pypi-shai-hulud" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dark Reading: &#39;Hades&#39; Attacks on PyPI Put New Spin on Shai-Hulud</a><a href="https://www.scworld.com/news/mini-shai-hulud-hades-variant-affects-23-pypi-package-versions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Media: Mini Shai-Hulud &#39;Hades&#39; Variant Affects 23 PyPI Package Versions</a><a href="https://cybersecuritynews.com/23-pypi-packages-compromised/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cybersecurity News: New Shai-Hulud Attack Compromises 23 PyPI Packages to Target MCP Developers</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 08</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-104" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce exploited-in-the-wild command-injection authorization advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-42271</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> CISA Catalogues LiteLLM&#39;s MCP Preview Endpoints as Exploited in the Wild <a href="#incident-104" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">CISA added CVE-2026-42271 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on June 8, 2026, with a remediation deadline of June 22. It is the first of two LiteLLM MCP endpoints to land there, three months ahead of CVE-2026-59822. jaydns disclosed the flaw in GHSA-v4p8-mg3p-g94g on April 21, 2026: <code>POST /mcp-rest/test/connection</code> and <code>POST /mcp-rest/test/tools/list</code>, the two endpoints that preview an MCP server before you save it, accepted a full server configuration in the request body, including the <code>command</code>, <code>args</code> and <code>env</code> fields the stdio transport uses. Handed a stdio configuration they tried to connect, which spawned the supplied command as a subprocess on the proxy host with the proxy process&#39;s privileges. Both were gated on a valid proxy API key and nothing else, so any authenticated caller, including one holding a low-privilege internal-user key, could run commands. Version 1.83.7 fixed it by requiring the <code>PROXY_ADMIN</code> role. The advisory scores it 8.7 and NVD 8.8. Wiz&#39;s honeypot research recorded exploitation in the wild, with attackers submitting fake MCP configurations whose embedded Python fetched a cryptominer while returning a valid MCP handshake so the connection test appeared to succeed.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/megamind-peeking.png" alt="megamind peering closely: no role check on the endpoint that spawns the process?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>megamind peering closely: no role check on the endpoint that spawns the process?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A preview button that runs the thing it is previewing. The fix was a single role check, so the endpoint was always meant to be privileged and simply never said so. The payload is the detail to sit with: it returned a valid MCP handshake on the way through, so the test the operator clicked reported success.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any LiteLLM caller holding an ordinary API key, down to a low-privilege internal-user key, runs arbitrary commands on the proxy host. Observed in the wild delivering cryptominers through configurations crafted so the connection test still looked successful.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog?field_cve=CVE-2026-42271" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog: CVE-2026-42271, BerriAI LiteLLM command injection</a><a href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/security/advisories/GHSA-v4p8-mg3p-g94g" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-v4p8-mg3p-g94g: LiteLLM MCP server preview endpoints spawn a caller-supplied command (CVE-2026-42271)</a><a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/ai-infrastructure-honeypot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wiz: what 90 days of AI infrastructure honeypots caught</a><a href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/releases/tag/v1.83.7-stable" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LiteLLM v1.83.7-stable release</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · june · 02</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-17" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection supply-chain github credential-theft data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Claude Code GitHub Action Prompt Injection Hijacks Any Downstream Repo <a href="#incident-17" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GMO Flatt Security&#39;s RyotaK and Microsoft Threat Intelligence published parallel research disclosing prompt-injection bypasses in Anthropic&#39;s official <code>claude-code-action</code> GitHub Action. Flatt&#39;s writeup, posted June 2, traced the <code>checkWritePermissions</code> function unconditionally trusting any actor whose login ended in <code>[bot]</code>, which let any GitHub App author crafted issues whose contents Claude then treated as authorized instructions. Microsoft&#39;s June 5 post documented a second path: the agent&#39;s <code>Read</code> tool sat outside the Bubblewrap sandbox that wrapped <code>Bash</code>, so <code>/proc/self/environ</code> was reachable from inside any triage run. Both chains exfiltrated the workflow&#39;s <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, OIDC token, and any other CI secrets via the GitHub MCP server&#39;s <code>update_issue</code> tool, WebFetch, or echoed log output. Anthropic rated the issues 7.8 under CVSS v4.0, shipped fixes across <code>claude-code-action</code> v1.0.94 and Claude Code 2.1.128, and paid a bounty. A variant of the same misconfiguration class was already exploited in February against Cline&#39;s triage workflow to steal an npm publish token and push an unauthorized <code>cline@2.3.0</code>.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/spiderman point.png" alt="checkWritePermissions · Read tool sandbox · GitHub MCP update_issue, lined up at exfil" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>checkWritePermissions · Read tool sandbox · GitHub MCP update_issue, lined up at exfil</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">An auth check that boils down to <code>actor.endsWith(&#39;[bot]&#39;)</code> is roughly the part of an auth system you&#39;d hope <code>--insecure-skip-tls-verify=true</code> was, only less polite. The <code>Bash</code> tool got a Bubblewrap sandbox. The <code>Read</code> tool got a permission check. Whoever signed off on that delta hadn&#39;t yet met an LLM willing to ask <code>Read</code> for <code>/proc/self/environ</code>.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any repository running the action with a `[bot]`-authored triage workflow could be coaxed into leaking its `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, OIDC token, and other workflow secrets, then accepting attacker-authored commits. Because `anthropics/claude-code-action` itself ran the vulnerable workflow, a successful compromise of the action&#39;s own repo would have flowed to every downstream consumer.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://flatt.tech/research/posts/poisoning-claude-code-one-github-issue-to-break-the-supply-chain/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GMO Flatt Security: Poisoning Claude Code, One GitHub Issue to Break the Supply Chain</a><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/06/05/securing-ci-cd-in-agentic-world-claude-code-github-action-case/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Security Blog: Securing CI/CD in an Agentic World, Claude Code GitHub Action Case</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/06/claude-code-github-action-flaw-let-one.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Claude Code GitHub Action Flaw Let One Malicious Issue Hijack Repositories</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 31</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-46" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization protocol-design advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-67333, CVE-2026-67336</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> better-auth&#39;s MCP Plugin Takes a javascript: Redirect URI and Advertises the none Algorithm <a href="#incident-46" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two better-auth advisories landed on May 31, 2026, and both reach MCP deployments through the <code>mcp</code> plugin that wraps the deprecated <code>oidc-provider</code>. In CVE-2026-67333, registered <code>redirect_uris</code> are never scheme-validated, so an attacker registers a client with a <code>javascript:</code> URI and the authorization server hands it back unchanged in the consent response. A consent page that assigns that value to <code>window.location.href</code> executes the attacker&#39;s script in the authorization server&#39;s own origin. Fixed in 1.6.13. CVE-2026-67336 covers the cryptographic defaults in the same two plugins: they advertise the <code>none</code> algorithm and accept plain PKCE, so an attacker can negotiate down to unsigned tokens or intercept authorization codes that S256 would have protected. Fixed in 1.6.11.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body"><code>none</code> has a decade of writeups behind it as the JWT footgun. Shipping it as an advertised default in 2026, in the plugin that fronts MCP auth, takes some doing.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Script execution in the authorization server origin, which means the victim&#39;s session and account takeover. Separately, tokens with no signature accepted by a server whose job is checking signatures.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/security/advisories/GHSA-86j7-9j95-vpqj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-86j7-9j95-vpqj: better-auth stored XSS via javascript: redirect_uri</a><a href="https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/security/advisories/GHSA-9h47-pqcx-hjr4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9h47-pqcx-hjr4: better-auth insecure cryptographic defaults in oidcProvider and mcp plugins</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 28</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-63" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication authorization data-leak data-exfiltration"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-50027</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-memory-service Guards /api/memories and Serves /api/documents to Anyone <a href="#incident-63" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-84hp-mqvj-3p8h, published May 28, 2026, covers <code>mcp-memory-service</code> before 10.67.1. Every HTTP route under <code>/api/documents/*</code> is served with no authentication dependency, whether the server is configured with an <code>MCP_API_KEY</code> or with OAuth. The <code>documents.py</code> router is constructed without a <code>dependencies=</code> argument, and the file never imports <code>Depends</code>. Six endpoints are exposed this way: upload, batch upload, history, content search, and two deletion routes. The neighbouring <code>/api/memories</code> router applies <code>Depends(require_write_access)</code> correctly, so the guard exists in the codebase and was not wired into this file. CVSS 9.8.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/hide-the-pain-harold.jpg" alt="the MCP_API_KEY you configured, protecting one of the two routers" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the MCP_API_KEY you configured, protecting one of the two routers</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Setting <code>MCP_API_KEY</code> produces a server that looks authenticated, and is, on the router where somebody remembered to say so.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An unauthenticated remote caller uploads content into the memory store, reads stored documents back out, and deletes them. The API key the operator configured has no effect on any of those routes.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-84hp-mqvj-3p8h" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-84hp-mqvj-3p8h: mcp-memory-service document API served without authentication</a><a href="https://pypi.org/project/mcp-memory-service" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PyPI: mcp-memory-service</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 27</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-61" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce supply-chain authorization filesystem"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-49986</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Cortex Trusts CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, So Any Cloned Repo Can Claim to Be the Cortex Install <a href="#incident-61" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">GHSA-gvpp-v77h-5w8g, published May 27, 2026, covers <code>neuro-cortex-memory</code> 3.17.0 and earlier. Claude Code sets <code>CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR</code> to whatever project the user has open, and Cortex&#39;s <code>_find_dev_source()</code> treats that path as a trusted developer checkout of Cortex itself. The check that decides, <code>_is_cortex_root()</code>, looks for an <code>mcp_server/</code> subdirectory and a <code>ui/unified-viz.html</code> file. When <code>open_visualization</code> is invoked, the handler builds a bootstrap path under that directory and runs it with <code>subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(bootstrap_path)])</code>. A second path in <code>http_launcher.py</code> rsyncs the same untrusted source into the Cortex plugin cache. CVSS 7.8, fixed in 3.17.1.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">One directory and one HTML file decide whether a path is your own source checkout. That is not a high bar for a repository whose entire purpose is to be cloned onto your machine.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Opening a cloned repository in Claude Code and invoking the visualization tool executes that repository&#39;s Python with the developer&#39;s privileges.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/cdeust/Cortex/security/advisories/GHSA-gvpp-v77h-5w8g" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-gvpp-v77h-5w8g: Cortex MCP server trusts CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR as a developer checkout</a><a href="https://github.com/cdeust/Cortex/releases/tag/v3.17.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cortex release v3.17.1</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 27</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-62" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf local-network authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-49857, CVE-2026-49856</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> auth-fetch-mcp Blocks ::ffff:127.0.0.1 and Node Hands It Back ::ffff:7f00:1 <a href="#incident-62" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Two SSRF-guard bypasses published May 27, 2026. In <code>auth-fetch-mcp</code> 3.0.1 and earlier, <code>assertSafeUrl()</code> in <code>src/security.ts</code> blocks private and loopback addresses, and <code>isPrivateV6()</code> handles the IPv4-mapped form by stripping the <code>::ffff:</code> prefix and calling <code>net.isIPv4()</code> on the remainder. Node&#39;s WHATWG URL parser hex-normalizes <code>[::ffff:127.0.0.1]</code> to <code>[::ffff:7f00:1]</code> before that check runs, so the remainder is <code>7f00:1</code>, <code>net.isIPv4()</code> returns false, and the address is classified as public. The <code>auth_fetch</code> and <code>download_media</code> tools then reach <code>127.0.0.1</code> (CVE-2026-49857, CVSS 7.4, fixed in 3.0.2). The same day, <code>@jshookmcp/jshook</code> 0.3.1 and earlier shipped a central SSRF authorization policy that its HTTP, TCP, and TLS RTT tools enforce through <code>resolveAuthorizedTransportTarget</code>, and that <code>network_icmp_probe</code> and <code>network_traceroute</code> skip entirely, calling native probes straight after hostname resolution (CVE-2026-49856, CVSS 4.3, fixed in 0.3.2).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/surprised-pikachu.jpg" alt="the loopback check, meeting the second spelling of loopback" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the loopback check, meeting the second spelling of loopback</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">One guard was beaten by the URL parser running ahead of it. The other was simply not called by two of the tools it governs. Same lesson twice about where a check has to sit.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>auth-fetch-mcp returns responses from loopback services its guard was written to block. jshook maps internal addresses, reachability, latency, and routes from the server&#39;s network position even when private-network access is disabled globally.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/ymw0407/auth-fetch-mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-pvrj-8cg3-j5f8" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-pvrj-8cg3-j5f8: auth-fetch-mcp SSRF bypass via IPv4-mapped IPv6 loopback (CVE-2026-49857)</a><a href="https://github.com/vmoranv/jshookmcp/security/advisories/GHSA-c5r6-m4mr-8q5j" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-c5r6-m4mr-8q5j: jshookmcp ICMP probe and traceroute skip the SSRF policy (CVE-2026-49856)</a><a href="https://github.com/ymw0407/auth-fetch-mcp/releases/tag/v3.0.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">auth-fetch-mcp release v3.0.2, the fix release for GHSA-pvrj-8cg3-j5f8</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-16" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="prompt-injection authorization privilege-escalation command-injection"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-46519, CVE-2026-47250</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-server-kubernetes Ships Two Access Control Bypasses in Two Weeks <a href="#incident-16" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Flux159&#39;s <code>mcp-server-kubernetes</code> shipped two access-control failures disclosed two weeks apart in late May and early June 2026. CVE-2026-46519 (CVSS 8.8), published May 21, found that the <code>ALLOWED_TOOLS</code>, <code>ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS</code>, and <code>ALLOW_ONLY_NON_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS</code> environment variables were enforced only inside the <code>tools/list</code> handler. The <code>tools/call</code> handler had none of those checks, so any client that already knew a tool name could invoke <code>kubectl_delete</code>, <code>exec_in_pod</code>, or <code>kubectl_generic</code> regardless of the configured restriction mode. v3.6.0 added matching enforcement at the execution layer. CVE-2026-47250 (CVSS 3.1), published June 5, showed that <code>kubectl_generic</code> still passed user-supplied flags straight to <code>kubectl</code> with no allowlist. A prompt injection planted in pod logs could nudge the agent to call <code>kubectl_generic</code> with <code>--server=https://attacker.example/</code> and <code>--insecure-skip-tls-verify=true</code>, sending the operator&#39;s bearer token to the attacker. v3.7.0 added flag filtering. The researcher confirmed the full prompt-injection-to-token-exfiltration chain end to end against a live <code>kind</code> cluster with Claude Haiku as the agent.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Both bugs assume the same thing: the restrictions defined in config are the restrictions the code is checking. They aren&#39;t, and they weren&#39;t. Putting an AI agent between the operator and <code>kubectl</code> doesn&#39;t change the lesson, except that the agent will type <code>--insecure-skip-tls-verify=true</code> for you, on request, from a pod log.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Pre-v3.6.0 deployments let any reachable client invoke arbitrary `kubectl` tools regardless of the configured restriction policy. Pre-v3.7.0 deployments let prompt-injected pod logs harvest the operator&#39;s kubeconfig bearer token, which then replays directly against the real cluster&#39;s API server.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://advisories.gitlab.com/npm/mcp-server-kubernetes/CVE-2026-46519/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitLab Advisory: CVE-2026-46519, MCP Server Kubernetes Tool Access Control Bypass</a><a href="https://advisories.gitlab.com/npm/mcp-server-kubernetes/CVE-2026-47250/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitLab Advisory: CVE-2026-47250, MCP Server Kubernetes kubectl-generic Flag Injection</a><a href="https://neuraltrust.ai/blog/kubernetes-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NeuralTrust: Why Your Kubernetes MCP Server May Be Open to Attack (CVE-2026-46519)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-181" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="supply-chain github rce credential-theft advisory"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-47751</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic&#39;s GitHub Action Enabled Every MCP Server a Pull Request Shipped <a href="#incident-181" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">jason-anthropic published GHSA-8q5r-mmjf-575q on May 20, 2026 against <code>anthropics/claude-code-action</code> before 1.0.74, crediting reptou through HackerOne and carrying CVE-2026-47751. Three things combined. The action checked out the pull request head branch, which the author of that pull request controls. It read <code>.mcp.json</code> from the working directory through its default setting sources. And it enabled every project MCP server it found via <code>enableAllProjectMcpServers</code>. An attacker who opened a pull request carrying a malicious <code>.mcp.json</code> therefore ran arbitrary code on the GitHub Actions runner as soon as a privileged user or an automatic trigger invoked Claude on that pull request, with the workflow&#39;s API keys and tokens in reach. The CVE record states that 1.0.74 restores <code>.claude/</code> and <code>.mcp.json</code> from the pull request base branch before the CLI runs. The advisory itself says only to update to the latest version. Anthropic rates it Moderate at 5.3 under CVSS 4.0. NVD did not publish the record until July 16.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/laughing-leo.png" alt="leo raising his glass: 5.3 moderate, for RCE on the runner" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>leo raising his glass: 5.3 moderate, for RCE on the runner</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Thirteen days before this advisory, Adversa published TrustFall, which is <code>enableAllProjectMcpServers</code> reading a cloned repository on a developer&#39;s laptop. This is the same setting in CI, where the secrets are better and nobody is watching the run. Filed High against the vendor&#39;s Moderate, because code execution on a build runner plus workflow credentials is not a 5.3 in this site&#39;s terms.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Opening a pull request against any repository running the action, and getting Claude triggered on it, executes attacker code on the runner with every secret the workflow holds.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action/security/advisories/GHSA-8q5r-mmjf-575q" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-8q5r-mmjf-575q: Malicious MCP Server Configuration in PRs Enables Remote Code Execution and Secret Exfiltration</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-47751" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD record for CVE-2026-47751, which states what 1.0.74 changed</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-49" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="authentication credential-theft data-leak advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-48039</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Meta Ads MCP Server Skips the 401, Then Returns the Operator&#39;s Access Token in the Error Body <a href="#incident-49" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">pipeboard-co&#39;s meta-ads-mcp lets AI assistants run Meta Ads campaigns. Through 1.0.108, <code>AuthInjectionMiddleware.dispatch()</code> at <code>http_auth_integration.py:272</code> handles an unauthenticated Streamable HTTP request by noting that no authentication tokens were found in the headers, then calling <code>call_next(request)</code> anyway. No 401 is ever issued. Tool handlers that find no per-request credential fall back to the <code>META_ACCESS_TOKEN</code> environment variable, so they run as the operator. When the downstream Meta Graph API call fails, <code>api.py:263-269</code> serializes the raw httpx request URL into the JSON-RPC response body, and that URL carries the access token as a query parameter. Fixed in 1.0.109. CVSS 9.1.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/always-has-been.png" alt="wait, the auth middleware just logs the missing token and continues?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>wait, the auth middleware just logs the missing token and continues?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The middleware detected the missing credentials, wrote that down, and continued. Then the error handler, whose job is to describe what went wrong, described it using the token.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Any caller who can reach the server invokes Meta Ads tools with the operator&#39;s credentials. Provoke an API error and the response hands back the long-lived access token itself.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/pipeboard-co/meta-ads-mcp/security/advisories/GHSA-9gw6-46qc-99vr" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-9gw6-46qc-99vr: meta-ads-mcp unauthenticated tool invocation and token disclosure</a><a href="https://github.com/pipeboard-co/meta-ads-mcp/releases/tag/1.0.109" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">meta-ads-mcp 1.0.109 release</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-15" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="data-exfiltration sandbox-escape authentication"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Claude Code SOCKS5 Sandbox Bypass Exfiltrates Credentials and MCP Configs <a href="#incident-15" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Aonan Guan, who leads cloud and AI security at Wyze Labs, publicly disclosed his second Claude Code network sandbox bypass in five months. The latest issue is a SOCKS5 hostname null-byte injection. Claude Code&#39;s proxy enforces its egress allowlist by passing the raw DOMAINNAME bytes from a CONNECT request through a JavaScript <code>endsWith()</code> check against the user&#39;s wildcard policy. JavaScript treats <code>\x00</code> as an ordinary UTF-16 code unit, so a crafted host like <code>attacker-host.com\x00.google.com</code> matches an allowlist entry for <code>.google.com</code> and is approved. When libc later resolves the hostname via <code>getaddrinfo()</code>, the C runtime truncates at the null byte and dials <code>attacker-host.com</code> instead. Every release from v2.0.24 (sandbox GA on Oct 20, 2025) through v2.1.89 was vulnerable. Anthropic shipped a fix in v2.1.90 on April 1, 2026, with no security note in the changelog, no advisory on the Claude Code page, and no CVE assigned. Exfiltration paths reachable from inside the sandbox include MCP server configs, <code>~/.claude.json</code>, project source, and anything else the agent could read.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The sandbox failed open because <code>endsWith</code> and <code>getaddrinfo</code> disagree about whether <code>\x00</code> is a character. That isn&#39;t an exotic bug. The Apache HTTP server fixed the SSL-certificate-null-byte version of it in 2009. Shipping a network policy that&#39;s robust against motivated attackers takes engineering. Shipping one quietly takes considerably less.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Arbitrary data exfiltration past the network allowlist for roughly 5.5 months across about 130 published versions. Users who relied on a wildcard allowlist during that window received no advisory telling them to rotate credentials.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://oddguan.com/blog/second-time-same-sandbox-anthropic-claude-code-network-allowlist-bypass-data-exfiltration/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aonan Guan: Second Time, Same Sandbox, Another Anthropic Claude Code Network Sandbox Bypass Enables Data Exfiltration</a><a href="https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/05/20/even-claude-agrees-hole-in-its-sandbox-was-real-and-dangerous/5243662" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Register: Even Claude agrees: hole in its sandbox was real and dangerous</a><a href="https://www.securityweek.com/anthropic-silently-patches-claude-code-sandbox-bypass/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SecurityWeek: Anthropic Silently Patches Claude Code Sandbox Bypass</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-13" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design government-guidance advisory ai-supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> NSA Publishes MCP Security Design Considerations <a href="#incident-13" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The NSA&#39;s Artificial Intelligence Security Center released a Cybersecurity Information Sheet titled &quot;Model Context Protocol (MCP): Security Design Considerations for AI-Driven Automation.&quot; The document flags MCP&#39;s &quot;rapid proliferation [that] has outpaced the development of its security model.&quot; It calls out the protocol&#39;s inversion of the typical client-server pattern (the server can prompt the client to take actions) and enumerates systemic concerns: trust boundary ambiguity, unverified task propagation, session-replay risk, and serialization issues. It urges &quot;heightened scrutiny&quot; for production deployments, especially in national-security and high-assurance environments.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">When the <em>NSA</em> publishes a Cybersecurity Information Sheet saying your protocol &quot;outpaced the development of its security model,&quot; &quot;expected behavior&quot; is probably not the response the rest of the industry wants on file.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Formal government-level acknowledgment that MCP&#39;s security model is underdeveloped, naming concrete protocol-design gaps that operators are expected to compensate for.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/Press-Releases-Statements/Press-Release-View/Article/4496698/nsa-releases-security-design-considerations-for-ai-driven-automation-leveraging/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NSA: Security Design Considerations for AI-Driven Automation Leveraging MCP</a><a href="https://www.executivegov.com/articles/nsa-model-context-protocol-deployment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NSA Urges Stronger Security Measures for MCP Deployments (ExecutiveGov)</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="government-guidance" aria-pressed="false">government-guidance</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 19</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-14" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="supply-chain credential-theft exploited-in-the-wild ai-supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-45321</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Mini Shai-Hulud Worm Weaponizes Claude Code and MCP Configs for Persistence <a href="#incident-14" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">TeamPCP&#39;s Mini Shai-Hulud worm campaign ran through April and May 2026, hijacking npm maintainer accounts and publishing self-propagating malware across more than 600 packages on npm and PyPI. The May 19 wave compromised the <code>atool</code> and <code>prop</code> accounts and pushed 639 malicious versions across 323 packages in Alibaba&#39;s @antv data visualization ecosystem in a 22-minute automated burst. Earlier waves hit SAP CAP / <code>mbt</code> (April 29), TanStack (May 11), Mistral AI, Guardrails AI, UiPath, and OpenSearch. Each compromised release ships a preinstall hook that downloads the Bun JavaScript runtime as a living-off-the-land binary, then executes a credential harvester that sweeps cloud tokens, CI secrets, and password-manager vaults. The novel part: the payload reads <code>~/.claude.json</code> and the host&#39;s MCP server configurations, then appends <code>SessionStart</code> hooks to <code>.claude/settings.json</code> so the next time Claude Code opens any project on the machine, the malware re-executes with full agent privileges. Researchers at Akamai, Snyk, Wiz, StepSecurity, and Phoenix Security all confirmed the AI-coding-agent persistence behavior independently.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/isthatthejoke.jpg" alt="first supply chain worm to use SessionStart hooks for persistence" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>first supply chain worm to use SessionStart hooks for persistence</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The threat model that produced <code>.claude/settings.json</code> as a hook execution surface assumed nobody would write to it. The threat model that left MCP server config readable assumed nobody would read it. Both held up fine until somebody did both at once with a worm named after a Frank Herbert monster.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Credential theft at scale across GitHub, npm, AWS, GCP, Azure, Vault, 1Password, and Bitwarden, plus self-propagation via stolen npm tokens and live AI-agent re-execution on every Claude Code session. Over 1,197 confirmed compromised repositories within hours of the @antv wave.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/mini-shai-hulud-worm-compromises.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Mini Shai-Hulud Worm Compromises TanStack, Mistral AI, Guardrails AI &amp; More Packages</a><a href="https://snyk.io/blog/mini-shai-hulud-antv-npm-supply-chain-attack/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Snyk: Mini Shai-Hulud Hits AntV, 300+ Malicious npm Packages Published via Compromised Maintainer Account</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/mini-shai-hulud-pushes-malicious-antv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Mini Shai-Hulud Pushes Malicious AntV npm Packages via Compromised Maintainer Account</a><a href="https://www.tenable.com/blog/mini-shai-hulud-frequently-asked-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tenable: Mini Shai-Hulud Supply Chain Attack CVE-2026-45321 FAQ</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · may · 07</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-60" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="rce supply-chain authorization cursor"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> TrustFall Puts an MCP Server in a Cloned Repo, and Anthropic Says the Trust Dialog Covers It <a href="#incident-60" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Adversa AI disclosed TrustFall on May 7, 2026. A cloned repository ships two files, <code>.mcp.json</code> and <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. Opening the folder and accepting the generic trust dialog applies those project settings, including <code>enableAllProjectMcpServers</code> and <code>enabledMcpjsonServers</code>, which start an attacker-controlled MCP server as an unsandboxed Node.js process holding the developer&#39;s privileges. Alex Polyakov and Sergey Malenkovich reported the same pattern across Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor CLI, and Copilot CLI. Adversa counts it as the third flaw of this class in six months, following CVE-2025-59536.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/best-i-can-do.jpg" alt="you clicked trust, so that is the threat model" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>you clicked trust, so that is the threat model</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Anthropic&#39;s position is that consent was obtained, so this sits outside the threat model. The dialog asks whether you trust the files in the folder. It does not mention that accepting starts a program.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Cloning a repository and clicking the trust prompt runs attacker code with full user privileges. The MCP server is not sandboxed, not confined to the project directory, and not restricted on the network.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://adversa.ai/blog/trustfall-coding-agent-security-flaw-rce-claude-cursor-gemini-cli-copilot/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adversa AI: TrustFall, 1-click coding agent RCE in Claude, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Copilot</a><a href="https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/05/07/claude-code-trust-prompt-can-trigger-one-click-rce/5235319" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Register: Claude Code trust prompt can trigger one-click RCE</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cursor" aria-pressed="false">cursor</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · april · 25</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-12" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce authentication exploited-in-the-wild supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-33032</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> nginx-ui MCP Endpoint Unauthenticated RCE <a href="#incident-12" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Pluto Security disclosed a critical (CVSS 9.8) vulnerability in nginx-ui&#39;s Model Context Protocol implementation. The MCP integration split traffic across two HTTP endpoints. <code>/mcp</code> handles session establishment and was correctly gated by an IP whitelist and auth middleware. <code>/mcp_message</code> handles tool invocation, including configuration writes and server restart, and shipped with no authentication at all. The default IP whitelist is empty, so the unauthenticated endpoint accepted connections from any address. Shodan turned up over 2,600 publicly exposed nginx-ui instances on the default port 9000. Pluto disclosed in early March 2026, v2.3.4 fixed it, and Recorded Future later listed the CVE among 31 vulnerabilities actively exploited by threat actors in March 2026.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">One MCP endpoint had IP allow-listing and authentication middleware. The other was the one that actually mattered, and it shipped without either. Same project, same PR, same review. The mental model under which <code>/mcp_message</code> doesn&#39;t need auth because <code>/mcp</code> already had it is the same one that puts a screen lock on the front camera only.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Unauthenticated remote modification of NGINX configuration, server restart, traffic interception, and administrator credential harvesting. Confirmed exploitation in the wild.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.endorlabs.com/vulnerability/cve-2026-33032" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Endor Labs: CVE-2026-33032, nginx-ui Unauthenticated MCP Endpoint</a><a href="https://www.darkreading.com/application-security/critical-mcp-integration-flaw-nginx-risk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dark Reading: Critical MCP Integration Flaw Puts NGINX at Risk</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/04/critical-nginx-ui-vulnerability-cve.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Actively Exploited nginx-ui Flaw (CVE-2026-33032) Enables Full Nginx Server Takeover</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="exploited-in-the-wild" aria-pressed="false">exploited-in-the-wild</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · april · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-11" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection supply-chain protocol-design"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-30623</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic MCP SDK STDIO Command Injection (Declined to Patch) <a href="#incident-11" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">OX Security disclosed a systemic command-injection vulnerability in Anthropic&#39;s official MCP SDKs across Python, TypeScript, Java, and Rust. The STDIO transport invokes a configured command string through the OS shell unconditionally. If the intended MCP binary doesn&#39;t exist, the shell still executes whatever command was supplied. OX identified four distinct exploitation families all tracing back to the same root cause, affecting more than 7,000 publicly accessible servers and 150 million package downloads, with an estimated 200,000 vulnerable instances across the ecosystem. Anthropic acknowledged the behavior, declined to modify the protocol, and updated its security guidance to advise that STDIO adapters be &quot;used with caution.&quot; The company characterized the existing design as a <em>secure default</em> with sanitization being the developer&#39;s responsibility. Downstream CVEs already cluster around the same root cause: CVE-2026-22252 (LibreChat), CVE-2026-22688 (WeKnora), CVE-2025-54994 (@akoskm/create-mcp-server-stdio).</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/theyre-the-same-picture.png" alt="critical vulnerability disclosure | expected behavior, by design" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>critical vulnerability disclosure | expected behavior, by design</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">&quot;Sanitization is the developer&#39;s responsibility&quot; is a fine policy for <code>printf(&quot;%s&quot;)</code>. It is a less fine policy for a protocol whose entire pitch is that you can wire up a <code>command</code> string from a config file and have a language model decide when to invoke it. The number of those 200,000 deployers who have read the updated security policy is fewer than 200,000.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Arbitrary OS command execution on hosts running vulnerable MCP servers, with no protocol-level fix forthcoming. Every implementer is now responsible for sanitizing input that the SDK explicitly hands to a shell.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.ox.security/blog/mcp-supply-chain-advisory-rce-vulnerabilities-across-the-ai-ecosystem/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OX Security: MCP STDIO Command Injection, Full Vulnerability Advisory</a><a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/04/anthropic-mcp-design-vulnerability.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Anthropic MCP Design Vulnerability Enables RCE, Threatening AI Supply Chain</a><a href="https://www.theregister.com/2026/04/16/anthropic_mcp_design_flaw/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Register: MCP &#39;design flaw&#39; puts 200k servers at risk</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · april · 13</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-76" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="ssrf local-network advisory"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-34884</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Apache SkyWalking’s MCP Server Lets the Caller Set the Backend URL, and NVD Got There in August <a href="#incident-76" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Qiuxia Fan announced CVE-2026-34884 on the oss-security list on April 13, 2026, crediting Andrea Cosentino. Apache SkyWalking MCP 0.1.0 carries two issues: the <code>set_skywalking_url</code> tool accepts a caller-supplied URL and turns it into server-side requests, and the MCP server passes GraphQL expressions through without adequate sanitization. Users were told to upgrade to 0.2.0. NVD scored the pair 9.8 and published its record on August 18, four months and five days after Apache announced it.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/waiting-skeleton.jpg" alt="skeleton waiting on a bench: the nvd record for a 9.8, four months on" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>skeleton waiting on a bench: the nvd record for a 9.8, four months on</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">A 9.8 in an Apache top-level project sat on oss-security for four months before the CVE databases caught up. Anyone tracking MCP exposure by watching NVD had an April-shaped hole in their inventory until last week, which is a decent argument for reading mailing lists.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>An attacker who can reach the MCP server redirects its backend requests to arbitrary destinations and injects expressions into the GraphQL queries it issues.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://seclists.org/oss-sec/2026/q2/117" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">oss-security: CVE-2026-34884, Apache SkyWalking MCP SSRF and GraphQL Expression Injection</a><a href="https://lists.apache.org/thread/s447p6h5dfr02lx17v27phoksgb8mkkp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apache announcement thread</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="advisory" aria-pressed="false">advisory</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · march · 17</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-38" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="rce command-injection credential-theft authorization cursor"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Proofpoint&#39;s CursorJack: Cursor MCP Deeplinks Let Any Link Claim Any Vendor&#39;s Name <a href="#incident-38" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Proofpoint published CursorJack on March 17, 2026, a proof-of-concept from Rachel Rabin, Anna Akselevich, and Stanislav Silberberg showing that Cursor&#39;s <code>cursor://</code> MCP deeplinks work as a delivery mechanism. A deeplink carries a base64-encoded MCP server config; clicking it pops the install prompt, and accepting runs that config&#39;s command with the developer&#39;s privileges. Two paths work: the <code>command</code> parameter executes locally, and the <code>url</code> parameter points Cursor at an attacker-hosted remote MCP server. The deeplink can also claim any server name it likes, <code>Azure DevOps</code> for instance, and Cursor never verifies the link came from the vendor it names.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Cursor shipped <code>1.3</code> to fix CVE-2025-54133, where the install dialog didn&#39;t show command arguments at all. CursorJack landed in March arguing the flow itself was the problem, not how the dialog renders it. Four months later DeepJack hid the arguments again with whitespace padding, which suggests Proofpoint had a point.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One click plus one Approve runs an attacker&#39;s command with the developer&#39;s privileges. Reverse shells, credential harvesting, lateral movement. MCP-capable IDEs live on workstations holding SSH keys, API tokens, cloud credentials, source code, and production access, which is exactly why they are worth aiming at.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.proofpoint.com/us/blog/threat-insight/cursorjack-weaponizing-deeplinks-exploit-cursor-ide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Proofpoint: CursorJack, weaponizing Deeplinks to exploit Cursor IDE</a><a href="https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/cursor-jack-attack-path-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Infosecurity Magazine: CursorJack Attack Path Exposes Code Execution Risk in AI Development</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cursor" aria-pressed="false">cursor</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · march · 16</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-10" data-severity="informational" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="protocol-design ai-supply-chain vendor-abandonment"> <div class="sev-bar informational"> <span class="l">[informational]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Perplexity Ditches MCP <a href="#incident-10" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">At its core, the article argues that MCP is too token-hungry to be practical at production scale, with tool definitions consuming the majority of context before any user request is even processed. Several major companies are independently abandoning it in favor of lighter-weight alternatives like traditional APIs and CLIs.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/yousureaboutthat.png" alt="everyone who said MCP would be the universal protocol" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>everyone who said MCP would be the universal protocol</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">&quot;Universal AI protocol&quot; was always going to mean &quot;burn 72% of your context window on tool definitions you&#39;ll never use,&quot; but I appreciate that we collectively had to spend a year discovering it.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>MCP&#39;s &quot;universal AI protocol&quot; vision is effectively dead for production use cases, surviving only as a niche tool for desktop/IDE integrations.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://byteiota.com/perplexity-ditches-mcp-72-context-waste-kills-protocol/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Perplexity Ditches MCP: 72% Context Waste Kills Protocol</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ai-supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">ai-supply-chain</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="vendor-abandonment" aria-pressed="false">vendor-abandonment</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · march · 05</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-9" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="prompt-injection credential-theft supply-chain"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-75130</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> ContextCrush Flaw in Context7 MCP Server <a href="#incident-9" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Noma Labs discovered the ContextCrush vulnerability in Context7, a registry that delivers coding documentation to AI assistants via an MCP server. Attackers manipulated the platform&#39;s Custom Rules feature to plant malicious instructions. When an AI coding assistant (like Cursor or Windsurf) queried the documentation, it ingested the poisoned rules via the trusted MCP channel and autonomously executed harmful actions, such as stealing .env files.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/werealltryingtofindtheguywhodidthis.jpg" alt="the documentation registry's threat model" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>the documentation registry&#39;s threat model</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Imagine trusting an unauthenticated third-party documentation registry to autonomously execute commands in your dev environment. Couldn&#39;t be me. Was probably you.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Widespread credential theft and data exfiltration via third-party documentation poisoning.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://noma.security/blog/contextcrush-context7-the-mcp-server-vulnerability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Noma Security Blog: ContextCrush Vulnerability</a><a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-75130" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVD: CVE-2026-75130</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="supply-chain" aria-pressed="false">supply-chain</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · february · 04</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-39" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="data-leak cross-tenant protocol-design authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2026-25536</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> MCP TypeScript SDK Routes One Client&#39;s Tool Output to a Different Client <a href="#incident-39" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Advisory GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7 landed on February 4, 2026 against <code>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> 1.10.0 through 1.25.3, and it is two bugs wearing one CVE. Sharing a single <code>StreamableHTTPServerTransport</code> across concurrent clients lets JSON-RPC message IDs collide, because every MCP client numbers its requests from zero and counts up, so the second client&#39;s request overwrites the first client&#39;s response mapping and the reply goes down the wrong HTTP connection. Sharing a single <code>McpServer</code> across multiple transports silently overwrites the Protocol object&#39;s internal <code>this._transport</code>, which misroutes server-to-client traffic including progress notifications and sampling requests. Both land hardest on stateless deployments that reuse instances across requests. Fixed in 1.26.0. CVSS 7.1.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/clippy.PNG" alt="it looks like you're trying to read another client's tool output. need help with that?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>it looks like you&#39;re trying to read another client&#39;s tool output. need help with that?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The isolation boundary between two tenants was a counter that both of them start at zero. Sixteen minor releases shipped before anyone noticed.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>One user&#39;s tool results, progress notifications, and sampling requests arrive on a different user&#39;s connection. No attacker positioning required; ordinary concurrent traffic is enough to leak.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GHSA-345p-7cg4-v4c7: @modelcontextprotocol/sdk cross-client data leak via shared server/transport instance reuse</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="protocol-design" aria-pressed="false">protocol-design</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · january · 21</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-8" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="ssrf cloud-metadata aws"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Microsoft MarkItDown MCP Server SSRF <a href="#incident-8" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">BlueRock researchers discovered a severe Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) flaw in the MCP server built for Microsoft&#39;s MarkItDown file converter. The server failed to validate URIs, allowing attackers to force the AI agent to query local cloud metadata endpoints (e.g., AWS 169.254.169.254). Subsequent scans revealed over 36% of public MCP servers contained similar SSRF vulnerabilities.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/clippy.PNG" alt="it looks like you're trying to leak AWS metadata. need help with that?" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>it looks like you&#39;re trying to leak AWS metadata. need help with that?</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">36% of public MCP servers shipped with the same vulnerability class. The author of that statistic is being polite. The actionable number is: don&#39;t run anything you didn&#39;t read yourself.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Exposure of AWS instance metadata, leading to the extraction of access keys, secret keys, and session tokens.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.bluerock.io/post/mcp-furi-microsoft-markitdown-vulnerabilities" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BlueRock: MCP FURI - Microsoft MarkItDown Vulnerabilities</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="ssrf" aria-pressed="false">ssrf</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cloud-metadata" aria-pressed="false">cloud-metadata</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="aws" aria-pressed="false">aws</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2026 · january · 20</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-7" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce file-overwrite git"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2025-68143, CVE-2025-68144, CVE-2025-68145</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic Git MCP Server RCE <a href="#incident-7" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Cyata researchers disclosed a chain of critical vulnerabilities in Anthropic&#39;s official Git MCP server. The flaws included an unrestricted git_init function, a path-validation bypass, and an argument-injection vulnerability. Attackers could chain these to turn arbitrary directories into Git repositories, overwrite system files, and achieve RCE via malicious .git/config manipulation.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/spiderman point.png" alt="git_init · path validator · arg parser, on disclosure day" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>git_init · path validator · arg parser, on disclosure day</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">CVSS 8.1 in the <em>official</em> server. Not a dodgy third-party one. The shipped-by-the-company-named-after-the-protocol one.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>High-severity (CVSS 8.1) arbitrary file deletion, file overwriting, and RCE.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://thehackernews.com/2026/01/three-flaws-in-anthropic-mcp-git-server.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Hacker News: Three Flaws in Anthropic MCP Git Server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="file-overwrite" aria-pressed="false">file-overwrite</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="git" aria-pressed="false">git</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · july · 15</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-6" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="sandbox-escape privilege-escalation filesystem"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2025-53109, CVE-2025-53110</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic Filesystem MCP Sandbox Escape <a href="#incident-6" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Cymulate disclosed two high-severity defects in Anthropic&#39;s official Filesystem MCP Server. Attackers exploiting these flaws could list, read, or write to directories outside the allowed scope. If the server was run as a privileged user, this could lead to full sandbox escape, manipulation of critical system files, and privilege escalation.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">&quot;Allowed scope&quot; was never going to survive contact with a model that&#39;s also been instructed to be helpful, accommodating, and never refuse a tool call.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Unauthorized host filesystem manipulation and sandbox escape.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://cymulate.com/blog/cve-2025-53109-53110-escaperoute-anthropic/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cymulate Blog: EscapeRoute - Anthropic MCP Vulnerabilities</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="sandbox-escape" aria-pressed="false">sandbox-escape</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="privilege-escalation" aria-pressed="false">privilege-escalation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="filesystem" aria-pressed="false">filesystem</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · july · 10</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-5" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce local-network authentication"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2025-49596</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Anthropic MCP Inspector Local Network RCE <a href="#incident-5" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Oligo Security and Tenable discovered a critical flaw (CVSS 9.4) in the Anthropic MCP Inspector tool. Because the interactive web UI launched via localhost lacked out-of-the-box authentication, an attacker on the same local network could inject malicious commands (NeighborJacking) or use cross-site attacks to achieve RCE.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/imtryingtounderstand.webp" alt="me reading &#34;localhost doesn't need auth&#34; in 2025" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>me reading &quot;localhost doesn&#39;t need auth&quot; in 2025</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The default debugging tool from the maintainers of the protocol shipped without authentication. The threat model, quoted: &quot;it&#39;s localhost.&quot; Localhost has been a hostile network since the invention of coffee shop Wi-Fi.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Arbitrary code execution via local network hijacking.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.oligo.security/blog/critical-rce-vulnerability-in-anthropic-mcp-inspector-cve-2025-49596" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Oligo Security: Critical RCE Vulnerability in Anthropic MCP Inspector</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="local-network" aria-pressed="false">local-network</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authentication" aria-pressed="false">authentication</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · july · 09</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-4" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="true" data-tags="rce command-injection mcp-remote"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">CVE-2025-6514</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> mcp-remote OS Command Injection <a href="#incident-4" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">The JFrog Security Research team discovered a critical vulnerability (CVSS 9.6) in mcp-remote, a popular proxy tool (over 437,000 downloads) used to connect local LLM hosts to remote MCP servers. If a user connected to a malicious remote MCP server, the server could send a booby-trapped authorization_endpoint URL that achieved full arbitrary OS command execution on the user&#39;s local machine.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">437,000 downloads. A booby-trapped <code>authorization_endpoint</code> URL. Full RCE on the client. The MCP supply chain isn&#39;t a chain so much as a single rusted carabiner.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Full system compromise and RCE on the client OS.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://jfrog.com/blog/2025-6514-critical-mcp-remote-rce-vulnerability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JFrog: CVE-2025-6514 - Critical mcp-remote RCE Vulnerability</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="rce" aria-pressed="false">rce</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="command-injection" aria-pressed="false">command-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="mcp-remote" aria-pressed="false">mcp-remote</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · june · 18</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-3" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="data-leak cross-tenant authorization"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> Asana MCP Server Cross-Tenant Data Leak <a href="#incident-3" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Work management platform Asana had to temporarily disable its experimental MCP feature after discovering a logic flaw in its implementation. The misconfiguration failed to isolate cross-tenant data, meaning AI agents could potentially access customer data, projects, and tasks belonging to entirely different organizations.</p>   <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Unauthorized exposure of customer data to other organizations.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://www.upguard.com/blog/asana-discloses-data-exposure-bug-in-mcp-server" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UpGuard: Asana Discloses Data Exposure Bug in MCP Server</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-leak" aria-pressed="false">data-leak</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="cross-tenant" aria-pressed="false">cross-tenant</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="authorization" aria-pressed="false">authorization</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · june · 12</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-2" data-severity="high" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="credential-theft llm-manipulation prompt-hub"> <div class="sev-bar high"> <span class="l">[high]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> LangSmith AgentSmith Prompt Hub Flaw <a href="#incident-2" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">A severe vulnerability (CVSS 8.8) dubbed AgentSmith was disclosed in LangSmith&#39;s Prompt Hub. The flaw exposed AI agents using MCP to data theft and manipulation, allowing malicious agents to hijack LLM responses and steal user API keys.</p>  <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">Naming your vulnerability after the bad guy from The Matrix doesn&#39;t make it cooler than &quot;forgot to scope an API key.&quot; But points for effort.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Credential theft and LLM manipulation.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://hackread.com/agentsmith-flaw-langsmith-prompt-hub/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hackread: AgentSmith Flaw in LangSmith&#39;s Prompt Hub</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="credential-theft" aria-pressed="false">credential-theft</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="llm-manipulation" aria-pressed="false">llm-manipulation</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-hub" aria-pressed="false">prompt-hub</button> </div> </div> </article>  <p class="date-stamp">2025 · may · 26</p> <article class="incident" id="incident-1" data-severity="critical" data-has-cve="false" data-tags="prompt-injection data-exfiltration github"> <div class="sev-bar critical"> <span class="l">[critical]</span> <span class="cve">no CVE assigned</span> </div> <div class="incident-body"> <h2> GitHub MCP Prompt Injection Data Heist <a href="#incident-1" class="permalink" aria-label="permalink">#</a> </h2> <p class="desc">Security researchers at Invariant Labs discovered a critical vulnerability affecting the official GitHub MCP integration. Attackers could create maliciously crafted issues in public repositories. When a developer asked their AI assistant to check open issues, the AI would read the malicious payload, get prompt-injected, and autonomously use the developer&#39;s credentials to exfiltrate private repository data (such as source code and salary information) into public pull requests.</p> <figure class="meme"> <img src="/memes/idontwanttobearound.png" alt="every dev reading this and quietly revoking their AI assistant's repo scope" loading="lazy"> <figcaption><span class="ex">EX.A</span>every dev reading this and quietly revoking their AI assistant&#39;s repo scope</figcaption> </figure> <div class="commentary"> <div class="label">commentary</div> <div class="body">The AI was <em>helpfully</em> reading the issue. The issue was <em>helpfully</em> telling it to leak code. There&#39;s no patch for &quot;documentation can be lies.&quot; That&#39;s the entire reading-the-internet problem condensed into one CVE.</div> </div> <div class="impact"> <div class="l">impact</div> <p>Exfiltration of private repository data including source code and sensitive information.</p> </div> <div class="sources"> <div class="l">sources</div> <a href="https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-github-vulnerability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Invariant Labs: MCP GitHub Vulnerability</a> </div> <div class="tags"> <span class="l">tags</span> <button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="prompt-injection" aria-pressed="false">prompt-injection</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="data-exfiltration" aria-pressed="false">data-exfiltration</button><button type="button" class="tag" data-filter-type="tag" data-filter-value="github" aria-pressed="false">github</button> </div> </div> </article>  <div class="no-matches">no incidents match this filter. lucky you.</div> <footer class="site-footer"> <div class="left">
model context problems &middot; an ongoing chronicle of MCP security incidents. data compiled from public disclosures.
</div> <div class="right"> <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#6a0c0f0f0e080b09012a07050e0f060905041e0f121e1a180508060f0744090507">contact</a> <a href="/rss.xml">rss</a> <span>&copy; 2026</span> </div> </footer> </div>  <script data-cfasync="false" src="/cdn-cgi/scripts/5c5dd728/cloudflare-static/email-decode.min.js"></script><script>(function(){function c(){var b=a.contentDocument||(a.contentWindow&&a.contentWindow.document);if(b){var d=b.createElement('script');d.innerHTML="window.__CF$cv$params={r:'a58214ee0ee919b6',t:'MTc5NDI4MjUwOA=='};var l=window.parent.location,o;try{o=new URL(l.origin).origin}catch(e){try{o=new URL(new URL(l.href).origin).origin}catch(e){o=''}}var u='/cdn-cgi/challenge-platform/scripts/jsd/main.js?ray=a58214ee0ee919b6',a=document.createElement('script');a.src=o?new URL(u,o).href:u;document.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(a);";b.getElementsByTagName('head')[0].appendChild(d)}}if(document.body){var a=document.createElement('iframe');a.height=1;a.width=1;a.style.position='absolute';a.style.top=0;a.style.left=0;a.style.border='none';a.style.visibility='hidden';document.body.appendChild(a);if('loading'!==document.readyState)c();else if(window.addEventListener)document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded',c);else{var e=document.onreadystatechange||function(){};document.onreadystatechange=function(b){e(b);'loading'!==document.readyState&&(document.onreadystatechange=e,c())}}}})();</script></body></html>